window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["4runner"]=[{"id":"4runner-front-bumper","title":"Steel Front Bumper for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":849,"high":1799},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","drill and bits","wire harness tools"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","light electrical for sensor and fog light reinstall"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor_bumper_front","armor","front-bumper","steel","winch-mount","approach-angle","parking-sensor"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Summit Bar Front Bumper (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":1699},{"name":"CBI Offroad Front Bumper (5th Gen 4Runner 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Expedition One 4Runner Front Bumper (5th Gen)","vendor":"Expedition One","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Fab Four 4Runner Winch Bumper (5th Gen)","vendor":"Fab Four","price_estimate_usd":1449}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Summit Bar 4Runner Product Page","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-winch","4runner-sliders","4runner-skid-plates"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner's factory bumper sits low with a full-width plastic chin that reduces approach angle \u2014 a steel replacement improves the geometry, mounts a winch properly, and adds the recovery infrastructure the factory design lacks.","description":"The ARB Summit Bar is the premium choice for the 4Runner and the most commonly specified bumper on serious build threads. It's full-width, designed to accept a Warn or Mile Marker winch in the integrated plate, and includes mounting provisions for OEM fog lights and parking sensors. The weld and finish quality is class-leading at this price point. It is not a light bumper \u2014 the Summit Bar adds approximately 80\u2013100 lbs \u2014 but on a 4Runner that is carrying expedition gear, the additional mass is already in the budget.\n\nParking sensors are present on most 5th gen 4Runner trims from 2010 onward and the position varies by trim year. Most quality bumpers include sensor relocation brackets and drilling templates. Confirm your specific trim's sensor configuration (number of sensors and position) against the bumper manufacturer's compatibility chart before ordering. CBI, ARB, Expedition One, and Fab Four all offer sensor-compatible designs \u2014 some require minor drilling to accept the OEM sensor housing, which takes 20 minutes and is not an obstacle.\n\nFull-width vs stubby is an approach-angle trade-off. Full-width bumpers protect the outer fender edges on brush and tight trail situations. Stubby bumpers (used by some Fab Four designs) improve the approach angle by removing the outer wing sections \u2014 the result is 3\u00b0\u20135\u00b0 more approach clearance at the cost of the outer fender protection. For rock crawling, stubby is often preferred. For overland and mixed use, full-width is more practical.\n\nThe improvement in approach angle from any of these bumpers over the OEM unit is roughly 5\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 on the 4Runner. The OEM bumper's low chin catches on exit lips and ledge faces that an aftermarket bumper clears. That's a tangible difference on moderate rocky terrain.","steps":["Confirm trim and parking sensor configuration \u2014 check driver's manual or count OEM sensors in factory bumper","Order bumper with appropriate sensor kit for your trim","Disconnect battery negative terminal","Remove OEM bumper: typically 6\u20138 bolts along top edge plus lower support brackets","Remove fog lights and wiring harness from OEM bumper; set aside","Remove parking sensors from OEM bumper; transfer to new bumper using provided drilling template","Transfer fog lights into new bumper housing","Test-fit new bumper with all wiring in place before torquing any hardware","Bolt bumper to frame using supplied hardware; torque to spec (typically 70\u201390 ft-lb)","Reconnect all lighting and sensor wiring; reconnect battery","Test all lighting, sensors, and camera function before closing up","If installing winch: complete winch installation on bench before bumper install \u2014 more manageable to torque winch mounting bolts before bumper is on the truck"],"pros":["ARB Summit Bar is the benchmark 4Runner bumper \u2014 excellent quality, proven fit, wide accessory ecosystem","Integrated winch plate provides the proper load path for 10,000+ lb winch pull","Approach angle improvement of 5\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 is meaningful on technical rocky terrain","D-ring recovery tabs rated to 4.75 tons on ARB designs \u2014 correct for trail recovery use","Parking sensor compatibility built into most quality designs \u2014 no need for DIY bracket fabrication"],"cons":["ARB Summit Bar is the most expensive option at $1,699 \u2014 verify the build justifies the premium vs CBI at $1,299","Full-width designs add 80\u2013100 lbs to the front of the truck","Solo install is manageable but a second person makes alignment and bolt starting much more manageable","Parking sensor relocation may require software recalibration on some 4Runner trims \u2014 check with dealer","Stubby bumper designs sacrifice outer fender protection that matters on brushy trails"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-bumper","title":"Rear Bumper and Swing-Out Tire Carrier for the 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":899,"high":1799},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","drill and bits","wire harness tools"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","light electrical for backup camera and lights"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor_bumper_rear","armor","rear-bumper","swing-out","tire-carrier","departure-angle","backup-camera"],"parts":[{"name":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Rear Bumper (5th Gen 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Relentless Fabrication 4Runner Rear Bumper with Swing-Out","vendor":"Relentless Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"Addictive Desert Designs 4Runner Rear Bumper","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":1199},{"name":"Ranch Hand Legend 4Runner Rear Bumper","vendor":"Ranch Hand","price_estimate_usd":849}],"citations":[{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Rear Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"},{"title":"Relentless Fabrication 4Runner Products","url":"https://www.relentlessfabrication.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-recovery-gear-kit","4runner-sliders"],"summary":"A steel rear bumper on the 4Runner improves departure angle, adds proper recovery points, and \u2014 when combined with a swing-out carrier \u2014 moves the spare tire off the interior floor and frees the cargo area entirely.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner carries its spare tire under the rear cargo floor, consuming significant interior space and adding weight above the cargo line. An aftermarket rear bumper with an integrated swing-out tire carrier moves the spare outside the vehicle, mounts it on a hinged arm that swings clear of the rear gate, and reclaims that floor space for gear. For overland rigs where the back of the 4Runner is a living space or gear storage system, this is a meaningful quality-of-life upgrade alongside the trail protection benefits.\n\nDeparture angle improvement is 3\u00b0\u20135\u00b0 over the OEM bumper. The factory rear bumper hangs lower than a steel replacement and has rounded corners that snag on exit obstacles. A straight-cut or notched steel bumper rides higher on the frame and presents a cleaner exit profile. The 4Runner's IFS-front and live-axle-rear combination makes departure angle more of a real-world constraint than approach angle on most trail scenarios \u2014 a high departure angle protects the bumper and rear skid from rock strikes as the truck exits ledges and steep transitions.\n\nSwing-out tire carriers for 33\" tires are bolt-on on most designs. Carriers for 35\" tires require more robust pivot hardware and often a reinforced bumper base \u2014 verify the carrier's rated tire diameter before ordering. Backup camera retention varies by bumper design; most quality bumpers include a camera relocation mount. Confirm camera mount compatibility for your 4Runner's specific camera type (OEM housing shape varies by year).\n\nRanch Hand is a budget-tier steel bumper option \u2014 the quality is adequate for overland use, but the design is less refined and departure angle improvement is more modest than CBI or Relentless designs. It's a usable option for builds where budget is the primary constraint.","steps":["Measure swing-out arc clearance if planning a tire carrier \u2014 verify the carrier clears the body when fully opened","Disconnect battery negative terminal","Remove OEM rear bumper: typically 4\u20136 bolts plus lower support brackets and wiring","Transfer trailer wiring connector from OEM to new bumper","Transfer backup camera from OEM bumper housing to new bumper camera mount","Test-fit new bumper before attaching wiring","Bolt bumper to frame; torque to spec","Reconnect trailer wiring and backup camera; test all functions","Install swing-out carrier hardware if applicable; mount spare tire and verify swing clearance at full open","Reconnect battery; verify all rear lights and camera"],"pros":["Swing-out carrier moves spare tire out of the cargo area \u2014 reclaims significant interior space on the 4Runner","3\u00b0\u20135\u00b0 departure angle improvement over OEM","Rated D-ring recovery tabs for rear vehicle recovery","Backup camera provisions on most quality designs","Relentless Fabrication swing-out designs are well-regarded for pivot quality and long-term use with a loaded carrier"],"cons":["Swing-out carriers carrying a 33\"+ spare add substantial cantilevered weight \u2014 torque pivot bolts to spec and recheck annually","Backup camera relocation may change viewing angle slightly; may need adjustment in camera settings","Ranch Hand is the budget option \u2014 adequate but less departure angle improvement than premium designs","Solo install is manageable; having a second person to hold the bumper while starting frame bolts saves significant frustration","Some swing-out designs add 80\u2013120 lbs total with the spare \u2014 rear suspension may need to account for this added weight"]},{"id":"4runner-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill and bits"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","underbody work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor_skid","armor","skid-plate","underbody-protection","engine-skid","transfer-case","4runner"],"parts":[{"name":"ASFIR 4Runner Full Underbody Skid Kit (5th Gen)","vendor":"ASFIR","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Skid Plate Kit (5th Gen 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Slick Rock Fabrications 4Runner Skid Plate System (5th Gen)","vendor":"Slick Rock Fabrications","price_estimate_usd":599},{"name":"Rock Slide Engineering 4Runner Skid Plates (5th Gen)","vendor":"Rock Slide Engineering","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"ASFIR 4Runner Products","url":"https://www.asfir4x4.com"},{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Skid Plate Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-bumper","4runner-sliders"],"summary":"Trail Edition and TRD Pro 4Runners come with factory skid plates \u2014 but they're thin stamped steel that covers the engine only. Aftermarket full skid systems extend that protection to the transfer case, fuel tank, and rear differential with heavier material that can take a real hit.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner Trail Edition and TRD Pro trims include factory aluminum skid plates on the engine and front underbody. SR5 and Limited trims have no factory skids at all. In either case, aftermarket full skid kits improve coverage and material thickness. The factory plates are 3mm aluminum and sized only to protect the oil pan and front crossmember \u2014 they won't handle a direct rock hit on the transfer case or a high-centering event on the fuel tank.\n\nASFIR's full kit is the most comprehensive option \u2014 it covers the front crossmember, oil pan, transmission, transfer case, fuel tank, and rear differential in 3/16\" steel. That's the deepest coverage available for the 5th gen. CBI Offroad's kit uses similar coverage with slightly lighter materials and a lower price point. Slick Rock Fabrications offers a steel kit at the most accessible price but with somewhat lighter gauge material \u2014 suitable for mild to moderate trail use.\n\nThe 4Runner's independent front suspension means the front skid attaches to the frame and subframe, not the lower control arms as on solid-axle vehicles. This matters because the skid must flex slightly relative to the suspension travel on IFS designs \u2014 most aftermarket kits account for this with slotted mounting holes or flexible bracket designs. Verify that the skid you're ordering is designed specifically for the 5th gen IFS architecture.\n\nThe fuel tank skid is particularly important on the 4Runner. The factory fuel tank on the 5th gen hangs relatively low and is exposed behind the rear axle. High-centering on a rock shelf can contact the tank directly \u2014 a 3/16\" steel skid prevents that from being a trip-ending event.","steps":["Lift and support vehicle on jack stands; provide full underbody access","Remove OEM factory skid plates on Trail/TRD Pro trims \u2014 typically 4\u20136 bolts per piece","Clean mounting surfaces; inspect for corrosion at frame mounting points","Test-fit each skid section dry to confirm fitment before installing hardware","Install front crossmember/oil pan skid first; use existing OEM holes where possible","Install transmission and transfer case skid \u2014 use a floor jack as an assist for the heavy center piece","Install fuel tank skid, ensuring clearance around fuel fill neck and tank straps","Apply anti-seize to all hardware","Torque all fasteners to spec; verify clearance between skids and driveshafts by rotating shafts by hand","Lower vehicle and roll forward slowly to confirm no binding or noise from skid contact"],"pros":["ASFIR full kit covers every major underbody component in one purchase","3/16\" steel handles direct rock impacts that destroy the factory aluminum plates","Slick surfaces on steel skids allow the truck to slide over rocks rather than snag","CBI kit includes additional frame reinforcement brackets not found in factory skid mounting","Trail and TRD Pro factory plate removal is clean \u2014 aftermarket units use the same bolt holes on most designs"],"cons":["Full steel kit adds 70\u2013110 lbs of underbody weight","5th gen IFS architecture requires IFS-specific mounting design \u2014 do not use a generic skid or one not spec'd for the 5th gen","Slick Rock Fabrications kit uses lighter gauge material \u2014 adequate for mild use, may deform on a direct rock hit at speed","Some kits reduce ground clearance by 1/4\"\u20133/8\" depending on design \u2014 measure before ordering for rock-crawling use","Fuel tank skid must clear tank straps and fill neck \u2014 installation requires care to avoid pinching or stressing the tank mounting hardware"]},{"id":"4runner-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders and Frame Rails for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":899},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill and bits","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","underbody work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["armor_sliders","armor","sliders","frame-rails","rocker-protection","rock-crawling"],"parts":[{"name":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Rock Sliders (5th Gen 2010-2024)","vendor":"CBI Offroad","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Shrockworks 4Runner Rock Sliders (5th Gen)","vendor":"Shrockworks","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Metal Tech 4x4 4Runner Frame Sliders (5th Gen)","vendor":"Metal Tech 4x4","price_estimate_usd":649}],"citations":[{"title":"CBI Offroad 4Runner Slider Product Page","url":"https://www.cbioffroad.com"},{"title":"Shrockworks 4Runner Products","url":"https://www.shrockworks.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-skid-plates","4runner-front-bumper"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner has a pronounced lower body step and exposed pinch welds below the door sills \u2014 frame-mounted sliders protect both and give you a reliable Hi-Lift jack point that the factory body cannot provide.","description":"The 4Runner's lower body structure is more exposed than the Tacoma's. The factory step molding below the doors is plastic-capped stamped steel, and the pinch weld that runs along the rocker panel is an accessible contact point on any technical trail. Rock sliders mount to the frame and extend beyond the rocker panel, intercepting rock hits before they reach the body. On the 5th gen, most slider designs also cover the forward section of the lower rocker, which is a common first-contact point when the truck pivots over a diagonal obstacle.\n\nN-Fab nerf bars are sold in Toyota fitments and are a popular accessory for the 4Runner \u2014 but they are body-mounted and designed as cab entry steps, not trail protection. They will bend or deform under the lateral load of a rock hit or hi-lift use. If you are buying sliders for off-road protection, ensure the product specifies frame mounting with steel gusset plates at the attachment points. CBI, Shrockworks, and Metal Tech all use this construction. Shrockworks in particular has a strong reputation in the 4Runner community for weld quality and fitment precision.\n\nThe 4Runner's wider body and higher center of gravity mean side-hill situations happen on moderate trails, not aggressive rock crawling. Sliders pay off earlier in the 4Runner's use profile than on a Tacoma. For overland builds that regularly traverse off-camber terrain or narrow shelf roads, sliders are baseline protection alongside skid plates.","steps":["Lift and support vehicle on jack stands; access the underside","Inspect frame rails for rust or damage at the planned mounting points","Test-fit slider assembly dry; confirm clearance with tires at full lock","Mark and center-punch drill locations per manufacturer template (most designs use existing body mount bolt locations plus new holes)","Drill any required new frame holes with appropriate bit; deburr edges","Apply anti-seize to all hardware","Install slider with all hardware finger-tight; align level and flush with outer rocker panel edge","Torque all fasteners to spec \u2014 typically 50\u201375 ft-lb for 1/2\" grade 8 hardware","Verify slider does not contact exhaust heat shields or brake lines at full suspension droop","Touch up raw steel edges with primer or satin black rattle can"],"pros":["Frame-mounted sliders protect pinch welds and rocker panels from the lateral rock hits that dent or crease 4Runner body panels","Hi-Lift jack points \u2014 the slider tube is the correct location to jack the 4Runner in the field","Shrockworks sliders are particularly well-regarded for weld quality and precise 4Runner fitment","CBI sliders include an integrated step tube \u2014 makes cab entry more manageable on a lifted 4Runner","Bolt-on install with no welding required"],"cons":["N-Fab nerf bars are not sliders \u2014 body-mounted steps cannot take a lateral load and should not be used as jack points","Adds 60\u201390 lbs to the build \u2014 consider combined weight with bumpers and skid plates","Some slider designs reduce ground clearance slightly below the rocker \u2014 verify before ordering for rock-crawling use","4Runner lower body steps (factory plastic) will require removal on some slider designs","Full-length slider options are limited for the 4Runner compared to the Tacoma market"]},{"id":"4runner-sliders-weld-on","title":"Weld-On Rock Sliders \u2014 The Heavy-Duty Option","vehicle":"4runner","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":9},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":1600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","MIG welder","angle grinder","drill","torque wrench","tape measure","magnetic level"],"skills_required":["welding","wrenching"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["armor","sliders","rocker-armor"],"parts":[{"name":"Weld-on / hybrid rock sliders (DOM tube)","vendor":"RCI Off Road / CBI Offroad Fab / Victory 4x4","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Weld consumables, weld-through primer, cavity wax","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Shop welding session if you do not weld","vendor":"local fab shop","price_estimate_usd":400}],"citations":[{"title":"CBI Offroad Fab \u2014 5th gen 4Runner bolt-on rock sliders","url":"https://cbioffroadfab.com/product/5th-gen-toyota-4runner-overland-series-bolt-on-rock-sliders/"},{"title":"RCI Off Road \u2014 4Runner sliders and armor","url":"https://rcimetalworks.com/product-category/toyota/4runner/"},{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 5th gen TRD Off-Road build references","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2022/07/14/5th-gen-4runner-trd-off-road-builds/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-sliders","4runner-skid-plates"],"summary":"The bolt-on sliders in the companion guide are right for the majority of 4Runner builds. This card is for the owner who genuinely puts the body on rock \u2014 where a weld-on or weld-reinforced hybrid carries far more load without distorting the pinch seam. It is more capable and considerably more committing to install.","description":"A bolt-on slider clamps and through-bolts to the pinch seam and handles steps, curbs, and the occasional rub. It is enough for most trucks and it is reversible.\n\nA weld-on ties the slider directly into the frame rail and rocker structure, so it shrugs off being loaded with the truck's full weight on a rock, being used as a high-lift point, or taking a hard drop onto a ledge. On a 4Runner that uses the slider as armor rather than as a step, the welded joint is what keeps repeated loading from eventually opening the pinch seam.\n\nThe honest test: if you have ever set the truck down on a slider and heard the body creak, or if you plan trails where the rocker will contact rock rather than clear it, the weld-on is the right answer. If you mostly want a step and protection from parking-lot and forest-road hazards, [[4runner-sliders|the bolt-on version]] is the better spend.\n\nTwo spec lines separate a good slider from a decorative one:\n\n- Tubing. Look for DOM (drawn over mandrel) tube, typically 1.75 in x 0.120-0.250 in wall, rather than welded HREW. DOM has no seam to concentrate stress and tolerates impact without cracking at the weld. A vendor that does not state the tube type on the product page is telling you something.\n- Plate and gusseting. The frame-side mounting plate should be at least 3/16 in and should spread load across two or more frame crossmember areas, not a single point. Count the frame attachment points: three per side is the minimum worth buying, four is better.\n\nGeometry is the other half. A kick-out design adds a usable step and a wider footprint for stepping into a lifted truck, but it catches on tight rock and it is the first thing to bend on a narrow trail. A flush, tucked design protects better on a true crawler and keeps the truck narrow. Pick around how you wheel, not how it looks parked.\n\nPlan a full day. This is fabrication, not bolt-on.\n\n1. Park level, chock the wheels, and set the truck on jack stands under the frame so the body sits at its natural ride height. Welding a slider with the body loaded differently than it sits is how door gaps end up wrong.\n2. Remove the plastic rocker cladding and any mud flaps. Pull back the fender liners at both ends of the rocker so you can see the pinch seam and the frame rails.\n3. Strip paint, seam sealer, and undercoating from every weld area down to bright metal with a flap disc. Weld-through primer goes on immediately after \u2014 bare steel starts flash rusting within the hour.\n4. Dry-fit and support the slider on a floor jack. Set the fore-aft position off the door openings, not off the wheel arches. Check tire clearance at full lock and at full compression, and confirm the slider is level to the rocker with a magnetic level.\n5. Tack at four points per side, then step back and re-measure. Compare left to right, check door gaps, and open and close both doors. Tacks are cheap to grind off; a full weld is not.\n6. Weld out in short stitches, alternating ends, letting the panel cool between passes. Continuous heat in one area warps the rocker and telegraphs through the paint.\n7. Dress the welds, prime, paint, and seam-seal every cut edge.\n8. Spray cavity wax or an internal frame coating into the rocker and frame cavity through the factory drain holes. This is the step most installs skip and the reason welded sliders rust from the inside on salt-belt trucks.\n\n- Airbag sensors. 5th gen 4Runners carry side-impact sensors near the B-pillar and rocker area. Disconnect the battery and wait at least 90 seconds before grinding or welding near them, and keep the welding ground clamp on the frame close to the weld so current does not route through body electronics.\n- Fuel and brake lines. Both run along the frame rail on the driver side. Trace and shield them before an arc gets anywhere near.\n- Fire watch. Sound deadening, the carpet edge, and the wiring loom behind the rocker all burn. Have an extinguisher within reach and a helper watching the interior side.\n- KDSS trucks. On a KDSS-equipped 4Runner the hydraulic lines and the sway bar linkage live in this area. Confirm clearance before you weld and re-check after; [[4runner-kdss-bleed-4runner|the KDSS service guide]] covers what those lines do and why pinching one is expensive.\n- Rust. On any truck older than about ten years, inspect the rocker and frame for scale before you commit. Welding new steel to a rusted seam moves the failure point rather than fixing it. [[4runner-frame-rust|The frame rust guide]] covers how to judge what you are looking at.\n- If you cannot weld, do not improvise. A shop session at $400-600 or a quality bolt-on is the honest alternative \u2014 a poor weld on a structural mount is worse than no weld.\n\nAs of 2026, weld-on and hybrid sliders for a 4th or 5th gen 4Runner run roughly $700-1,200 a pair bare, plus $150-350 for powder coat if not included. Consumables, weld-through primer, and cavity wax add $50-80. A fab shop that welds customer-supplied sliders typically charges $400-600 for the pair.\n\nAll-in that is $900-1,700 done at home, or $1,200-2,200 with shop labor \u2014 against roughly $500-900 installed for a quality bolt-on. Pair them with [[4runner-skid-plates|proper skid plates]]; sliders protect the rockers, not the drivetrain underneath.\n\nStep up to weld-on or hybrid sliders only if you load the body on rock \u2014 the bolt-on is plenty for most 4Runners. Insist on DOM tube, at least 3/16 in mounting plates, and three or more frame attachment points per side. Prep to bright metal, tack and measure before you weld out, and wax the rocker cavity afterward. Done properly, it is the last slider the truck will ever need."},{"id":"4runner-locker-front","title":"Front Locker \u2014 Selectable Traction Up Front","vehicle":"4runner","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":3000},"tools_required":["dial indicator","inch-pound torque wrench","case spreader","bearing puller","air line tools","gear marking compound"],"skills_required":["axle setup","advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["locker","axle-upgrade","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker, Toyota 8 in clamshell IFS front, 30 spline \u2014 RD121 for 3.73 and numerically lower ratios","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1250},{"name":"ARB Air Locker, Toyota 8 in clamshell IFS front, 30 spline \u2014 RD111 for 3.91 and numerically higher ratios","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1250},{"name":"Air compressor, manifold, solenoid and switch","vendor":"ARB CKMA12 / CKMTA12","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"Carrier bearing, seal and setup kit","vendor":"Yukon / Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"East Coast Gear Supply \u2014 ARB RD121 Toyota 8 in IFS Air Locker, 30 spline, 3.73 and down","url":"https://eastcoastgearsupply.com/i-138654-toyota-8-ifs-arb-air-locker-rd121-30-spline-3-73-down.html"},{"title":"Stage 3 Motorsports \u2014 03-24 4Runner / 05-23 Tacoma 30-spline 8 in clamshell IFS ARB Air Locker (3.91 and up)","url":"https://www.stage3motorsports.com/rd111-03-22-4runner-05-22-tacoma-nitro-gear-axle-8-clamshell-ifs-arb-air-locker-3.91.html"},{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 ARB Air Locker manifold and solenoid install, running a front locker","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/09/14/arb-air-locker-manifold-solenoid-install/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-locker-rear","4runner-tire-fitment-33s"],"summary":"The rear locker in the companion guide is the first and biggest traction upgrade. A front locker is the next step for a 4Runner that wheels genuinely hard \u2014 it plants both front tires on a steep, off-camber ledge where an open front diff stalls the climb. The catch is the independent front end, which sets the rules for which locker you buy and how you are allowed to use it.","description":"With both rear tires already locked, traction loss moves to the front. Locking the front sends torque to the front tire that still has grip, which is what clears a stepped ledge or a loose, off-camber climb.\n\nOn the 4Runner's independent front suspension a selectable locker is the only sane type. An automatic or lunchbox locker in the front of an IFS truck loads the steering under power, produces unpredictable push in corners, and hammers the CVs on pavement. A selectable \u2014 an ARB air locker or an Eaton e-locker \u2014 stays fully open for normal steering and engages only when you flip the switch, so daily manners and tire scrub are untouched.\n\nEvery 4Runner from 2003 forward runs an 8 in clamshell, reverse-rotation front differential with 30-spline output. It is the same basic unit across 03-24 4Runners and 05-23 Tacomas, which is why the aftermarket support is good.\n\nThe part number is not one number, and this is where orders go wrong: the ARB front unit is split by ring gear ratio. RD121 covers 3.73 and numerically lower ratios; RD111 covers 3.91 and numerically higher. A 5th gen 4Runner leaves the factory at 3.727, so a truck that has not been regeared takes RD121 \u2014 but a truck that has already gone to 4.56 or 4.88 takes RD111. Confirm the ratio stamped on your carrier or count it by hand before ordering, and cross-check against ARB's current application chart, because supersessions happen.\n\nNote that the rear is a different animal: 03-09 trucks use an 8 in rear, and 2010 and later got the improved 8.2 in rear with shimmed side adjusters, more ring gear bolts, and larger bearings. Rear part numbers do not carry across to the front. [[4runner-locker-rear|The rear locker guide]] covers that side.\n\nThe independent front uses CV half-shafts, not a solid axle shaft. A locked front planting torque at full steering lock and full droop is exactly the load that snaps a CV or an inner stub. Three habits keep that from happening:\n\n1. Engage with the wheels as straight as the line allows. Torque through a CV at extreme angle is the failure case.\n2. Modulate the throttle. Bouncing the truck on a locked front is what breaks parts; steady, deliberate throttle is what climbs.\n3. Turn it off at the top. The front locker is a tool for a specific obstacle, not a setting you leave on. Driving a locked front on hard surfaces binds the driveline and scrubs tires.\n\nThis is the whole reason most builds lock the rear first and add the front only when the wheeling actually demands it. On 33s or larger, also budget for stronger half-shafts or accept that CVs become a consumable.\n\nThis is genuine axle-setup work, not a bolt-in:\n\n1. Drain the front diff, remove the front driveshaft, and pull the front differential assembly. On the 4Runner the clamshell design means the carrier comes out as an assembly rather than through a rear cover.\n2. Record the existing backlash and the carrier shim stack before you disturb anything. You are rebuilding to the same pattern unless you are regearing at the same time.\n3. Press the old carrier bearings off, press new ones onto the locker carrier, and install with the recorded shims as a starting point.\n4. Set backlash to spec \u2014 typically 0.005-0.008 in on this diff \u2014 and verify with a dial indicator on the ring gear.\n5. Check the tooth contact pattern with marking compound on both drive and coast sides. Adjust shims and repeat until the pattern is centered. This step is where the job is either done or not done.\n6. Set carrier bearing preload with an inch-pound torque wrench.\n7. Drill and fit the bulkhead for the air line, route the line away from heat and steering movement, and secure it every few inches. A chafed air line is the most common ARB failure and it always happens on the trail.\n8. Mount the compressor somewhere dry and protected, wire the solenoid and switch on a fused, switched circuit, and pressure-test at 100-105 psi before you refill the diff.\n9. Refill with 75W-85 or 75W-90 GL-5 gear oil per the service manual and check for leaks after a heat cycle.\n\nIf you are already opening the diff, do the regear at the same time. Paying axle-setup labor twice is the most avoidable expense in this whole job \u2014 see [[4runner-regear|the regear guide]] for ratio selection against tire size.\n\n- Air supply. An ARB needs a compressor and a protected line; an e-locker needs a clean switched circuit and a good ground. Budget for the support system, not the locker alone.\n- Set-up quality. A front diff that howls or eats bearings six months later is a shim job that was rushed. If you have not set a pattern before, this is the job to hand to a shop.\n- Front and rear ratios must match exactly on a full-time or part-time 4WD truck. Mismatched ratios destroy the transfer case.\n- Do not engage the front locker on pavement, and do not engage it at speed on a loose surface with the wheels turned.\n- Watch tie rods and ball joints after adding a front locker. The extra torque finds worn steering parts fast \u2014 [[4runner-tie-rods-4runner|the tie rod guide]] and [[4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner|the lower ball joint guide]] cover what to inspect.\n\nAs of 2026, an ARB front air locker for the 8 in clamshell runs roughly $1,150-1,400, plus $350-450 for a compressor, manifold, solenoid, and switch, plus $150-220 for bearings, seals, and setup hardware. Shop labor for the install and setup runs $700-1,200, and doing a regear at the same time adds roughly $400-600 in parts but very little extra labor.\n\nRealistic all-in: $1,700-2,200 doing your own setup work, $2,400-3,000 at a shop. An Eaton e-locker where available runs a few hundred less on the hardware but does not bring an air system you can also use for tires.\n\nAdd a front locker only after the rear, and only on a 4Runner that wheels hard enough to need it. Buy the selectable that matches your ring gear ratio \u2014 RD121 for 3.73 and lower, RD111 for 3.91 and higher on the 8 in clamshell. Drive it with the wheels straight to spare the CVs, set the carrier up properly, and combine it with a regear while the diff is open. It is the upgrade that unlocks the steepest lines."},{"id":"4runner-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker for the 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 ARB, Eaton, and TRD","vehicle":"4runner","category":"axles","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":649,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","dial indicator","gear oil","RTV sealant","bearing press or access to press"],"skills_required":["differential service","gear setup basics","bearing press operation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["locker","rear-locker","differential","traction","ARB","TRD","rock-crawling"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Toyota 8.9\" Rear Differential (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Eaton ELocker for Toyota 8.9\" (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"TRD Mechanical Locker (dealer-installed factory option, 4Runner Trail/TRD Pro)","vendor":"Toyota Racing Development","price_estimate_usd":650}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker Toyota 8.9\" Product Page","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker Toyota Product Page","url":"https://www.eaton.com/us/en-us/catalog/vehicle/eaton-elocker.html"}],"alternatives":["4runner-mid-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses a Toyota 8.9\" ring gear rear differential \u2014 the ARB Air Locker and Eaton ELocker are the two quality aftermarket options, and the TRD mechanical locker is the factory bolt-on solution available on Trail and TRD Pro trims.","description":"The 4Runner Trail Edition and TRD Pro models come from the factory with a TRD mechanical locker in the rear differential \u2014 it's a mechanical cam-and-pawl design that engages both rear wheels when actuated via a dash switch. If your 4Runner is a Trail or TRD Pro trim, you already have a rear locker. SR5 and Limited 4Runners do not have a factory locker and require aftermarket installation.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker for the Toyota 8.9\" rear axle is the highest-performance aftermarket option. When disengaged, it behaves exactly like a factory open differential \u2014 no on-road driving compromises. When engaged via air pressure, both rear wheels lock together for full traction in low-grip situations. The ARB locker requires an air compressor (dedicated ARB compressor, $400\u2013$700, or a dual-use on-board air system) and air line routing to the differential. The total system cost including the compressor is $1,250\u2013$1,550.\n\nThe Eaton ELocker eliminates the air requirement. It uses an electromagnetic coil to lock the carrier \u2014 flip the in-cab switch and both rear wheels lock. The engagement is clean and on-road behavior when disengaged is identical to an open differential. For builds where on-board air is not already planned (no air bumper, no air lockers front and rear), the Eaton ELocker is the most cost-effective and installation-friendly option.\n\nThe 4Runner 8.9\" rear differential uses a standard gear setup that most shops familiar with Toyota axles can service. Backlash spec is 0.005\"\u20130.008\" and carrier bearing preload follows Toyota's standard procedure. This is the same differential used in Tundra and Land Cruiser 200 Series applications, so parts availability and shop familiarity are good.","steps":["Verify rear axle ring gear size and carrier configuration \u2014 confirm 8.9\" application and 4WD vs 2WD carrier type","Drain rear differential gear oil","Remove rear axle shafts (backing plate nuts, pull shafts straight out)","Remove differential cover; clean all gasket surfaces","Remove carrier bearing caps \u2014 mark left/right orientation clearly before removal","Remove carrier assembly; note original shim positions at each bearing cup","Press out OEM side gears, spider gears, and cross-shaft","Install locker internals per manufacturer installation guide \u2014 ARB and Eaton both include detailed instructions","For ARB: install air fitting in differential cover; route air line through axle housing to bulkhead fitting","Reinstall carrier with original shims; set backlash to 0.005\"\u20130.008\" spec","Verify bearing preload with dial indicator per Toyota spec","Install differential cover with new gasket or RTV; torque bolts in star pattern","Reinstall axle shafts; torque backing plate nuts to spec","Refill with Toyota-spec GL-5 75W-90 gear oil","For ARB: route and connect air line to compressor; test engagement before trail use","For Eaton: route wiring harness to cab switch; test engagement"],"pros":["ARB Air Locker delivers full on-demand locking with zero on-road compromise when disengaged","Eaton ELocker requires no air system \u2014 cleaner install for builds without on-board air","TRD factory locker on Trail/TRD Pro trim is the most straightforward solution if you can start with the right trim","Toyota 8.9\" is a robust axle that handles locker loads well for the 4Runner's use profile","Rear locker provides traction that lift and tires cannot deliver \u2014 essential for serious rocky terrain"],"cons":["ARB Air Locker total system cost including compressor is $1,250\u2013$1,550","Differential carrier disassembly requires a bearing press and familiarity with gear setup \u2014 professional install recommended for first-time locker installers","Incorrect backlash setting causes premature ring-and-pinion wear","TRD dealer locker pricing varies by region \u2014 confirm current dealer pricing before planning","Eaton ELocker wiring harness routing through the firewall adds install time compared to the ARB air line approach on trucks already running on-board air"]},{"id":"4runner-regear","title":"Re-Gearing the 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Ratios for 33s and 35s","vehicle":"4runner","category":"axles","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2600},"tools_required":["dial indicator","bearing puller","press","inch-pound torque wrench","case spreader","marking compound"],"skills_required":["differential setup","gear pattern reading","precision torque"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["regear","axles","gears","differential","8-inch","ifs"],"parts":[{"name":"Nitro Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion Set (Front Clamshell + Rear 8\")","vendor":"Nitro Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":540},{"name":"Nitro Gear 5.29 Ring & Pinion Set (Front + Rear)","vendor":"Nitro Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":560},{"name":"Master Install Kit (front and rear, bearings + shims + seals)","vendor":"Nitro Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Nitro Gear 4Runner Ring & Pinion Catalog","url":"https://www.nitro-gear.com"},{"title":"Toyota 8-inch Differential Setup Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-locker-rear","4runner-tire-fitment-33s"],"summary":"Run 4.88 gears if you put 33s on a 5th gen 4Runner, and 5.29 if you go to 35s. The factory 3.73 (V6) or 4.30 (some packages) ratio leaves the 1GR-FE V6 hunting for gears and the transmission shifting constantly once you add tire weight and diameter.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses an independent front suspension with a clamshell front differential and a solid Toyota 8\" semi-float rear. Both ends carry the ring and pinion, so a re-gear means setting up two differentials \u2014 and the IFS front clamshell is one of the more demanding setups to get a correct gear pattern on. This is not a first-time differential job. If you have never set up gears before, pay a shop that has done Toyota 8\" clamshells specifically.\n\nThe reason you re-gear is tire diameter. Stock 4Runner tires are roughly 30\u201331\" tall. Going to a 285/70R17 (about 32.7\") or a 285/75R17 (about 34\") drops your effective gearing and forces the V6 to work harder, especially in the higher-altitude climbs around Phoenix and the Mogollon Rim. A 33\" tire on factory gears feels noticeably sluggish and the transmission downshifts on grades it used to hold. Re-gearing to 4.88 restores the factory drivability you had on stock tires. For 35s, 5.29 is the right target.\n\nYou probably don't need this if you stay on 32\" or smaller tires and mostly drive pavement \u2014 the penalty is mild and the cost is real. Re-gearing makes the most sense once you are at 33\"+ and using the truck off-road regularly.\n\nA complete re-gear needs a ring and pinion set for both axles in the same ratio, a master install kit (carrier and pinion bearings, crush sleeve or solid spacer, shims, seals, marking compound), and the setup tools: dial indicator with magnetic base, a press for the bearings, a case spreader for the front clamshell, and an inch-pound torque wrench for pinion preload. Budget marking compound and patience for multiple pattern checks per axle.\n\n1. Drain both differentials and remove the carriers, recording existing shim thicknesses as a starting reference\n2. Press old pinion bearings off; press new races into the housings\n3. Set pinion depth using the master kit's pinion shim, then check the gear pattern with marking compound \u2014 adjust shim until the contact pattern is centered on the tooth flank\n4. Set pinion preload with the crush sleeve (rear) or solid spacer plus shims (preferred for repeatability) to the inch-pound spec\n5. Set carrier bearing preload and backlash (0.005\"\u20130.008\" typical for the Toyota 8\") using the side shims\n6. Re-check the pattern under load, confirm backlash, then reassemble\n7. Repeat the full process on the front clamshell \u2014 the case spreader is required to seat the carrier\n8. Refill with the correct gear oil (75W-85 or 75W-90 GL-5; add friction modifier if running a clutch-type LSD/locker)\n\nThe front clamshell is where most home setups go wrong \u2014 the pinion depth and the spreader procedure are unforgiving, and a bad pattern howls or fails early. Always match ratios front and rear exactly; a mismatch binds the drivetrain in 4WD and destroys gears. Break in new gears gently: 500 miles of varied driving with no towing and a 30-minute cooldown after the first highway run, then change the oil to flush break-in metal. If your 4Runner has an electronic rear locker, re-gearing does not affect it, but confirm the carrier you keep is correct for your ratio split (most 8\" carriers cross the 4.88/5.29 break, but verify).\n\nGears and a master kit run about $800\u2013$900 in parts for both axles (Nitro Gear 4.88 set around $540, master install kit around $280). Professional setup of both differentials runs $900\u2013$1,700 in labor depending on shop, putting a turn-key re-gear at roughly $1,400\u2013$2,600. DIY drops it to parts cost plus tools, but the clamshell setup risk is real \u2014 a botched front pattern costs more than the labor you saved."},{"id":"4runner-frame-rust","title":"Frame Rust Inspection and Prevention on the 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"body","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":250},"tools_required":["flashlight","wire brush","pick or screwdriver","creeper","fluid film applicator"],"skills_required":["inspection","basic prep"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2024"],"tags":["body","rust","frame","corrosion","inspection","fluid-film"],"parts":[{"name":"Fluid Film Black (gallon, frame cavity coating)","vendor":"Fluid Film","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Woolwax Undercoating Kit with Spray Gun","vendor":"Woolwax","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"POR-15 Rust Preventive Coating (quart)","vendor":"POR-15","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota Frame Corrosion Service Campaigns","url":"https://www.toyota.com/recall"},{"title":"4Runner Frame Rust Inspection Threads","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-skid-plates","4runner-rear-bumper"],"summary":"Inspect the rear frame rails, the body mounts, and the area above the rear axle first \u2014 these are where 4Runner frames rot. A truck living in Phoenix is at low risk, but any 4Runner with a Rust Belt history needs a real inspection before you trust the frame, regardless of how clean the body looks.","description":"Toyota frame corrosion is a documented problem on Tacoma, Tundra, and 4Runner trucks from the salted-road states. Toyota ran frame replacement and inspection campaigns on several model years, but those windows have largely closed, and a used 4Runner you buy today is your responsibility to evaluate. The body can look spotless while the frame is scaling from the inside out, because the rails are boxed steel and rot starts in the cavities where you can't see it.\n\nIf your 4Runner has spent its life in Arizona or the Southwest, frame rust is genuinely unlikely and a quick visual is enough. The risk is buying a truck that was shipped from the Northeast or Midwest, or one that spent winters near the coast. Surface rust \u2014 a light orange dusting you can wire-brush back to solid metal \u2014 is normal and not a structural concern. What you're hunting for is scaling, flaking, and any spot where a pick or flat screwdriver punches through or crushes the steel. That means the section is compromised.\n\nA bright flashlight, a creeper to get fully under the truck, a wire brush, and a pick or flathead screwdriver for probing suspect areas. For prevention work, a frame cavity oil like Fluid Film or a wax-based undercoat like Woolwax plus a spray gun, and POR-15 for treating localized surface rust you've cleaned back to bare metal.\n\n1. Get the truck on stands or a lift so you can see the full length of both frame rails\n2. Inspect the rear half of the frame first \u2014 the section above and behind the rear axle, the rear spring hangers, and the rear body mounts are the classic failure zones\n3. Check every body mount: rotted mounts let the body shift and are a structural concern\n4. Probe any flaking or scaling area with the pick \u2014 solid steel resists, rotted steel crushes or punctures\n5. Inspect the frame near the steering box, the front crossmember, and the lower control arm mounts\n6. For prevention: wire-brush surface rust back to solid metal, treat with POR-15, then coat the whole frame and spray Fluid Film or Woolwax into the frame drain holes to protect the cavities\n7. Re-apply cavity coating annually if the truck sees salt or coastal air\n\nA flashlight inspection of the outside is not enough \u2014 the rot is internal, so probe and trust the pick over your eyes. Pay attention to the frame drain holes; if they're plugged with mud, the cavity holds moisture and rots faster. Never undercoat over active flaking rust without treating it first \u2014 you'll seal moisture against the metal. If you find a section that punctures or a body mount that's gone, stop treating it as a cosmetic issue: a perforated frame is a safety and structural problem, and on a heavily rotted truck the repair can exceed the vehicle's value. Walk away from a purchase candidate with frame perforation unless you're equipped to do frame work.\n\nInspection costs nothing but time. Prevention is cheap insurance: a gallon of Fluid Film runs about $45 and a Woolwax kit with a gun is around $130, enough for several seasons. Localized rust treatment with POR-15 is about $40 a quart. A professional frame-off rust repair or partial frame replacement runs into the thousands \u2014 which is exactly why the annual cavity coating on a clean frame is worth doing before rot ever starts."},{"id":"4runner-brake-upgrade-trail-tow","title":"Brake Upgrades for Trail and Towing (5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"brakes","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":500},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","brake bleeder","flare wrench","C-clamp"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","brake bleeding"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland","tow_rig"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["brakes","upgrade","towing","stainless-lines","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Powerstop / Akebono Severe-Duty Pads (front + rear)","vendor":"Akebono / Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Stainless Braided Brake Lines (4-line kit)","vendor":"Goodridge","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Slotted OEM-Size Rotors (pair, front)","vendor":"Centric / Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Braking Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Brake Pad Compound Comparison","url":"https://www.tirerack.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-pads-rotors","4runner-brake-fluid-flush"],"summary":"Loading a 4Runner with armor, 33s, and a trailer changes its stopping distance, and the fix is rarely a \"big brake kit\" \u2014 it's better pads, fresh fluid, and stainless lines for a firm pedal. For most built 4Runners, severe-duty pads and a high-temp fluid flush deliver the biggest real-world improvement for the least money. Save the four-piston conversions for dedicated tow rigs.","description":"> **Safety critical.** Brakes are not the place to experiment with bargain parts or skip the bleed. If you're not confident bleeding the system to a firm pedal, this is a job to hand to a shop.\n\nThe 5th gen 4Runner's factory brakes are adequate for a stock truck but get overwhelmed when you add rotating mass (heavier wheels and 33s), weight (bumpers, drawers, fuel), and a trailer. The result is a longer pedal, fade on a long descent, and a soft feel as the rubber flex lines balloon under hard pressure. The honest order of upgrades is: high-temperature fluid, then a quality severe-duty pad compound, then stainless lines for pedal firmness, and only then larger hardware if you genuinely tow heavy and regularly.\n\nA big-brake kit looks impressive and adds real thermal capacity, but for most overland and trail builds it's money that would do more on tires, recovery gear, or suspension. Be honest about your use: a weekend trail truck does not need $1,500 of brake hardware.\n\nA metric socket set and torque wrench, a flare wrench for the line fittings, a C-clamp or caliper tool to compress the pistons, and a brake bleeder (one-person bleeder, vacuum, or a helper). Severe-duty pads front and rear, stainless braided lines, fresh high-temp DOT 3/4 fluid, and slotted OEM-size rotors if yours are worn or you want a touch more fade resistance.\n\n1. Replace pads and rotors first using standard procedure; bed the pads per the manufacturer's instructions\n2. To add stainless lines, unbolt each factory rubber line at the hard-line junction and caliper, capping the hard line to limit fluid loss\n3. Install the braided line with new copper crush washers and route it clear of suspension travel and tire rub at full stuff and full lock\n4. Bleed every corner starting at the farthest from the master cylinder until fluid runs clear and bubble-free\n5. With the engine off, pump to a firm pedal; if it stays soft, bleed again \u2014 air is the usual cause\n6. Test at low speed in a safe area before any loaded drive, checking for pull, soft pedal, or line rub at full lock\n\nRoute stainless lines so they cannot contact the tire at full lock or rub the knuckle through suspension travel \u2014 a chafed line is a brake failure. Use fresh crush washers every time; reused ones weep. Bleed thoroughly and confirm a firm pedal before trusting the truck \u2014 a spongy pedal after a line swap means trapped air, not \"it'll settle.\" Bed new pads properly or you'll get uneven deposits and a pulsing pedal that feels like warped rotors.\n\nSevere-duty pads run about $130 for all four corners, stainless lines around $120 for a four-line kit, and slotted rotors roughly $140 a pair up front. A complete pad/rotor/line/fluid refresh lands near $400 in parts. A four-piston big-brake kit starts around $1,200 and is honestly more than most builds need \u2014 spend there only if you tow heavy on grades regularly."},{"id":"4runner-front-pads-rotors","title":"Front Brake Pads and Rotors","vehicle":"4runner","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":350},"tools_required":["19mm socket","14mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp or caliper compressor","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM front brake pads (2010-2019)","part_number":"04465-35290","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Toyota OEM front brake pads (2020-2024)","part_number":"04465-35330","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Toyota OEM front rotors (pair)","part_number":"43512-60240","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Akebono ProACT ceramic pads (low dust)","part_number":"ACT1303","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Powerstop drilled & slotted rotors (alternative)","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Front Brake Replacement Walkthrough","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/03/26/replacing-brake-pads-rotors-4runner/"},{"title":"4Runner Brake Parts List and Torque Specs","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/brake-replacement-parts-list-5th-gen.34536/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-rear-brakes"],"summary":"Front pads on the 4Runner run 40,000\u201360,000 miles in mixed driving; the job takes under 2 hours, costs $120 for pads-only or $350 with new OEM rotors, and the caliper bracket bolts torque to 91 ft-lb.","description":"The 4Runner's front brake setup is conventional sliding-caliper, single-piston-per-side \u2014 the same architecture Toyota has used on body-on-frame trucks for decades. That's a good thing: parts are common, the procedure hasn't changed across the 4th gen (2003\u20132009) and 5th gen (2010\u20132024), and you can usually do the job with hand tools you already own.\n\nPads first, rotors when needed. Toyota's spec is to replace rotors when they fall below 27mm (factory thickness is 28mm on most years). In practice, you'll get one set of pads on a healthy rotor before warpage or scoring forces a replacement. If you off-road and dunk the rotors in water \u2014 creek crossings, mud \u2014 they may warp at 30,000 miles even when pad life is still good. That's not a defect, it's heat-cycling.\n\nPad choice matters for what you'll feel pedal-side. OEM Toyota pads (Advics or Akebono OE) are the quietest and produce moderate dust. Akebono ProACT ceramics produce less dust than OE and feel nearly identical. Avoid the bargain semi-metallic pads at chain auto parts stores \u2014 they bite harder cold but they grind your rotors faster and squeal in stop-and-go. For loaded overlanding rigs running 33s, step up to EBC Yellowstuff or Powerstop Z36 \u2014 they tolerate sustained heat from steep descents better than OE.\n\nRotors: stick with Toyota OEM or Centric Premium. Drilled-and-slotted from Powerstop or R1 Concepts look aggressive but offer zero performance benefit for a 4,800-lb truck on the street \u2014 the drill holes become stress risers and the slots wear pads faster. If you want better fade resistance, the upgrade path is bigger brakes (Powerbrake X-Line for 6th gen Tacoma calipers swap) not \"performance\" rotors on stock calipers.\n\nThe two caliper bracket bolts (19mm, 91 ft-lb) and the two caliper slider bolts (14mm, 30 ft-lb) are the critical torques. Get either wrong and the caliper either binds (rotor drag, premature pad wear, hot wheels) or works loose (vibration, eventual failure). Always use a torque wrench on brakes \u2014 they're life-safety hardware.","steps":["Loosen front lug nuts while wheels are on the ground. Lift and support the front of the truck on jack stands at the frame.","Remove front wheels. Inspect old rotor and pad thickness.","Open the brake fluid reservoir cap (relieves pressure when compressing pistons). Cover the fender with a rag.","Remove the two caliper slider bolts (14mm) on the rear of the caliper. Slide the caliper off the bracket. Hang it from a coat hanger or bungee \u2014 never let it dangle by the hose.","Pull old pads and shims out of the bracket. Note orientation.","Compress the piston back into the caliper with a C-clamp using an old pad as a buffer. Move slowly to avoid forcing fluid backward into ABS.","Remove the two caliper bracket bolts (19mm) from the steering knuckle. Lift bracket off.","Pull the old rotor. Wire-brush the hub face clean to the bare metal \u2014 even small rust ridges cause runout.","Install new rotor (lightly oil the hub face to prevent rust seizure \u2014 keep oil off the friction surface). Reinstall the bracket. Torque the bracket bolts to 91 ft-lb.","Install new pads with new anti-rattle shims/clips. Apply a thin film of brake grease to the back of the pad and to the slider pins.","Slide the caliper over the pads onto the bracket. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 30 ft-lb.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 83 ft-lb (5th gen) or 76 ft-lb (4th gen).","Pump the brake pedal 5\u20136 times until firm before driving. Top off fluid. Bed in pads with 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph."],"pros":["Standard sliding caliper \u2014 no special tools, common torque sizes","Same procedure 2003\u20132024 with only pad/rotor part number changes","OEM Toyota parts under $300 for full job; $120 pads only","50K+ pad life typical for daily-driven 4Runners"],"cons":["Rotors warp from creek crossings before they wear out on heavy trail rigs","Cheap semi-metallic pads will grind rotors faster than they save you money","The 2020+ pad part number changed \u2014 verify by VIN before ordering","No factory brake upgrade path; bigger brakes mean swapping calipers from Tundra or aftermarket"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-brakes-4runner","title":"Rear Brake Pads and Rotors","vehicle":"4runner","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":280},"tools_required":["17mm socket","14mm socket","c-clamp or brake caliper tool","torque wrench","wire brush","brake cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["safety-critical","wear-item"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM Toyota Rear Brake Pad Set","part_number":"04466-60140","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Akebono Pro-ACT Rear Pads (ceramic)","part_number":"ACT995","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"OEM rear rotor (pair)","vendor":"Toyota / Centric","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"4Runner Brake Pad and Rotor Installation guide","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/03/26/replacing-brake-pads-rotors-4runner/"},{"title":"How to Replace Rear Brake Pads & Rotors on a 2018 Toyota 4Runner","url":"https://howtoo.com/guide/orion/how-to-replace-rear-brake-pads-rotors-on-a-2018-toyota-4runner-diy-guide"},{"title":"Brake Replacement Parts List 5th Gen","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/brake-replacement-parts-list-5th-gen.34536/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-pads-rotors-4runner","4runner-brake-fluid-flush-4runner"],"summary":"Rear brake pads and rotors on a 5th gen 4Runner are a one-hour-per-side job; OEM pads (04466-60140) are the right choice for daily and overland duty because they handle the truck's weight without dust storms.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses a single-piston floating caliper at each rear corner, sliding on two greased pins. Pads typically last 50,000\u201380,000 miles depending on how much towing and trail braking the truck sees. Rotors last for two pad sets if you bed in cleanly. Slotted or drilled rotors offer no real benefit on a 4Runner \u2014 Toyota's solid rear rotors run cool enough, and slotting adds noise. Centric Premium or OEM-replacement smooth rotors are the right call.\n\nPad choice matters more than rotor choice. Akebono Pro-ACT ($45) is a low-dust ceramic option and is what comes on many Toyota and Lexus models from the factory. Hawk LTS ($60) bites harder when towing or descending grades but throws more dust. Avoid semi-metallic budget pads \u2014 they chew rotors and squeal cold. For a truck doing trail recovery, overland trips, or any towing, ceramic OEM-spec pads are the right balance.\n\nCaliper bracket bolts torque to 90 Nm (66 ft-lbs). Slide pin bolts torque to 34 Nm (25 ft-lbs). The slide pins must be clean and re-greased with high-temp silicone caliper grease \u2014 a dry pin is the most common cause of uneven pad wear and one-sided heat. The piston pushes straight back without rotation on this caliper, so a standard C-clamp works to retract it before pad install. Don't force it \u2014 open the bleeder on the caliper before retracting if the master cylinder is full, otherwise you push old fluid backward through the ABS unit.\n\nBed-in matters: after install, do 8 to 10 medium stops from 40 mph down to 10 mph without coming to a full stop, then drive five minutes to cool. This transfers an even layer of pad material to the rotor and is what separates a quiet new brake job from a job that pulses and squeals.","steps":["Loosen rear lug nuts, jack up rear, support on stands, remove wheels.","Remove the two caliper slide pin bolts (14mm) on the back of the caliper.","Swing the caliper up off the bracket and hang it from a coil with a wire \u2014 do not let it dangle by the hose.","Pull old pads out of the bracket; note the shim and clip orientation.","Remove the two caliper bracket bolts (17mm) to free the rotor.","Wire-brush the hub face, then slide the new rotor on.","Torque the bracket bolts to 66 ft-lbs.","Retract the caliper piston with a C-clamp, with the bleeder cracked if fluid is full.","Insert new pads with anti-rattle clips greased on the contact ears only.","Re-grease slide pins, reinstall caliper, torque slide bolts to 25 ft-lbs.","Pump the brake pedal to seat pads before driving.","Bed in with 8\u201310 medium stops from 40 to 10 mph."],"pros":["One-hour-per-side DIY with hand tools","OEM-equivalent pads keep the truck quiet and clean","Inexpensive vs. shop labor"],"cons":["Requires correct torque on slide pins to avoid uneven wear","Cheap pads squeal and chew rotors \u2014 buy decent ones","Pumping fluid backward through ABS can damage the module if you skip the bleeder step"]},{"id":"4runner-cooling-service","title":"Cooling System Service \u2014 Coolant, Radiator, and Trans Cooling","vehicle":"4runner","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":350},"tools_required":["drain pan","socket set","funnel with no-spill adapter","hose pliers"],"skills_required":["fluid service","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["cooling","coolant","radiator","trans-cooler","maintenance","1gr-fe"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Super Long Life Coolant (SLLC, pink, premixed gallon)","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"OEM Toyota Radiator (1GR-FE 4.0L V6)","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":240},{"name":"Aftermarket Transmission Cooler (stacked-plate, with fittings)","vendor":"Hayden / Derale","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota SLLC Coolant Specification","url":"https://www.toyota.com"},{"title":"1GR-FE Cooling System Service Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues","4runner-atf-flush"],"summary":"Use only Toyota Super Long Life Coolant (the pink SLLC) and change it at the factory interval \u2014 the first change at roughly 100,000 miles, then every 50,000 after. The 1GR-FE V6 cooling system is reliable, but it's specific about coolant chemistry, and the towing and low-speed crawling that off-road use adds make the transmission cooling the part most worth upgrading.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner runs the 1GR-FE 4.0L V6, a stout engine with a cooling system that gives little trouble when serviced correctly. The one hard rule is coolant type: Toyota's SLLC is a pink, pre-mixed organic-acid formula, and mixing it with a green or universal coolant degrades its corrosion protection and can gel. If you're topping off or refilling, use SLLC and nothing else.\n\nWhere off-road and tow use actually stresses the 4Runner is transmission heat, not engine heat. Slow crawling in low range, long climbs, and towing all generate ATF heat with little airflow to shed it, and the factory cooler is sized for normal driving. Adding an auxiliary transmission cooler is the single most valuable cooling upgrade for a 4Runner that works for a living. Engine overheating, by contrast, is rare on a healthy 1GR-FE and usually points to a failing water pump, a clogged radiator, or low coolant rather than an undersized system.\n\nFor a coolant change: Toyota SLLC (about two gallons premixed for a full drain and fill), a drain pan, and a funnel with a no-spill adapter to burp air from the system. For a transmission cooler add-on: a stacked-plate cooler, mounting hardware, hose, and clamps or fittings to splice into the cooler lines.\n\n1. With the engine cold, open the radiator drain petcock and remove the cap to drain the coolant into a pan\n2. Close the drain, refill with SLLC, and run the engine with the cap off (or with a no-spill funnel) until the thermostat opens and the level drops\n3. Top off, squeeze the upper hose to burp trapped air, and bring the system to full operating temp with the heater on high\n4. Check for leaks, confirm the level at the overflow when cold, and recycle the old coolant\n5. For a trans cooler: mount the auxiliary cooler ahead of the A/C condenser or radiator with good airflow\n6. Splice it into the transmission cooler line (typically the return line back to the pan), route hose away from heat and moving parts, and clamp securely\n7. Check ATF level after a drive cycle and inspect all connections for leaks before trusting it off-road\n\nNever mix coolant types \u2014 if the system has been topped with the wrong color, do a full flush and refill with SLLC. Burp the air out thoroughly; a trapped air pocket creates a false hot spot and can spike the temp gauge even with a full system. When adding a trans cooler, don't undersize the hose or kink it on the routing, and keep it clear of the exhaust and the fan. If your temp gauge climbs on a grade with a healthy system, check the fan clutch and the radiator fins for mud packing before assuming a deeper problem \u2014 trail silt clogs the radiator face and is an manageable fix.\n\nA DIY coolant change is about $40\u2013$55 in SLLC. An auxiliary transmission cooler runs $80\u2013$120 in parts and an hour or two to install \u2014 the best cooling money you can spend on a wheeling 4Runner. A replacement OEM radiator, if yours is clogged or weeping, is around $240 plus coolant. The full system rarely needs more than fluid and the occasional radiator over the life of the truck."},{"id":"4runner-radiator-fan-clutch","title":"Radiator and Fan Clutch Replacement (5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":500},"tools_required":["socket set","fan clutch wrench set","drain pan","pliers"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","cooling system service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["cooling","radiator","fan-clutch","overheating","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Denso OEM Radiator (1GR-FE)","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Aisin Fan Clutch (1GR-FE)","vendor":"Aisin","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Toyota SLLC Pink Coolant (1 gal)","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Cooling System Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Denso Radiator Application Data","url":"https://www.denso-am.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-cooling-service","4runner-water-pump"],"summary":"If your 4Runner creeps toward hot while towing or crawling in Phoenix heat, the two parts that matter most are the radiator and the engine-driven fan clutch. A tired fan clutch is the quiet culprit behind low-speed overheating, and a 12\u201315 year old plastic-tank radiator is a roadside breakdown waiting to happen. Both are doable at home in an afternoon for $150\u2013$300 in parts.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses a mechanical fan clutch driven off the water pump \u2014 not an electric fan \u2014 so cooling at low speed and idle depends entirely on that clutch grabbing hard when the engine bay heats up. A worn clutch freewheels, and you'll watch the gauge climb in stop-and-go traffic or on a slow technical climb while it stays fine at highway speed. The radiator is a plastic-tank/aluminum-core design, and the plastic tanks get brittle with age and Arizona heat, eventually splitting at a seam.\n\nDoing both together makes sense because they share the same access and the same coolant drain. This is intermediate work \u2014 the fan clutch nut is often seized and needs a proper fan clutch wrench set, and the radiator swap means handling brittle hoses and the transmission cooler lines.\n\nA metric socket set, a fan clutch wrench set (the holding tool plus the large clutch nut wrench), pliers for hose clamps, and a drain pan. A Denso OEM radiator, an Aisin fan clutch, fresh hoses if yours are original, and Toyota SLLC. If your truck has the factory tow package, have a flare wrench and catch pan ready for the trans cooler line fittings.\n\n1. Drain the cooling system cold from the radiator petcock\n2. Remove the fan shroud halves, then break the fan clutch loose from the water pump hub with the clutch wrench set (it's a normal right-hand thread on this engine)\n3. Disconnect the upper and lower radiator hoses and, on tow-package trucks, the transmission cooler lines \u2014 catch the ATF that drains\n4. Lift the radiator out, transfer any brackets, and set the new radiator in place\n5. Bolt the new fan clutch to the hub and reinstall the shroud\n6. Reconnect hoses and cooler lines, refill with SLLC, and burp the system to temperature\n7. Watch the gauge through a full warm-up and a test drive, and recheck coolant level after it cools\n\nThe fan clutch nut fights back \u2014 don't round it with a crescent wrench, use the right tool. On the radiator, the lower hose and the trans cooler lines are where people make a mess; have pans ready and expect to lose a little ATF, then top the transmission afterward. Don't skip burping the system, and don't trust a \"it warmed up fine in the driveway\" test \u2014 load it on a drive before you call it done. As always on the 1GR-FE, Toyota SLLC only.\n\nA Denso radiator runs about $180, an Aisin fan clutch around $120, and coolant $30, so a combined job is roughly $250\u2013$330 in parts. Done separately at a shop, expect $400\u2013$500 each. If you're already chasing a low-speed overheat, replace both while you're in there \u2014 the labor overlap makes the second part nearly free in time."},{"id":"4runner-cv-axle-4runner","title":"Front CV Axle Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":450},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","35mm socket","19mm socket","breaker bar","torque wrench","pry bar","hammer","anti-seize","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","front-end disassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["drivetrain","axles"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM Front CV Axle (L/R) \u2014 5th Gen","part_number":"43430-60083","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Toyota Updated Axle Nut","part_number":"90177-A0023","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"GSP Premium Loaded CV Axle","vendor":"GSP","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Cardone Select New CV Axle","vendor":"Cardone","price_estimate_usd":160}],"citations":[{"title":"How To Replace Front CV Axle \u2014 4Runners.com","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/how-to-replace-front-cv-axle.2527/"},{"title":"Toyota 4Runner Front End Torque Specs (2020)","url":"https://toyota-specs.com/cars/2020/4runner4.0/torque-specs_front.php"},{"title":"OEM Toyota CV Axle 43430-60083","url":"https://parts.toyotaofnashua.com/oem-parts/toyota-2023-2024-toyota-4runner-cv-axle-left-right-front-4343060083"},{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner CV Boot Replacement Guide","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2023/08/30/cv-boot-replacement-guide-5th-gen-4runner/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tie-rods-4runner","4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner"],"summary":"Replace a 5th-gen 4Runner front CV axle with a Toyota OEM unit (PN 43430-60083) in two to four hours; torque the axle nut to 174 ft-lbs and use the updated nut PN 90177-A0023 with new OEM axles.","description":"Front CV axles on the 5th gen 4Runner (2010-onward) and the related GX460 typically fail from torn boots first. The boot rips on rocks or against a poorly clearanced UCA after a lift, grease flings out, the joint runs dry, and within a few thousand miles you have a clicking sound on tight turns under throttle. Once you hear the click, the joint is done \u2014 re-booting at that stage is a temporary fix. Replace the axle assembly.\n\nToyota OEM is the honest answer for stock or moderately lifted 4Runners that see daily driving. The OEM axle (43430-60083 fits both sides on 2011-2024 4Runners and FJ Cruisers) is rebuildable, the boots last twice as long as most aftermarket boots, and the joint stays quiet at full droop with lift. Expect about $400-450 for the OEM unit. Note that current production OEM axles require the updated axle nut (90177-A0023) \u2014 buy two if you're doing both sides.\n\nAftermarket options like GSP Premium Loaded or Cardone Select run $140-170 and are acceptable for stock-height 4Runners. They will not hold up as well on a lifted truck running 33s or 34s and dropping into the throttle in 4-low. The cheapest econo-brand axles ($60-90) bind at lift-height droop angles and tear boots within a year \u2014 skip them.\n\nIf you are lifted more than 2.5 inches up front, you are running the CV at angles it was never designed to operate at. The two things that prolong axle life on a lifted 4Runner are: (1) limiting down-travel with proper bumpstops so the CV never goes past its safe angle, and (2) upgrading to aftermarket upper control arms that re-locate the upper ball joint to keep the spindle more vertical. Without those changes, even an OEM CV axle is on a clock.\n\nCommon mistake: forgetting to back off the axle nut before lifting the truck. The nut requires 174 ft-lbs of torque to remove and the wheel needs to be on the ground (or a helper holding the brake) so the hub doesn't spin.","steps":["Loosen the front lug nuts, then loosen the axle nut while the wheel is still on the ground \u2014 staking-clip side must be unpeened first using a punch.","Jack the front, set jack stands under the frame rails, and remove the wheel.","Pull the brake caliper off and hang it from the coil spring with mechanic's wire. Do not unhook the brake line.","Remove the rotor.","Disconnect the outer tie rod from the steering knuckle (19mm castle nut, cotter pin first).","Loosen and remove the two lower control arm bolts at the spindle (19mm). Do not disturb the LCA-to-frame bolts \u2014 those affect alignment.","Pull the axle nut off completely. Swing the knuckle outward and pull the CV stub out of the hub. If it's stuck, give it a few persuasive hits with a brass drift on the threaded end.","Pry the inner CV joint out of the front differential with a pry bar against the inner cup. Catch the small amount of diff fluid that leaks.","Wire-brush both the hub splines and the differential output splines. Apply anti-seize to the hub splines.","Slide the new axle's inner joint into the differential \u2014 it should click into the C-clip with firm hand pressure. If it won't seat, you'll need to tap it home gently with a soft mallet.","Reconnect the outer stub through the hub, reinstall the LCA bolts and torque to 118 ft-lbs, reconnect the tie rod (67 ft-lbs castle nut, new cotter pin).","Install the NEW axle nut (PN 90177-A0023 if running new OEM axle). Torque to 174 ft-lbs with the wheel on the ground. Stake the nut collar into the groove on the axle.","Reinstall rotor, caliper, and wheel. Torque lug nuts to 83 ft-lbs.","Check front diff fluid level \u2014 top off if any was lost during the swap."],"pros":["Stops the clicking and prevents stranding when the joint finally lets go","OEM-quality axles last 100,000+ miles on a stock-height truck","Clean access compared to many other vehicles \u2014 no ABS sensor or vacuum disconnect to fight"],"cons":["Axle nut requires impact tools or a long breaker bar to break free","Old axle inner joint can be stubborn out of the differential \u2014 wear safety glasses","C-clip on the inner stub must seat fully or the axle will work itself loose under throttle"]},{"id":"4runner-front-driveshaft-ujoint","title":"Front Driveshaft and U-Joint Service (4th and 5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"drivetrain","type":"procedure","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":700},"tools_required":["metric socket set","torque wrench","hydraulic press or heavy bench vise","angle grinder or die grinder","snap ring pliers","grease gun","paint marker","pry bar"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","u-joint service","press work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["drivetrain","driveshaft","u-joint","vibration","4wd","clunk"],"parts":[{"name":"SKF UJ-10430 serviceable u-joint (clip-retained conversion \u2014 verify cap diameter and span against your shaft)","vendor":"SKF","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Toyota OEM front driveshaft assembly (5th gen 4WD)","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Aftermarket front driveshaft assembly (non-staked joints)","vendor":"aftermarket","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"Driveshaft flange bolt hardware (replace if stretched)","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":20}],"citations":[{"title":"4WD Front Driveshaft U-Joint Replacement \u2014 4Runner Forum","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org/4th-gen-t4rs/252362-4wd-front-driveshaft-u-joint-replacement.html"},{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Drivetrain Rebuild: Driveshafts","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/5th-gen-4runner-drivetrain-rebuild-driveshafts-rebuild.37444/"},{"title":"Replacing staked-in U-joints on Toyota driveshafts (method and SKF UJ-10430 conversion)","url":"https://www.toyotanation.com/threads/replacing-staked-in-u-joints-on-04-highlander-awd.1699639/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-cv-axle-4runner","4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner","4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner"],"summary":"A clunk on 4WD engagement or a vibration that builds under throttle in 4HI usually traces to a dry or seized front driveshaft U-joint. Here is the part most write-ups get wrong: the factory joints on the 4Runner front shaft are **staked in**, not clip-retained. There are no snap rings to pull. Servicing one means grinding the factory stakes out of the yoke bores and converting to a clip-retained joint like the SKF UJ-10430 \u2014 a real press job, not a driveway hour. Budget $30 in parts and an afternoon if you have a press, $100-200 to hand the shaft to a driveline shop, or $300-550 for a complete replacement shaft.","description":"The 4Runner's front driveshaft leads a rough life for a part nobody thinks about. On SR5, Trail, and TRD Off-Road trucks the transfer case is part-time, so in 2WD the front shaft turns with the wheels but carries no torque and sees no articulation cycling \u2014 the joints sit in one position, the grease migrates, and the needle bearings brinell into the cup races. On Limited models with the full-time Torsen center differential the front shaft is always driven, which keeps the joints exercised but wears them faster in raw miles. Different failure paths, same part.\n\n**Clunk on 4WD engagement or on throttle transition.** The most common complaint. A worn joint has enough rotational backlash that the shaft snaps through it each time load reverses. Confirm by crawling under the truck with it in neutral and the wheels chocked: grab the shaft with both hands and try to rotate it against the flanges. Any perceptible rotational play at the joint, as opposed to normal driveline lash at the diff, is a bad joint.\n\n**Vibration that builds with speed in 4WD only.** Points at the front shaft specifically, since it is only loaded in 4WD on part-time trucks. A vibration present in 2WD is a rear shaft, tire balance, or carrier bearing issue instead.\n\n**Rust-colored powder around the bearing caps.** The tell-tale. Fretting corrosion from a dry joint pushes fine reddish-brown dust out around the cap seals. If you see it, the joint is dry and its remaining life is short.\n\n**Notchy rotation.** With the shaft out, work each joint through its full sweep by hand. It should move smoothly in every direction with light, even resistance. Notchiness, a tight spot, or any grinding feel means replace.\n\n**Slip yoke binding.** Separate failure, often mistaken for a joint. The splined slip section should telescope by hand with moderate effort. A seized slip yoke transmits driveline plunge straight into the transfer case output and diff pinion, and it produces a shudder on suspension compression rather than a speed-related vibration.\n\nToyota assembles these driveshafts by pressing a die into the yoke bore after the bearing cap is seated, rolling a burr of yoke material over the cap edge to lock it in. There is no groove and no clip. That construction is why Toyota sells the driveshaft as an assembly and why dealers will tell you the joints are not serviceable.\n\nThey are serviceable, with caveats. The conversion is well documented across the Toyota truck platforms: grind or drill away the staked material at all four cap bores until the caps can move, press the old joint out, clean the bores back to round, and install a clip-retained joint that carries its own retaining grooves. The SKF UJ-10430 is the joint most commonly cited for this conversion on Toyota truck shafts. **Measure before you buy** \u2014 cap diameter and cap-to-cap span vary by shaft and year, and a joint that is close but not correct will either not press in or will press in loose. Take the old joint or the shaft to a driveline shop and have them confirm the number.\n\nIf that paragraph made you uneasy, that is the correct reaction, and the alternative is legitimate: a local driveline shop will do the same conversion for $100-200 with the right press and tooling, and they will balance the shaft afterward. For a lot of owners that is the better trade.\n\nMetric sockets and a torque wrench, a hydraulic press (a 12-ton shop press, or a heavy bench vise with sockets as a distant second choice), an angle grinder or die grinder with a carbide burr for the stakes, snap ring pliers for the new clips, a grease gun, a paint marker, and a pry bar. A bearing splitter helps. Penetrating oil for the flange bolts, which live in road salt and mud.\n\n1. **Chock the rear wheels, set the parking brake, and support the truck on stands.** Never work under a truck on a jack.\n2. **Mark the shaft-to-flange orientation at both ends** with a paint marker, and mark which end faces the transfer case. Driveshafts are balanced as assemblies and clocking one 180 degrees introduces a vibration that no amount of joint work will cure.\n3. **Break the flange bolts loose** at the front differential and transfer case flanges. They are frequently seized \u2014 penetrating oil and patience beat a rounded head.\n4. **Remove the shaft.** Support its weight as the last bolts come out.\n5. **Clean the yoke bores** with a wire wheel so you can see the staked material clearly.\n6. **Grind the stakes.** Work the burr away from each of the four cap bores with a carbide burr or a small grinding stone, removing the rolled-over material and no more. You are removing the lip, not opening the bore.\n7. **Press the old joint out.** Support the yoke ear, press one cap through, rotate, press the other. Expect it to be stubborn \u2014 a staked joint that has never moved often needs heat on the yoke ear and real press force.\n8. **Clean and inspect the bores.** They must be round, clean, and free of galling. A bore that got opened up by the grinder is scrap, and that shaft now needs replacement.\n9. **Press the new joint in square.** Start one cap, insert the cross, then work both caps in alternately with the cross held centered. Keep every needle bearing standing in its cup \u2014 one tipped needle produces a tight joint that fails within a few thousand miles.\n10. **Seat the retaining clips** in their grooves, both sides of both caps.\n11. **Check the joint** by hand through its full sweep. Smooth and free, no bind, no notch. If it is tight, seat the caps by striking the yoke ears sharply with a brass hammer, which relieves the preload.\n12. **Grease the joint** until clean grease purges from all four cap seals.\n13. **Reinstall in the marked clock position** and torque the flange bolts to the service-manual value for your year \u2014 do not guess, and do not reuse visibly stretched hardware.\n14. **Test.** Quiet surface, 4HI, gentle acceleration and deceleration. The clunk and vibration should be gone.\n\n**Grinding into the bore is the failure that ends the job.** Take the stake and stop. If you open the bore even slightly, the new cap presses in loose, walks under load, and destroys the yoke.\n\n**Pressing a joint in cocked cracks a cap.** Go slow, check squareness constantly, and back off and realign the moment anything binds.\n\n**Balance is real.** Reinstall in the original clock position. If a vibration remains after a known-good joint, the shaft is bent or out of balance and the answer is a shop balance or a replacement shaft.\n\n**Do not neglect the slip yoke.** While the shaft is out, grease the slip splines and confirm they telescope freely. This is the second-most-common front driveline complaint and you are already there.\n\n**Exercise 4WD.** The root cause of most of these failures is disuse. Engage 4HI for a few miles on a loose surface once a month. It keeps the joints, the slip yoke, the front diff, and the actuator alive. While you are thinking about it, check the front diff fluid \u2014 [[4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner]] \u2014 and the transfer case, [[4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner]].\n\n**Consider the whole shaft on a high-mileage truck.** If both joints are bad, the slip yoke is worn, and the shaft has 180,000 miles on it, the arithmetic favors a replacement assembly with non-staked joints over rebuilding a tired core.\n\n| Path | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| DIY conversion joint (SKF UJ-10430 class) | $25-40 per joint |\n| Driveline shop: press joints, balance shaft | $100-250 |\n| Aftermarket front driveshaft assembly | $280-380 |\n| Toyota OEM front driveshaft assembly | $500-700 |\n| Dealer labor to replace the shaft | $150-300 on top of the part |\n\nThe value play is the driveline shop. They have the press, the correct joints on the shelf, the tooling to keep the bores true, and a balancer \u2014 for $100-250 you get a shaft that is right, without owning a press or gambling on grinder control. DIY the removal and reinstallation, which is the part that saves real labor, and hand them the shaft on the bench."},{"id":"4runner-alternator-replacement","title":"Alternator Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"electrical","subcategory":"charging","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":750},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","10mm socket","12mm socket","14mm socket","serpentine belt tool","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["serpentine belt routing","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["1GR-FE","charging","winch","dual-battery"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM Toyota alternator 130A (5th gen 2010-2021)","part_number":"27060-31190","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":540},{"name":"Denso 130A reman alternator (4.0L 1GR-FE)","part_number":"104210-2864","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"DC Power 270A XP high-output alternator (5th gen)","part_number":"11517-270XP","vendor":"DC Power Inc.","price_estimate_usd":740},{"name":"Aftermarket 130A direct-fit (BBB / Quality-Built / Remy)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota OEM 27060-31190 Alternator \u2014 American Toyota Parts","url":"https://parts.americantoyota.com/oem-parts/toyota-alternator-2706031190"},{"title":"Rareelectrical 130A Alternator 1GR-FE Specs","url":"https://www.rareelectrical.com/i-26933570-130a-alternator-compatible-with-2010-2021-toyota-4runner-v6-4-0l-1gr-fe-dohc-sr5-limited-trail-trd.html"},{"title":"DC Power 270A XP High-Output Alternator \u2014 5th Gen 4Runner","url":"https://www.dcpowerinc.com/products/11517-270-xp-toyota-4runner-2011-4"}],"alternatives":["4runner-engine-air-filter","tacoma-alternator"],"summary":"A 4Runner alternator that's failing usually shows a dim battery light or AC ripple on the bus before it quits; OEM replacement is a Denso 130A unit, and if you're running a winch or fridge, the DC Power 270A XP is the high-output upgrade that fits the same bracket.","description":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 in 4th gen (2003\u20132009) and 5th gen (2010\u20132024) 4Runners uses a 130-amp Denso alternator with an SD7 7-groove decoupler pulley. OEM part number is 27060-31190. The decoupler pulley is important \u2014 it lets the alternator overrun briefly during shift changes, which smooths belt behavior and reduces shock load on the bearings. Cheap aftermarket alternators sometimes ship with a solid pulley; that's a no for 1GR-FE. Get the decoupler version.\n\nSymptoms of a failing alternator: battery light flickering at idle, dim headlights that brighten with rev, a whining noise that scales with engine RPM, accessories cycling on and off, or a multimeter reading at the battery below 13.8V with the engine running (a healthy charge is 13.8\u201314.4V). Don't replace based on the dash light alone \u2014 a load-test at a parts store (free at AutoZone, O'Reilly, Advance) confirms it before you spend the money. The other failure that looks like an alternator is a corroded ground strap from engine to chassis; check both before condemning the alternator.\n\nAccess on the 1GR-FE is from above. Unlike some V6s, you don't have to drop the front skid or pull the radiator fan. The alternator sits high on the passenger side, behind the serpentine pulley path. Belt comes off with a long ratcheting wrench on the tensioner pulley. Hardware is two main bolts plus the back-of-alternator B+ terminal (10mm nut) and the field plug. Total tool list is short. The fight, when it happens, is wiggling the alternator out past the AC line and coolant hose \u2014 patience and a hand at the right angle gets it out.\n\nIf you're a stock daily driver, the 130A OEM (or reman Denso) is right-sized. If you've added a winch, a fridge, off-road lights, or a dual-battery system, look at high-output. The DC Power Inc. 270A XP is a popular direct-fit upgrade for the 5th gen \u2014 same bracket, same harness, more than double the output at idle. Note that you may need a heavier B+ cable to handle the added current; the stock 6-gauge is sized for 130A.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal.","Loosen the serpentine belt with a ratcheting wrench on the tensioner pulley. Slip the belt off the alternator pulley.","Remove the rubber boot from the alternator B+ terminal. Loosen the 10mm nut and pull the cable off.","Unplug the field connector (squeeze tab and pull).","Remove the upper and lower alternator bolts (typically 14mm).","Wiggle the alternator out of its bracket. AC line and coolant hose are in the way \u2014 work slowly.","Compare old to new for pulley type, mounting points, and connector orientation.","Install new alternator in reverse order. Torque mounting bolts to 30 ft-lbs (lower) and 18 ft-lbs (upper) per shop manual. Torque B+ nut to 10 ft-lbs.","Route belt per under-hood diagram. Tensioner snaps back into place.","Reconnect negative battery, start engine, verify 13.8\u201314.4V at the battery."],"pros":["OEM Denso reman is reliable and uses the right decoupler pulley","Bolt-in upgrade to 270A for winch and accessories","Tool list is short","Access on 1GR-FE is from above \u2014 no skid pan removal"],"cons":["Wrong pulley type from cheap aftermarket can shred belts","High-output may need heavier B+ cable","Dash light alone is not enough \u2014 load-test first","AC line and coolant hose make removal tight"]},{"id":"4runner-dual-battery","title":"Dual Battery and Power System","vehicle":"4runner","category":"electrical","subcategory":"dual_battery","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":9},"cost_usd":{"low":450,"high":1800},"tools_required":["wire crimpers (hydraulic lug crimper)","socket set","drill","multimeter","wire stripper","heat gun","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["12V wiring"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["electrical","dual-battery","aux-power","charging"],"parts":[{"name":"Auxiliary battery (Group 27/31 AGM or 100 Ah LiFePO4)","vendor":"Odyssey / Northstar / Battle Born / Dakota Lithium","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"DC-DC charger, 25-40 A","vendor":"Redarc BCDC1225D / Victron Orion-Tr Smart / National Luna","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"Aux battery tray or cargo-area mount","vendor":"sPOD / Slee / Victory 4x4 / DIY","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"2 AWG or 4 AWG welding cable, lugs, ANL fuses and holders, heat shrink","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":160}],"citations":[{"title":"Offroadium \u2014 4Runner electrical and dual-battery mods","url":"https://offroadium.com/toyota-4runner-mods.html"},{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 overland 5th gen builds and power systems","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2022/07/14/5th-gen-4runner-trd-off-road-builds/"},{"title":"ARB 4x4 Accessories \u2014 accessory power and 12V system reference","url":"https://arbusa.com/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-alternator-replacement","4runner-winch"],"summary":"The moment a 4Runner build adds an overland fridge, recovery lighting, or a winch, the single factory battery becomes the weak link. A night running a fridge or a long winch pull can leave you unable to start. A dual-battery system isolates the loads that must never drain the start battery from everything else. This is a safety-critical 12V job \u2014 undersized or unfused cable in an engine bay is a fire, not an inconvenience.","description":"The point is separation. The start battery stays dedicated to firing the engine; the auxiliary battery carries the fridge, the camp lights, the air compressor, and the inverter. A device between them ties the two together only when the alternator is charging, so you can flatten the aux battery overnight and still start at dawn.\n\nThere are two ways to do that, and they are not equivalent:\n\n- A voltage-sensitive relay (VSR) or solenoid isolator watches the start-battery voltage and closes a contactor above roughly 13.2 V. It costs $80-150 and it works. What it cannot do is control the charge profile \u2014 the aux battery only ever sees whatever the alternator is putting out, which is a compromise voltage tuned for a lead-acid start battery.\n- A DC-DC charger takes alternator voltage in and produces a proper multi-stage charge profile out, selectable for flooded, AGM, or lithium chemistry. It costs $220-450 and it is the correct answer if the aux battery is AGM or LiFePO4.\n\nOn any 4Runner from roughly 2016 onward, treat the DC-DC charger as mandatory rather than optional. Toyota's smart charging strategy varies alternator output to save fuel, and a plain relay hung off a variable-voltage system chronically undercharges the aux battery. A lithium aux battery makes the DC-DC charger non-negotiable regardless of year \u2014 LiFePO4 will happily pull more current than the alternator wants to give and can cook it.\n\nMatch the aux battery to the loads:\n\n- AGM (Group 27 or 31, roughly 70-100 Ah). Tolerates repeated discharge far better than a starting battery, seals against the vibration and angles a trail 4Runner sees, and costs $250-450. Usable capacity is about half of rated, because taking an AGM below 50 percent shortens its life sharply.\n- LiFePO4 (100 Ah). Roughly 60 percent lighter, usable to about 90 percent depth of discharge, and it holds voltage flat under load so the fridge does not brown out. It costs $500-900 and it needs a charger that speaks lithium and a BMS with low-temperature charge cutoff if you camp in freezing weather.\n- Do not use a second starting battery as an aux. It will be dead within a season of camp cycling.\n\nPlacement on a 4Runner is genuinely tight. The 5th gen engine bay has no factory second-battery location, so builds go one of two ways: a slim aux battery on a fabricated tray on the passenger side, or a relocation to the cargo area in a sealed, vented box. The cargo-area route is easier to fit and keeps the battery out of underhood heat, but it means a long high-current run and it puts 40-70 lb behind the rear axle. Confirm clearance from the intake tract and any exhaust heat before you commit to an underhood tray, and never place a flooded battery inside the cabin without a vented box plumbed outside.\n\n1. Size the cable for current and length, not for what fits the lug. A 25 A DC-DC charger on a 6 ft underhood run wants 6 AWG minimum; a cargo-area relocation is a 20-25 ft round trip and wants 2 AWG. Undersized cable drops voltage, the charger never reaches absorption, and the aux battery never fills.\n2. Use fine-strand welding cable rather than solid-core building wire. It survives vibration and it bends around the frame without work-hardening.\n3. Fuse both ends of every battery-to-battery run, within 7 in of each battery post. An ANL or MEGA fuse rated one step above the charger's maximum draw is correct \u2014 40 A for a 25 A charger, 60 A for a 40 A charger. The fuse protects the cable, not the device.\n4. Crimp lugs with a hydraulic crimper and seal with adhesive-lined heat shrink. A crimp made with a hammer or pliers is a resistance heater waiting for a long winch pull.\n5. Ground the aux battery to clean, bare, paint-free metal on the frame or body, with a star washer, and duplicate the ground cable size on the positive side. Undersized or corroded grounds cause the flicker, the brownouts, and the mystery voltage drops that plague rushed installs.\n6. Route through grommets, never through a bare drilled hole. Add a rubber grommet or a bulkhead fitting anywhere cable crosses sheet metal, and secure the run every 12-18 in.\n7. Label both ends of every cable before you close it up. You will be diagnosing this at night in three years.\n\nA built 4Runner running a winch, a fridge, a compressor, and a light bar can outrun the factory alternator, which is roughly 130-150 A depending on year and trim. Symptoms are dimming headlights under load and system voltage sagging below about 13.5 V at idle with everything on.\n\nTest before you spend. With the engine running and accessories off, a healthy system reads roughly 13.8-14.7 V at the start battery. Turn on every accessory and watch it: a drop below 13.2 V that does not recover means the alternator is at its limit, and a higher-output unit is worth considering \u2014 [[4runner-alternator-replacement|the alternator guide]] covers the swap and the output options.\n\nNote that a winch does not run off the alternator in any meaningful sense. A [[4runner-winch|winch]] pulls 300-450 A on a hard line and comes almost entirely out of the battery. That is why the winch stays on the start battery, or on a parallel-capable setup, rather than on the aux battery.\n\n- Disconnect the negative terminal on the start battery before you begin, and keep a wrench off the positive post while anything else is connected.\n- Do not tie the aux battery into the factory charging harness at the fuse box. Run dedicated cable from the start battery post.\n- Verify the DC-DC charger's ignition-sense wire is on a genuinely switched circuit. Wired to a constant source, it will slowly drain the start battery. [[4runner-accessory-switch-wiring|The accessory switch wiring guide]] covers finding a proper switched source.\n- Check the aux battery voltage after a 30 minute drive. AGM should be showing above 13 V and climbing; if it is not, the cable is undersized or the ignition sense is not seeing 12 V.\n- Retorque every lug after the first heat cycle.\n\nA VSR-based system with a Group 27 AGM, a tray, and proper cable runs roughly $450-700 in parts. A DC-DC system with a Group 31 AGM lands $700-1,100. A lithium build with a 40 A DC-DC charger, a 100 Ah LiFePO4, and a cargo-area relocation runs $1,300-1,800. Shop installation typically adds $400-800 depending on how much fabrication the mount needs.\n\nAdd a dual-battery system once the build runs a fridge, camp lights, or a winch. Use a DC-DC charger rather than a bare relay on anything 2016 or newer, or on any AGM or lithium aux battery. Size and fuse the cable properly at both ends, ground to bare metal, and verify alternator headroom with a multimeter before you assume you need a bigger alternator. Done right, it is what keeps the camp powered and the truck starting, every single time."},{"id":"4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues","title":"1GR-FE 4.0L V6 \u2014 Known Issues and Preventive Maintenance","vehicle":"4runner","category":"engine","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":600},"tools_required":["socket set","OBD2 scanner","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["diagnosis","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["engine","1gr-fe","reliability","secondary-air","maintenance","v6"],"parts":[{"name":"Secondary Air Injection (AI) Pump and Valve Assembly","vendor":"Toyota / Dorman","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Denso Iridium Spark Plugs (set of 6, 1GR-FE)","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"OEM Toyota PCV Valve and Valve Cover Gasket Set","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":70}],"citations":[{"title":"1GR-FE Secondary Air Injection P2440/P2441 Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Toyota 1GR-FE Maintenance Schedule","url":"https://www.toyota.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-spark-plugs","4runner-cooling-service"],"summary":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 is one of the most reliable engines Toyota builds, and the one weak spot on 5th gen 4Runners is the secondary air injection (AI) system \u2014 the P2440/P2441 codes that throw a check engine light around 80,000\u2013120,000 miles. Beyond that, this engine asks only for its scheduled fluids and plugs and routinely runs past 250,000 miles.","description":"The 1GR-FE is a timing-chain V6 (no belt to replace) used across the 4Runner, Tacoma, FJ Cruiser, and others. It is genuinely durable, and most \"problems\" people report are normal wear items handled on schedule. The exception worth knowing about before you buy or while you own one is the secondary air injection system. On 5th gen trucks it's a cold-start emissions device \u2014 an air pump and a pair of switching valves \u2014 and the valves stick or corrode, the pump fails, and you get a P2440 or P2441 and a check engine light. It does not affect drivability or reliability, but it will fail an emissions test (relevant in Phoenix's emissions counties) and the OEM repair is not cheap.\n\nThe rest of the engine's maintenance story is short. Spark plugs are iridium and last around 100,000\u2013120,000 miles. The PCV valve and valve cover gaskets are the usual minor oil-seepage items at higher mileage. Keep the oil changed on schedule and the cooling system on Toyota's SLLC, and the 1GR-FE is about as worry-free as a trail engine gets.\n\nAn OBD2 scanner to read and confirm codes, a basic socket set, and a torque wrench for plug and cover work. For the AI system specifically, the OEM pump-and-valve assembly or a quality aftermarket equivalent. For routine work, Denso iridium plugs and OEM PCV and gasket parts.\n\n1. Scan any check engine light and record the exact code before throwing parts at it\n2. For P2440/P2441, confirm whether it's a stuck switching valve (sometimes cleanable) or a failed pump \u2014 bench-test the pump for operation\n3. Replace the AI valves and/or pump assembly with the engine cold; torque the manifold and bracket fasteners to spec\n4. Clear the code and complete a cold-start drive cycle to confirm the monitor passes \u2014 emissions testing requires the readiness monitor set\n5. For routine maintenance, replace iridium plugs at interval and gap-check before install (they come pre-gapped \u2014 verify, don't bend the tip)\n6. Inspect the PCV valve and valve cover gaskets at higher mileage for oil seepage and replace as needed\n\nDon't ignore the AI code as \"emissions\" if you live in an emissions-test county \u2014 you won't pass inspection with the monitor incomplete. Some owners delete or bypass the AI system; that is not emissions-legal for a registered street vehicle and can itself trip codes, so weigh it against your local rules. On spark plugs, the rear bank requires removing the intake on the 1GR-FE, so it's a few hours of work, not minutes \u2014 budget the time. Watch oil consumption above 150,000 miles; a modest increase is normal, but a sudden jump with blue smoke points to valve seals or rings and deserves a compression and leak-down test before any trail trip.\n\nThe AI pump-and-valve repair runs $300\u2013$400 in parts (OEM) and $200+ in labor if you farm it out, so call it $300\u2013$600 turn-key. A full iridium plug set is about $60 in parts; the labor is the intake removal time. PCV and valve cover gaskets are around $70 in parts. None of this is recurring at short intervals \u2014 the 1GR-FE's running cost over a decade is low, which is exactly why it's the engine to look for in a used 4Runner."},{"id":"4runner-water-pump","title":"1GR-FE Water Pump Replacement (5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":400},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drain pan","serpentine belt tool","coolant"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","cooling system service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["engine","water-pump","cooling","1gr-fe","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Aisin Water Pump (1GR-FE, WPT-190)","vendor":"Aisin","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Toyota SLLC Pink Coolant (1 gal)","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Serpentine Belt (1GR-FE)","vendor":"Gates / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"1GR-FE Water Pump Service Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Aisin WPT-190 Application Data","url":"https://www.aisinworld.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-cooling-service","4runner-serpentine-belt"],"summary":"The 1GR-FE water pump is a belt-driven unit on the front of the engine, and it's an afternoon job you can do in your own driveway. Use an Aisin pump \u2014 it's the OEM supplier \u2014 and replace the serpentine belt and coolant while you're in there. Plan on 3\u20135 hours and around $90\u2013$150 in parts if you do the work yourself.","description":"The water pump on the 4.0L V6 isn't a common failure item, but when it goes it usually announces itself first: a weep from the bottom of the pump, a faint coolant smell, or a bearing that gets noisy. Most pumps last well past 150,000 miles. Because the pump sits behind the serpentine belt and shares the front of the engine with the belt tensioner and idler pulleys, it makes sense to treat all of it as one service rather than going back in twice.\n\nThis is solidly an intermediate job, not a beginner one \u2014 you're draining coolant, fighting belt tension, and torquing a pump housing to a gasket surface that has to seal. Nothing here is beyond a careful DIYer, but rushing the gasket or under-torquing the bolts leads to a leak that sends you right back in.\n\nA metric socket set, a torque wrench, and a belt tool or a long ratchet to release the automatic tensioner. An Aisin water pump (WPT-190 is the common 1GR-FE application), a fresh OEM gasket if it doesn't come with the pump, a serpentine belt, and a gallon or two of genuine Toyota SLLC (the pink coolant). A drain pan and a funnel round it out.\n\n1. Let the engine cool completely, then drain the coolant from the radiator petcock into a clean pan\n2. Release the serpentine belt tensioner and slip the belt off; note the routing or photograph it first\n3. Remove the accessories or brackets blocking pump access as needed, then unbolt the water pump from the block\n4. Clean the gasket surface back to bare metal \u2014 no old gasket material, no nicks\n5. Install the new pump with a fresh gasket, then torque the bolts in a cross pattern to spec (do not overtighten into aluminum)\n6. Reinstall the belt per the routing, refill with SLLC, and burp the system by running the engine to temperature with the cap loosened per procedure\n7. Check for leaks cold and hot, and top off the overflow after the first heat cycle\n\nThe most common mistake is reusing old coolant or mixing in green or universal coolant \u2014 the 1GR-FE wants Toyota SLLC, and mixing types can gel. The second is air in the system: if you don't burp it properly you'll get a hot spot and a temperature spike on the first drive. Torque the pump bolts evenly; aluminum threads strip if you crank one corner down. And use Aisin or Toyota \u2014 cheap eBay pumps fail early and you'll do the job twice.\n\nAn Aisin pump is around $75, the belt about $35, and a gallon of SLLC roughly $30, so you're at $90\u2013$140 in parts doing it yourself. A shop will charge $300\u2013$400 turn-key including the belt and coolant. Given the pump's long life, most owners only see this once in their ownership \u2014 doing it yourself with quality parts is the clear value play."},{"id":"4runner-fuel-pump","title":"Fuel Pump Replacement (5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":450},"tools_required":["socket set","fuel line disconnect tool","screwdriver","shop towels","fire extinguisher"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","fuel system safety"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["fuel","fuel-pump","no-start","in-tank","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Denso / Aisan In-Tank Fuel Pump Assembly","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Fuel Pump Strainer / Sock","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":20}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Fuel System Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Denso Fuel Pump Application Data","url":"https://www.denso-am.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-fuel-system-service"],"summary":"A 4Runner that cranks but won't start, sputters under load, or dies at low fuel level may have a failing in-tank fuel pump. It's an intermediate driveway job that doesn't require dropping the tank on most years \u2014 there's an access panel under the rear seat. Budget around $180 for a Denso pump and two to four hours, and treat the fuel-safety steps as non-negotiable.","description":"> **Safety critical.** You're working with gasoline. Relieve fuel pressure first, disconnect the battery, work outdoors or in a well-ventilated space, keep a fire extinguisher within reach, and no open flames or sparks anywhere near the work.\n\nThe 1GR-FE 4Runner uses an electric in-tank fuel pump module that combines the pump, the level sender, and a strainer sock. These are durable and rarely fail before high mileage, but when they do the symptoms are a long crank with no start, a whining or silent pump on key-on, stalling under hard acceleration, or a stumble that shows up when the tank gets low and the pump can't stay submerged. Confirm the diagnosis with a fuel pressure test before replacing \u2014 a clogged strainer, a bad relay, or a fuel filter can mimic a dead pump.\n\nThe good news is access: on most 5th gen years there's a service hatch beneath the rear seat cushion, so you replace the module without dropping the tank. That turns a miserable job into a manageable one.\n\nA metric socket set, a fuel line quick-disconnect tool, a flat screwdriver to rotate the lock ring, and plenty of shop towels. A Denso or Aisan OEM-grade pump module (avoid no-name pumps \u2014 fuel pumps are a place cheap parts fail early), a fresh strainer sock, and a fire extinguisher staged nearby. Eye protection is smart.\n\n1. Relieve fuel pressure: pull the fuel pump fuse or relay, start the engine, and let it stall, then crank briefly to clear residual pressure\n2. Disconnect the negative battery terminal\n3. Fold the rear seat and lift the access panel to expose the top of the pump module\n4. Disconnect the electrical connector and the fuel line using the proper disconnect tool \u2014 never pry\n5. Rotate and remove the lock ring, then lift the module out slowly, tilting to clear the float arm\n6. Transfer the level sender if required, install the new module with a fresh seal, and reseat the lock ring evenly\n7. Reconnect the line and connector, restore the fuse/relay and battery, cycle the key several times to prime, and check for leaks before starting\n\nDon't skip the pressure relief \u2014 disconnecting a line under pressure sprays fuel. Lift the module out gently so you don't bend the float arm and throw off your fuel gauge. Seat the lock ring fully and evenly or you'll get a fuel-smell leak at the top of the tank. Use an OEM-grade pump; a failed bargain pump leaves you stranded and back in the tank. After install, check thoroughly for leaks at the line and the seal before driving.\n\nA Denso/Aisan pump module runs about $180, a strainer sock about $20. A shop charges $350\u2013$450 with labor. Because the under-seat access removes the worst of the labor, this is a strong DIY job \u2014 confirm the diagnosis with a pressure test first so you're not replacing a good pump over a $15 filter or a relay."},{"id":"4runner-fuel-system-service","title":"Fuel System Service and Range \u2014 Filter, Pump, and Tank Options","vehicle":"4runner","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":900},"tools_required":["socket set","fuel line disconnect tools","fuel pressure gauge","drain container"],"skills_required":["fuel system safety","electrical basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["fuel","fuel-pump","fuel-filter","long-range","tank","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM Toyota Fuel Pump Assembly (in-tank, 1GR-FE)","vendor":"Toyota / Denso","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Long Range Automotive Replacement Fuel Tank (87L/23gal)","vendor":"Long Range Automotive","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"RotopaX 2-Gallon Fuel Pack with Mount","vendor":"RotopaX","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Fuel Pump Service Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Long Range Automotive 4Runner Tank Fitment","url":"https://www.longrangeautomotive.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues","4runner-cargo-storage"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner's in-tank fuel pump and lifetime filter rarely fail, so most \"fuel system service\" is really about extending range for overland trips. The factory 23-gallon tank gives a real-world 350\u2013400 miles; if you're running remote desert routes, an auxiliary tank or secured fuel cans matter more than any pump upgrade.","description":"The 4Runner's fuel system is conventional and durable. The fuel pump lives in the tank as a module, and the filter is integrated into that module \u2014 Toyota considers it a lifetime part, so there's no scheduled filter change. Pumps do eventually wear, usually past 150,000 miles, with symptoms like long cranking, high-rpm stumble, or a no-start after the truck sits. A fuel pressure test tells you whether the pump is the culprit before you drop the tank.\n\nFor an overland-focused 4Runner, the more common project is range. The factory tank's roughly 350\u2013400 mile usable range is fine for most weekends but tight for long, fuel-stop-sparse routes. A replacement long-range tank adds meaningful capacity by swapping the OEM tank for a larger one, which is the cleanest solution because it keeps the fuel low and protected. Secured fuel cans (RotopaX or a quality jerry can on a bed or rack mount) are the lower-cost alternative \u2014 never carry loose fuel inside the cabin or unsecured.\n\nFor a pump replacement: a fuel pressure gauge to confirm the diagnosis, fuel line disconnect tools, a socket set, and a container to catch fuel from the dropped tank. For a range build: a long-range replacement tank kit with its fittings and straps, or properly mounted external fuel containers with a secure rack or bracket.\n\n1. Relieve fuel system pressure (pull the fuel pump fuse and run the engine until it stalls) before opening any fuel connection\n2. To access the pump on the 5th gen, you typically drop or lower the tank \u2014 disconnect the filler neck, lines, and electrical, support the tank on a jack, and lower it\n3. Remove the pump module lock ring, lift out the assembly (mind the float arm), and transfer or replace as needed\n4. Install the new module with a fresh seal, reconnect lines and electrical, raise and secure the tank\n5. Cycle the key several times to prime, then check fuel pressure and inspect every connection for leaks before driving\n6. For a long-range tank, follow the kit's procedure \u2014 it mirrors the tank-drop steps with the larger unit and its specific straps and breather routing\n7. For external cans, mount to a rated bracket on a rack or bed, never inside the cabin, and check the mount after the first off-road run\n\nFuel work is a fire-and-vapor hazard \u2014 work outside or in a well-ventilated space, kill all ignition sources, keep an extinguisher within reach, and relieve pressure first. Don't bend the pump module's float arm or you'll get a false fuel gauge reading. When mounting external fuel, weight and security are the issues: a full RotopaX or jerry can is heavy, so mount it where it won't shift and won't put fuel near the exhaust or cabin. If you add a long-range tank, recalibrate your range expectations and confirm the gauge reads correctly across the new capacity before relying on it in remote country.\n\nAn OEM fuel pump assembly is about $250\u2013$300 in parts; the labor is the tank-drop time. A long-range replacement tank kit runs $800\u2013$900 and is the premium range solution. Secured fuel cans are the budget path at $60\u2013$90 each plus a mount \u2014 enough to add 4\u20136 gallons of carefully stored reserve for a desert crossing without touching the factory system."},{"id":"4runner-accessory-switch-wiring","title":"Accessory Switch Panel and Wiring (5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":350},"tools_required":["crimpers","wire strippers","multimeter","trim tools","drill","heat gun"],"skills_required":["12v wiring","fuse and relay basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["interior","electrical","switches","accessory-wiring","lighting"],"parts":[{"name":"Switch Pros / sPOD 6-Switch Power System","vendor":"Switch Pros","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"Factory-Style Switch Blanks (OEM-look toggles)","vendor":"Cali Raised / aftermarket","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Fused Distribution Block + Relays + Wire","vendor":"Blue Sea / generic","price_estimate_usd":80}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Accessory Wiring Reference","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"12V Relay and Fusing Basics","url":"https://www.bluesea.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-dual-battery","4runner-alternator"],"summary":"If you're adding light bars, a compressor, a fridge, or rock lights, wire them through a switched, fused power system rather than tapping random circuits behind the dash. A dedicated 6-switch system like a Switch Pros or sPOD costs around $300 and is the clean, safe way to do it; a DIY relay-and-fuse-block setup does the same job for less if you're comfortable with 12V wiring. Either way, every accessory gets its own fuse.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner has factory blank switch locations that take OEM-look toggles, which is why built 4Runners can run a lot of accessories without looking like a science fair. The real decision is how you distribute and protect the power. A pre-built switch system mounts a relay/fuse module in the engine bay, runs a single control cable to an in-cab keypad, and gives you labeled, individually fused outputs \u2014 it's tidy, reliable, and removes most of the guesswork. A DIY build using a fused distribution block, relays, and factory-style switches achieves the same result for roughly a third of the cost, at the price of your time and care.\n\nWhat you should never do is splice accessories into existing circuits or run unfused wire from the battery. That's how electrical fires and mystery gremlins start. This is intermediate work \u2014 not because any single step is hard, but because doing it cleanly and safely takes patience and a multimeter.\n\nCrimpers, wire strippers, a multimeter, interior trim removal tools, a drill for any panel openings, and a heat gun for adhesive-lined heat shrink. Either a Switch Pros/sPOD system, or a fused distribution block, relays, appropriately gauged wire, and factory-style switch blanks. Use marine-grade tinned wire and adhesive heat shrink for anything in the engine bay.\n\n1. Plan every circuit first: list each accessory, its current draw, and the wire gauge and fuse size it needs\n2. Mount the power module (or distribution block and relays) in a dry, protected spot in the engine bay\n3. Run the main feed from the battery through a master fuse within a few inches of the positive terminal\n4. Route control wiring into the cabin through an existing grommet \u2014 never drill the firewall without a proper grommet\n5. Install switches in the factory blank locations and connect each output to its accessory with the correct gauge and an inline fuse\n6. Ground accessories to clean, bare metal \u2014 bad grounds cause more 12V problems than bad power\n7. Test each circuit with the multimeter before final assembly, checking for correct voltage and no shorts\n\nFuse everything, and fuse the main feed right at the battery \u2014 an unfused main is the single most dangerous shortcut in accessory wiring. Match wire gauge to current draw; undersized wire on a winch or compressor circuit overheats. Protect every pass-through with a grommet and keep wiring away from heat and moving parts. Seal engine-bay connections against Phoenix dust and monsoon moisture with adhesive heat shrink. And label your circuits \u2014 future you will thank present you.\n\nA Switch Pros or sPOD system runs about $300 and is worth it for the time saved and the clean result. A DIY distribution block, relays, switches, and wire totals roughly $80\u2013$120 if you source carefully. Factory-look switch blanks are about $40 a set. The pre-built route is the value play for most people; the DIY route makes sense if you genuinely enjoy 12V work and want to save the difference."},{"id":"4runner-cargo-storage","title":"Cargo Drawers and Interior Storage for the 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":1400},"tools_required":["socket set","drill","trim removal tools","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","measuring"],"build_tiers":["overland","daily_driver","mild_trail"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["interior","storage","drawers","cargo","overland","molle"],"parts":[{"name":"Goose Gear Drawer Module (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Goose Gear","price_estimate_usd":1295},{"name":"DECKED Drawer System (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"DECKED","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"Rear Window MOLLE Panel and Seatback Organizer","vendor":"Blue Ridge Overland Gear","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Drawer System Fitment","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Goose Gear 4Runner Module Specs","url":"https://www.goosegear.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-fuel-system-service","4runner-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"A drawer system is the highest-value interior upgrade for an overland 4Runner because it turns the cargo area into organized, lockable storage with a flat sleeping or load platform on top. Build it to your actual use, though \u2014 a full Goose Gear module is overkill if you mostly day-trip, where a straightforward MOLLE panel and a few bins do the job for a fraction of the cost.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner's cargo area is generous but wastes vertical space when you toss gear in loose. A drawer system solves three problems at once: it keeps recovery gear and tools sorted and reachable, it locks valuables out of sight, and it gives you a flat deck that doubles as a sleeping platform or a stable base for a fridge and storage bins. The two ends of the market are DECKED (a sealed, weatherproof, rugged poly system at a moderate price) and Goose Gear (a lighter, more configurable plywood modular system that costs more but lets you tailor the layout and add a sleep platform). Both are bolt-in and reversible.\n\nYou don't need a drawer system to enjoy the truck. If your trips are short and your gear is light, a MOLLE panel on the rear quarter window, a seatback organizer, and a couple of cargo bins or a slide-out tray cover most needs for under $200. Match the build to how you actually use the truck \u2014 a 100-pound drawer module you fill with air is dead weight and lost fuel economy.\n\nFor a drawer install: the drawer kit, a socket set, a drill for any required mounting points, trim removal tools to pull the cargo panels, and a tape measure to confirm fitment against your specific trim (a sliding rear cargo deck or third-row delete changes the dimensions). For a light build: a MOLLE panel, fasteners, and the bins or organizers you choose.\n\n1. Confirm your cargo area configuration \u2014 third-row models and sliding-deck trims have different floor dimensions, so verify the kit matches before ordering\n2. Remove the cargo floor panels and any factory storage to expose the mounting points\n3. Dry-fit the drawer module to confirm it clears the wheel wells and seatback fold\n4. Bolt the module to the factory tie-down points or the kit's specified anchors \u2014 avoid drilling into anything structural or near fuel/brake lines\n5. Reinstall side panels and trim around the module\n6. For a lighter build, mount the MOLLE panel to the rear quarter trim and load your organizers and bins\n7. Load-test the platform and confirm the seats still fold and latch before a trip\n\nWeight is the real cost of a drawer system \u2014 a loaded module plus a fridge and water can add 150+ pounds high and rearward, which affects handling, payload, and fuel economy, so build deliberately and keep heavy items low. Confirm fitment against your exact trim before buying; the sliding rear deck and third-row variants are not interchangeable. Don't drill blindly into the floor \u2014 fuel and brake lines run underneath, so use the factory anchor points or the kit's hardware. Keep your recovery gear and fire extinguisher reachable, not buried at the bottom of a drawer you have to unload to open.\n\nA DECKED system runs about $1,100 and a Goose Gear module about $1,300 plus accessories \u2014 premium but durable and resale-friendly. The budget path is real and effective: a MOLLE panel and seatback organizer set is around $140, and a slide-out cargo tray adds maybe $200, giving you 80% of the organization for well under a fifth of the cost. Decide based on whether you sleep in the truck and carry a fridge, or need your gear to stop sliding around."},{"id":"4runner-atf-flush","title":"ATF Flush \u2014 A750F 5-Speed Automatic","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":180},"tools_required":["24mm socket (refill plug)","14mm socket (drain plug)","5mm hex bit (check plug)","10mm socket (pan bolts)","torque wrench (in-lb and ft-lb)","fluid transfer pump","infrared thermometer or OBD-II scanner","drain pan"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","ATF temperature check"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","transmission","drivetrain","atf"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Genuine ATF WS (1 qt)","part_number":"00289-ATFWS","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Aftermarket Toyota WS ATF (Idemitsu, Aisin)","vendor":"Idemitsu / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Transmission pan gasket (if dropping pan)","vendor":"Toyota / OEM","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Transmission filter (if servicing internally)","vendor":"Toyota / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 Drain and Fill 5th Gen","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2023/05/07/transmission-fluid-change-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"BobIsTheOilGuy \u2014 Toyota A750E/F DIY Flush Procedure","url":"https://bobistheoilguy.com/forums/threads/toyota-a750e-f-do-it-yourself-transmission-flush.283617/"},{"title":"TheTrackAhead \u2014 Transmission Fluid Flush 4th Gen 4Runner","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/transmission-fluid-flush-and-fill-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner","4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner"],"summary":"A drain-and-fill on the A750F pulls about 4 quarts of Toyota WS ATF; a full system flush takes 11\u201312 quarts because the torque converter holds half the fluid you can't drain in one pass.","description":"The 4th-gen (2003\u20132009) and 5th-gen (2010\u20132024) 4Runner V6 share the A750F 5-speed automatic, an Aisin-built sealed transmission with no dipstick. Toyota markets it as \"lifetime\" fluid, which is marketing \u2014 the fluid darkens and shears down between 60k and 90k miles, and 4Runners that see trailer towing, sustained Phoenix heat, or low-range crawling on washboard show ATF degradation well before that. A 60k-mile drain-and-fill is realistic preventive maintenance. A full flush (3 drain-and-fills with drives in between) at 90k\u2013100k miles is appropriate if the fluid has never been changed.\n\nFluid choice is non-negotiable: Toyota Genuine ATF WS (P/N 00289-ATFWS) or a verified equivalent (Idemitsu TLS-LV, Aisin AFW+). Toyota's own service manual warns that wrong-spec fluid will harm shift quality and damage the valve body. The cheaper \"universal\" Dexron/Mercon blends are not interchangeable here \u2014 they will shift harshly and shorten the transmission's life. Budget 12 quarts of WS to do a full flush.\n\nA drain-and-fill removes about 4 quarts, leaving roughly 7\u20138 quarts of old fluid in the torque converter, valve body, and cooler lines. To dilute old fluid effectively, do three drain-and-fills with 100-mile drives in between. After the third, you've cycled the new fluid through every chamber and the system reads about 90% fresh. Skipping the multi-pass cycle leaves you with 60\u201370% fresh fluid \u2014 better than nothing, but not a true service.\n\nThe level-check procedure is the trickiest part. There is no dipstick. Fluid level is verified by draining the overflow tube while the transmission is at exactly 104\u2013113\u00b0F (40\u201345\u00b0C). Too cold and you'll overfill; too hot and you'll underfill. Use an OBD-II scanner that reads transmission temperature (a basic Bluetooth ELM327 + Torque app works) or an infrared thermometer aimed at the pan. Loosen the 5mm hex check plug at temperature and let excess drip until flow reduces to a slow dribble, then torque to 15 ft-lbs.","steps":["Drive the vehicle 15 minutes to warm the transmission to operating temperature, then park level.","Remove the 24mm refill plug FIRST on top of the transmission (under a heat shield on some trims). If you can't get it loose, stop \u2014 you can't refill what you drain.","Position a drain pan under the transmission. Remove the 14mm drain plug and let fluid drain fully (10 minutes).","Measure the drained fluid \u2014 you'll typically capture 3.7\u20134.0 quarts. Inspect for burnt smell, metal flakes, or discoloration.","Reinstall and torque the drain plug to 21 ft-lbs.","Pump in the same quantity of fresh Toyota WS through the refill plug using a transfer pump.","Reinstall and torque the refill plug to 29 ft-lbs.","Start the engine. With your foot on the brake, cycle the shifter through P-R-N-D-3-2-L and back to P, pausing 3 seconds in each gear.","Drive 100 miles of mixed driving to circulate the new fluid through the torque converter and valve body.","Repeat steps 1\u20139 twice more for a true flush (three drain-and-fills total).","After the final fill: with engine idling and transmission at 104\u2013113\u00b0F, loosen the 5mm hex check plug.","Let excess fluid dribble out. When flow slows to drops, torque the check plug to 15 ft-lbs and shut off the engine."],"pros":["Catches metal debris and fluid breakdown before they damage the valve body","$100 in fluid vs. $4,000+ for a rebuilt transmission","A750F responds well to fresh WS \u2014 shift quality noticeably improves","Multi-pass drain-and-fill is safer than a machine flush on high-mileage units"],"cons":["No dipstick \u2014 level check requires temperature accuracy","Toyota WS is roughly $10/qt and you'll need 12 quarts for a full flush","Three drives between fills means this is a weekend job, not an hour","Refill plug can be seized at high mileage \u2014 break loose before draining"]},{"id":"4runner-brake-fluid-flush-4runner","title":"Brake Fluid Flush","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":70},"tools_required":["10mm box wrench","clear vinyl tubing","catch bottle","turkey baster or fluid pump","rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","two-person bleeding or vacuum pump"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["brake-service","maintenance-interval","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Genuine Toyota DOT 3 brake fluid (1 qt)","part_number":"00475-1BF03","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Valvoline DOT 3/4 brake fluid (32 oz)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Motul DOT 5.1 brake fluid (1L)","vendor":"Motul","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Mityvac vacuum bleeder kit","part_number":"MV8000","vendor":"Mityvac","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota 4Runner Brake Fluid Flush \u2014 Top4Runners","url":"https://www.top4runners.com/ja/2007/brakeflush.html"},{"title":"Bleeding Brakes on a 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2021/10/21/bleeding-brakes-fluid-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Toyota 4Runner Brake Fluid Capacity Discussion","url":"https://www.toyotanation.com/threads/whats-the-brake-fluid-capacity.1185666/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-pads-rotors","4runner-brake-upgrade-trail-tow"],"summary":"Flush your 4Runner's brake fluid every 30,000 miles or 3 years; you need about 1 quart of DOT 3, a 10mm wrench, and clear tubing to do it right.","description":"Brake fluid is hygroscopic \u2014 it absorbs moisture from the air. After three years, even sealed brake systems pick up enough water to drop the boiling point by 30\u00b0C or more. On a stock 4Runner that's a slow-degradation issue. On a heavy 4Runner running 33s or doing long downhill grades in 4Lo, that water turns to vapor under sustained braking and your pedal goes to the floor. This is the failure mode behind most \"my brakes disappeared\" complaints on Tacoma World, IH8MUD, and the 4Runner forums.\n\nToyota specifies DOT 3 from the factory and the fill cap says so. DOT 4 is compatible and has a higher dry boiling point, which makes it the right choice for a rig that does long descents or runs big tires. DOT 5.1 is also glycol-based and compatible with DOT 3/4. The only fluid you must never use is DOT 5 \u2014 that's silicone-based, will not mix with DOT 3/4, and will destroy the ABS unit. The bottles look similar; read the label.\n\nCapacity for a full flush is 1 quart on a 4th gen (2003\u20132009) and 1\u20131.5 quarts on a 5th gen (2010\u20132024) because the longer brake lines hold more fluid. Buy two quarts. You'll use about 1.2 quarts for the flush and keep the second for top-offs over the next year. Always use a fresh, sealed container \u2014 opened brake fluid begins absorbing water immediately and is suspect after 12 months on a shelf.\n\nOrder of bleeding is farthest from master cylinder to nearest: right rear, left rear, right front, left front. The 5th gen's ABS/VSC system holds fluid in the modulator and won't fully flush from a bench bleed \u2014 for a complete flush, Toyota Techstream or a service tool that cycles the ABS pump is recommended, but for a routine fluid swap the standard four-corner bleed is sufficient. Watch the reservoir level constantly \u2014 if it runs dry you've sucked air into the master cylinder and the job doubled in length.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine off, parking brake set.","Pop the hood, locate the brake fluid reservoir on the driver-side firewall.","Use a turkey baster or fluid pump to remove old fluid from the reservoir, leaving 1/4\" in the bottom.","Refill the reservoir to MAX with fresh DOT 3 or DOT 4.","Start at the right rear caliper. Place a box wrench on the bleeder valve (10mm).","Attach clear vinyl tubing to the bleeder, route the free end into a catch bottle.","Helper pumps brake pedal 3 times, holds on the third pump.","Crack the bleeder 1/4 turn \u2014 fluid and any air come out. Helper holds pedal to floor.","Close the bleeder before helper releases the pedal.","Repeat until clear new fluid runs out \u2014 about 4\u20136 cycles per corner.","Check reservoir level after every two bleeds; never let it drop below MIN.","Move to left rear, then right front, then left front. Same procedure.","Top up reservoir to MAX with fresh fluid.","Pump pedal to verify firm feel before moving the vehicle.","Test brakes at low speed before normal driving."],"pros":["Restores brake pedal feel","Prevents brake fade on long descents","Cheap insurance against a vapor-lock incident","1\u20132 hour job at home with $25 in fluid"],"cons":["DOT 5 contamination requires a full system rebuild","Letting the reservoir run dry adds an hour of master cylinder bleeding","ABS unit on 5th gen won't fully flush without a scan tool that cycles the pump","Brake fluid eats paint \u2014 wipe spills immediately"]},{"id":"4runner-cabin-filter-4runner","title":"Cabin Air Filter Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"cabin_filter","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.15,"high":0.3},"cost_usd":{"low":12,"high":35},"tools_required":["your hands"],"skills_required":["follow tabs and clips"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["cabin-filter","hvac","maintenance","5-minute-job"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM cabin air filter (5th gen)","part_number":"87139-YZZ20","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Premium Guard cabin filter","part_number":"PC5667","vendor":"Premium Guard","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"EPAuto carbon-activated cabin filter","part_number":"CP285 (CF10285)","vendor":"EPAuto","price_estimate_usd":16}],"citations":[{"title":"PartsGeek \u2014 2010-2024 4Runner cabin air filter PC5667 fitment","url":"https://www.partsgeek.com/j5tkrv8-toyota-4runner-cabin-air-filter.html"},{"title":"Toyota Parts Deal \u2014 4Runner OEM cabin air filter","url":"https://www.toyotapartsdeal.com/oem-toyota-4runner-cabin_air_filter.html"},{"title":"4Runners.com \u2014 Cabin filter part number clarification","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/cabin-air-filter-part-number-clarification-please.26561/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-engine-air-filter-4runner"],"summary":"The 4Runner cabin air filter sits behind the glove box. Replacement is a five-minute job with no tools \u2014 drop the glove box, slide out the filter tray, swap the element, slide it back. Toyota recommends every 15,000\u201330,000 miles. Dusty Phoenix or dirt-road use cuts that to 10,000.","description":"Toyota put the cabin filter in one of the friendliest locations in the industry. There are no covers to pry off, no fasteners to remove, no kneeling on the floor \u2014 the glove box itself swinging down on its two stops.\n\n**Why it matters.** The cabin filter catches everything before it hits the HVAC evaporator. A clogged filter reduces airflow, lets the evaporator collect dust and moisture (which grows mildew \u2014 that musty smell on first start), and makes the blower work harder. On 5th-gen 4Runners (2010\u20132024) the factory filter is a basic paper element; aftermarket carbon-activated versions filter odors and fumes from the truck ahead of you, which matters more than people think in dirt-road convoys.\n\n**5th gen vs. 4th gen.** Both generations use a glove-box-access filter. 5th-gen (2010\u20132024) takes Toyota part 87139-YZZ20 (or aftermarket PC5667, CP285). 4th-gen (2003\u20132009) takes a different filter \u2014 87139-YZZ08 \u2014 and the housing is slightly smaller. The aftermarket cross-references usually call this out clearly. Check the box before you install.\n\n**Direction matters.** The filter has an arrow on the frame indicating airflow direction. Install with the arrow pointing down (toward the floor on most 4Runners \u2014 confirm against the printing on the housing). Backward orientation reduces filtration and can cause the pleats to collapse over time.\n\n**Change interval.** Toyota's maintenance schedule says 30,000 miles. In real-world use that's optimistic. If you live somewhere dusty (Phoenix, the Mountain West, anywhere with monsoon dust storms), or wheel in dirt regularly, plan on 10,000\u201315,000 miles. Pull it out and look \u2014 if it's gray and packed with debris, replace it. They cost $15.","steps":["Open the glove box.","Empty the contents.","Squeeze the sides of the glove box inward to release the two side stops. The box swings all the way down to the floor.","The cabin filter housing is the rectangular plastic cover directly behind where the glove box was \u2014 about 6\" wide, 2\" tall.","Push in the two tabs on the cover and pull the cover toward you. The filter tray slides out with the cover.","Note the airflow arrow direction on the old filter. Discard the old filter.","Slide the new filter into the tray with the airflow arrow matching the original orientation (arrow down).","Slide the tray back into the housing until the tabs click.","Lift the glove box back into position, compressing the side stops so it seats fully."],"pros":["Five-minute job","No tools required","$15 part","Restores HVAC airflow and prevents evap mildew"],"cons":["4th-gen and 5th-gen filters are different \u2014 verify part number","Carbon filters cost more but catch odors stock paper does not","Arrow direction matters \u2014 backwards installation reduces filtration","Some aftermarket filters fit loosely \u2014 Toyota OEM and Premium Guard PC5667 are the tightest fits"]},{"id":"4runner-engine-air-filter-4runner","title":"Engine Air Filter Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.1,"high":0.25},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":60},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver (optional)","shop vacuum or rag"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","filter","intake","1gr-fe"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM Engine Air Filter (5th gen 2010-2024, 4.0L)","part_number":"17801-38051","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Toyota OEM Engine Air Filter (4th gen 2003-2009, 4.0L)","part_number":"17801-0P010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Denso 143-3058 (OE-supplier equivalent, 5th gen)","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":20},{"name":"Wix 49143 / FRAM CA10171 (aftermarket paper)","vendor":"Wix / FRAM","price_estimate_usd":16}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota OEM 17801-38051 Air Filter Element (2010-2024 4Runner / FJ Cruiser)","url":"https://parts.nucartoyotanorthattleboro.com/oem-parts/toyota-oem-new-replacement-engine-air-filter-cleaner-element-2010-2019-4runner-2010-2014-fj-cruiser-1780138051"},{"title":"Genuine Toyota 17801-0P010 Air Filter (4.0L V6 4Runner / FJ Cruiser / Tacoma)","url":"https://parts.frontiertoyota.com/oem-parts/toyota-genuine-toyota-engine-air-filter-element-4-0l-v6-4runner-fj-cruiser-tacoma-tundra-178010p010"}],"alternatives":["4runner-cabin-filter-4runner","4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues"],"summary":"The 4Runner engine air filter is a five-minute swap with no tools required on any year \u2014 flip the clips, lift the airbox lid, drop the new element in. The part you want is 17801-38051 on a 2010-2024 truck and 17801-0P010 on a 2003-2009. Buy paper, not oiled cotton, and change it at 30,000 miles on pavement or 15,000 if you spend real time on dirt.","description":"A clogged air filter rarely throws a code. It quietly costs you throttle response and a percent or two of fuel economy, and on a dusty truck it eventually starts passing what it can no longer hold. That last part is the one that matters \u2014 a filter loaded past its capacity does not block dirt, it leaks it, and the 1GR-FE's mass airflow sensor sits directly downstream. Filter maintenance on this platform is less about horsepower and more about keeping abrasive silica out of an engine that will otherwise run 300,000 miles without complaint.\n\nThe two 4Runner generations use different elements and they are not interchangeable, despite both being 4.0L V6 trucks.\n\n**2010-2024 (5th gen, 1GR-FE):** Toyota 17801-38051, which supersedes the earlier 17801-38050. This is a flat rectangular panel shared with the 2010-2014 FJ Cruiser. Denso 143-3058 is the same element from the OE supplier at a lower price. Wix 49143 and FRAM CA10171 are the common aftermarket paper equivalents.\n\n**2003-2009 (4th gen, 1GR-FE / 2UZ-FE V8):** Toyota 17801-0P010, which supersedes 17801-31090. Shared with the 2005-2013 Tacoma and 2007-2010 FJ Cruiser.\n\nIf you are ordering online and the listing shows both numbers under one part, check the picture. The 4th gen element is noticeably taller in the frame. Getting the wrong one is not dangerous, it will not seat, and you will find out with the hood up.\n\nPaper is the right answer for the overwhelming majority of 4Runners, and it is worth being blunt about why.\n\n**Paper (OEM-style disposable):** Filters to roughly 98-99% efficiency at the particle sizes that matter, costs $16-25, and gets thrown away. No maintenance, no failure mode beyond neglect. This is what belongs in a truck that sees fire roads and desert washes.\n\n**Oiled cotton gauze (K&N and similar):** Flows more air, filters less. On a naturally aspirated 4.0L with a stock intake tract, the horsepower difference is inside the noise floor \u2014 you will not feel it. What you can feel is the downside: over-oiling after a cleaning leaves an oil film that migrates onto the MAF sensor's hot-wire element, and a contaminated MAF throws lean codes (P0171/P0174) and drives poor fuel trims. If you already run one, clean it sparingly, let it dry fully, and use half the oil you think you need. See [[4runner-common-trouble-codes]] for what a fouled MAF looks like on a scanner.\n\n**Dry synthetic (AEM DryFlow and similar):** Splits the difference \u2014 reusable, no oil, no MAF risk. Costs $60-70 up front and washes out with water. Defensible for someone who genuinely changes filters every 10,000 miles in dust, less so for anyone else.\n\nThe honest verdict: on a dusty truck, filtration efficiency is worth more than airflow, and a $20 paper element replaced on schedule beats a $70 reusable one that gets cleaned when someone remembers.\n\nToyota's maintenance schedule calls for inspection at 15,000-mile intervals and replacement at 30,000 miles under normal conditions. That number assumes pavement. Realistic intervals:\n\n- **Pavement commuter:** 30,000 miles\n- **Regular Forest Service roads, gravel, or a dusty commute:** 15,000-20,000 miles\n- **Desert washes, staging areas, convoy dust, or a Baja-style trip:** inspect after the trip; a single hard weekend can load a filter more than a year of highway\n\nThe common advice to hold the filter up to a shop light and replace it when you cannot see through it is unreliable. A paper element traps fine particulate deep in the media where light still passes, and by the time it visibly darkens end to end it is well past useful. Mileage and conditions are better guides than appearance. What appearance *is* good for: if you pull it and find leaves, seed pods, or rodent bedding, something upstream needs attention regardless of mileage.\n\n**A partially seated lid is the whole failure mode.** One unlatched clip lets unfiltered air bypass the element entirely, and it makes no noise to tell you. Check every clip by hand after closing.\n\n**Rodent nests are common in trucks that sit.** Acorns, insulation, and bedding in the lower airbox get pulled straight into a fresh filter within a few miles. Clear it out before installing, and if you find chewed wiring nearby, deal with that first.\n\n**Snorkel owners have a different job.** A snorkel changes the intake path but not the filter, and the head unit's pre-cleaner or cyclonic separator needs its own periodic dump. A snorkel does not extend filter life on dust \u2014 in some staging-area conditions it shortens it by feeding the box cleaner but faster-moving air.\n\n**Do not oil a paper element.** People occasionally spray a disposable filter with K&N oil thinking it improves filtration. It restricts flow, sheds oil into the intake, and contaminates the MAF.\n\n**If a lean code appears right after a filter change,** the usual causes are a dislodged intake boot clamp, a lid gasket pinched by the new element, or MAF contamination from a previously oiled filter. Check physical seal before ordering sensors.\n\n| Item | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Toyota 17801-38051 (5th gen OEM) | $22-28 |\n| Toyota 17801-0P010 (4th gen OEM) | $20-26 |\n| Denso 143-3058 | $18-22 |\n| Wix 49143 / FRAM CA10171 | $14-18 |\n| Dry synthetic reusable (AEM/S&B) | $60-75 |\n| Shop labor to do it for you | $20-40 on top of the part |\n\nOEM elements are available from any Toyota parts counter and the online dealer sites; Denso and Wix are stocked at RockAuto and most local stores. Buying two at a time and keeping the spare in the cargo area is worth doing if you travel dirt \u2014 it turns a trip-ending dust event into a ten-minute stop.","steps":["Open the hood and locate the black plastic airbox on the passenger side, against the firewall.","Release the metal spring clips on the airbox lid. They flip up with finger pressure, or with a small flathead if corroded.","Lift the lid \u2014 the intake hose acts as a hinge, so nothing needs disconnecting. Do not force it past its natural travel or you will strain the hose clamp at the throttle body end.","Pull the old filter straight up and out. Note the orientation: pleats face down into the body of the box.","Vacuum or wipe out debris in the lower housing. Do not blow it out with compressed air while the intake tube is connected \u2014 you will push the debris downstream.","Drop the new filter in, pleats down. The rubber sealing lip should sit flat all the way around with no gap or pinch.","Lower the lid and reseat every clip. Confirm each one clicks fully home.","Close the hood. Drive it \u2014 no reset, no relearn, no drive cycle required."],"pros":["No tools, no jack, no fluids, and no way to get it dangerously wrong","The cheapest maintenance item on the truck with a measurable effect on how it drives","Doubles as a five-minute inspection of the airbox for rodent damage"],"cons":["Skipped more often than any other service because nothing warns you","Oiled-cotton filters can contaminate the MAF sensor and cause lean codes that cost far more than the filter saved","The two generations use different elements and online listings frequently mix them up"]},{"id":"4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner","title":"Front Differential Fluid Change","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":50},"tools_required":["24mm socket or hex bit","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024","2025-2026"],"tags":["axles","gear-oil","service-interval","1gr-fe"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM 75W-85 GL-5 differential gear oil (1 quart)","part_number":"08885-02506","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Toyota OEM 75W-85 GL-5 (alternate part #)","part_number":"08885-02906","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Toyota OEM fill/drain crush gasket","part_number":"12157-10010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2},{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 75W-90 GL-5 (1 quart, alternate)","part_number":"VV975","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":13}],"citations":[{"title":"BlauParts \u2014 Toyota 4Runner Differential Fluid Gear Oil Type Guide","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-differential-fluid-does-my-toyota-4runner-take.html"},{"title":"Trail4R \u2014 4Runner Driveline Fluids: How and When to Change Them","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/12/24/4runner-driveline-fluids-how-and-when-to-change-them/"},{"title":"The Track Ahead \u2014 Front and Rear Differential Fluid Replacement: 4th Gen Toyota 4Runner","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/front-and-rear-differential-fluid-replacement-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-rear-diff-fluid","4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner"],"summary":"The 4Runner front differential takes about 1.3 quarts of 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil and Toyota's recommended interval is 30,000 miles for severe service \u2014 the 1GR-FE platform has run reliably for two decades because owners actually do this.","description":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 4Runner uses a conventional open or limited-slip front differential. It's not part-time disconnect like a Wrangler \u2014 even in 2WD the front ring and pinion are turning, not driving. That means the fluid sees thermal cycling even when you're not in 4HI, and on a 4Runner that lives in Arizona heat or pulls a trailer, the front diff fluid breaks down faster than most owners assume.\n\nToyota's current spec is 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil. The OEM part number for the bottle on the parts counter is 08885-02506 (also packaged as 08885-02906 in some regions). Both are the same fluid. The older 4th-gen owner's manual originally listed 75W-90 for some build years, but Toyota's current TSB and the 2010+ spec moved everything to 75W-85 \u2014 using 75W-85 is correct across 2003-2024 4Runners. A name-brand synthetic 75W-90 GL-5 like Valvoline SynPower works fine if the parts house is out of OEM.\n\nCapacity is 1.2 to 1.3 liters (1.3 to 1.4 quarts). The right approach is the same as every other diff: pump until it runs out the fill hole, then stop. Don't try to hit a specific number \u2014 vehicle level matters more than the spec sheet.\n\nBoth plugs are 24mm hex on most years (some 4th-gen use a 10mm internal hex). They're located on the front cover of the diff. The fill plug is the higher one; the drain plug is below it. Always crack the fill plug FIRST. A stuck fill plug after you've drained means a tow.\n\nService interval is the part most owners get wrong. Toyota's normal-service interval reads as \"inspect\" rather than \"replace,\" which gets read as \"do nothing.\" The severe service schedule \u2014 which applies to anyone who tows, wheels in dust, or drives in stop-and-go \u2014 calls for 30,000 miles. If you wheel in water at all, change it after every deep crossing; water in a differential is fast destruction.","steps":["Park on level ground. Drive a few miles to warm the fluid for cleaner drainage.","Slide drain pan under the front diff.","Crack the FILL plug first (24mm hex, upper plug on front cover). If it won't break loose, stop.","Remove the drain plug (lower plug). Let it drain 15\u201320 minutes.","Inspect drained fluid. Dark brown is normal aging. Milky means water \u2014 investigate seals and the breather tube. Metal flakes mean ring-and-pinion wear.","Install new crush gasket on drain plug. Torque to 36 ft-lb.","Use a fluid pump to add 75W-85 GL-5 through the fill hole. Stop when it dribbles out steady.","Install fill plug with new crush gasket. Torque to 36 ft-lb.","Wipe down, check for leaks after a short drive."],"pros":["Cheap maintenance that prevents expensive ring-and-pinion damage","Fluid condition gives early warning of pinion bearing wear or water intrusion","OEM fluid is available at any Toyota dealer for under $20/quart","Same 75W-85 spec covers front and rear \u2014 buy 3 quarts, do both diffs"],"cons":["24mm hex requires a specific bit \u2014 not in every starter socket set","No drain plug magnet from the factory on most years \u2014 aftermarket upgrade recommended","Severe service interval is genuinely needed for off-road and tow rigs, not marketing fine print"]},{"id":"4runner-oil-change-4runner","title":"Oil Change \u2014 1GR-FE 4.0L V6","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":80},"tools_required":["14mm socket","64mm oil filter cap socket","drain pan","torque wrench","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["oil-change","1gr-fe","4.0l","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"0W-20 full synthetic (6.4 qt 2010+ / 5.5 qt 4th gen)","part_number":"08880-83865","vendor":"Toyota / API SP equivalent","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Toyota cartridge oil filter kit","part_number":"04152-YZZA1","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Drain plug gasket (aluminum washer)","part_number":"90430-12031","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota 1GR-FE 4.0L service info \u2014 oil capacity and cartridge filter spec","url":"https://techinfo.toyota.com"},{"title":"4Runner forum drain plug torque (27 ft-lb) and filter housing torque (18 ft-lb)","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-spark-plugs-4runner","4runner-serpentine-belt"],"summary":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 holds 6.4 quarts of 0W-20 (5th gen 2010+) or 5.5 quarts of 5W-30 (4th gen 2003\u20132009). Cartridge filter is on the bottom of the engine with a built-in drain valve. Drain plug torque 27 ft-lb, filter housing 18 ft-lb. Job takes 30\u201360 minutes.","description":"The 1GR-FE V6 powers every 4Runner from 4th gen (2003) through 5th gen (2010\u20132024). The 4th gen used 5W-30 conventional or blend; the 5th gen switched to 0W-20 full synthetic for fuel economy. Either works mechanically \u2014 match the manual to keep warranty and CARB OBD-II monitors happy.\n\n**Cartridge filter design.** Toyota's cartridge filter sits on the bottom of the engine, behind the front skid plate. It has a clever drain valve \u2014 thread a small plastic drain tool (included in the Toyota filter kit) into the bottom of the housing first, and oil drains out through a hose you can route into your pan. This keeps oil off the skid plate. Without the drain tool, oil sprays everywhere when you crack the housing.\n\n**Cap socket.** 64mm 14-flute oil filter cap socket fits both gens. Cheap aluminum versions strip teeth out of the cap \u2014 buy the steel version (~$15 at any parts store) once and keep it for life.\n\n**Skid plate access.** Stock front skid plate has an oval cutout that gives you access to the filter and drain plug without dropping the skid. Aftermarket skids (Rago Fabrication, RCI, BAMF) keep the access port; bare-bones plate-style skids don't, and you'll have to drop them. Plan accordingly.\n\n**Oil choice.** 0W-20 full synthetic is correct for 5th gen. Mobil 1 EP, Pennzoil Platinum, Valvoline EP, and Toyota TGMO branded oils are all SN+/SP-rated and work fine. For high-mileage 4Runners (200K+) some owners step up to 5W-30 to quiet a slightly noisy timing chain \u2014 Toyota's TSB allows this in hot climates without warranty implications.","steps":["Run engine 2\u20133 minutes to warm and thin the oil. Shut off.","Lift the front of the truck or work over ramps. The skid plate access port should align with the filter housing.","Position drain pan under the drain plug (rear of oil pan). Remove 14mm drain plug. Drain 5+ minutes.","While draining, thread the small plastic drain tool (from the Toyota filter kit) into the bottom of the filter housing. Oil will drain through it into your pan.","Once filter housing is drained, use the 64mm cap socket to remove the housing cover. Lift out the old cartridge.","Wipe the housing clean. Replace both O-rings on the cover (one large, one small for the drain valve plug).","Snap the new cartridge into the cover. Reinstall, torque to 18 ft-lb (25 Nm). Do not exceed \u2014 the aluminum housing strips.","Remove the drain tool from the housing. Install the small drain valve plug with its new O-ring. Snug, no torque spec, do not overtighten.","Install drain plug with new aluminum crush washer. Torque to 27 ft-lb (37 Nm).","Add 6.4 qt (5th gen) or 5.5 qt (4th gen) of correct viscosity oil through the valve cover fill cap.","Start engine. Verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds. Idle 30 seconds.","Shut off, wait 5 minutes, check dipstick. Top off as needed."],"pros":["Drain tool keeps oil off everything","100K timing chain (no belt to worry about)","Bulletproof 1GR-FE \u2014 300K is the expected service life","Cartridge filter is environmentally friendlier than spin-on"],"cons":["Cap socket is a special tool ($15)","O-ring forgotten on the drain valve = leak after 100 miles","Aluminum housing strips threads if overtightened","Aftermarket skid plates may block filter access"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-diff-fluid","title":"Rear Differential Fluid Change (4th and 5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":90},"tools_required":["24mm socket","10mm hex bit (some years)","3/8 breaker bar","fluid transfer pump","drain pan","torque wrench","jack and jack stands","wheel chocks","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["axles","fluids","75w-85","gl-5","maintenance","differential"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Genuine Differential Gear Oil LT 75W-85 GL-5 (quart)","part_number":"08885-02506","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Valvoline SynPower / Mobil 1 / Amsoil 75W-90 GL-5 full synthetic (accepted substitute)","vendor":"Valvoline / Mobil 1 / Amsoil","price_estimate_usd":13},{"name":"Drain plug crush gasket","part_number":"90430-24003","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2},{"name":"Fill plug crush gasket","part_number":"12157-10010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2},{"name":"Fluid transfer hand pump with flexible tube","vendor":"generic","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail4R \u2014 4Runner driveline fluids: how and when to change them","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/12/24/4runner-driveline-fluids-how-and-when-to-change-them/"},{"title":"The Track Ahead \u2014 front and rear differential fluid replacement, 4th gen 4Runner","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/front-and-rear-differential-fluid-replacement-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"},{"title":"Blauparts \u2014 Toyota 4Runner differential gear oil type guide","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-differential-fluid-does-my-toyota-4runner-take.html"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner","4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner","4runner-locker-rear"],"summary":"Toyota specifies **75W-85 GL-5** gear oil for the rear differential on every 4th and 5th gen 4Runner. Capacity is whatever the housing holds to the fill port \u2014 plan on roughly 3 quarts and buy 4. There is no drain interval published for normal driving; the practical answer is 30,000 miles, or 15,000 if you tow, carry roof weight, or wheel hard. It is a 30-45 minute job with one socket and a hand pump. And the single most important warning on this page: **the transfer case takes a completely different oil.** Do not fill it with what you put in the diffs.","description":"**What Toyota specifies:** Toyota Genuine Differential Gear Oil LT 75W-85 GL-5, part number 08885-02506, in both the front and rear differentials.\n\n**What most people actually use:** 75W-90 GL-5 full synthetic, because 75W-85 is scarce at parts counters and Toyota's own supply has been intermittent. This substitution is enormously common across the 4Runner, Tacoma, and Tundra platforms, and there is no documented pattern of differential failures attributed to it. If you tow near GVWR or live somewhere summer pavement bakes, the slightly heavier 75W-90 is arguably the better choice anyway.\n\n**What to actually buy:** a full synthetic GL-5 in either weight from a reputable brand \u2014 Toyota, Valvoline SynPower, Mobil 1, Amsoil Severe Gear, Red Line, or Royal Purple. Skip the conventional gear oil. The price difference over a 30,000-mile interval is a few dollars.\n\n**Friction modifier:** not needed. The factory rear differential on an SR5 or Limited is open, and the Auto LSD on those trims is a brake-based traction function that does not touch the differential at all. TRD Off-Road and TRD Pro models use an **electric locker**, which is a mechanical dog-clutch engagement, not a clutch-pack limited slip. None of these want a friction modifier. If you have installed an aftermarket clutch-type LSD, follow that manufacturer's instruction instead.\n\n**The transfer case trap, again.** Toyota calls for SAE 75W LF transfer gear oil in the transfer case \u2014 a different, much lighter, much harder-to-find fluid. Red Line MT-LV 70W/75W GL-4 is the manufacturer-confirmed direct replacement. Filling the transfer case with 75W-90 GL-5 is one of the most common mistakes on this platform. See [[4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner]] before you do all three at once.\n\nThe 4Runner rear axle is a Toyota drop-out third-member design \u2014 the ring and pinion assembly installs through the **front** of the housing, not behind a removable rear cover. That is why you will not find a cover to pull and a gasket to scrape. Drain and fill plugs only, which makes this one of the least messy differential services on any truck.\n\n4th gen (2003-2009) trucks run the 8-inch rear. 2010 and later 5th gen trucks got an improved **8.2-inch** rear with shimmed side adjusters, more ring gear bolts, and larger bearings \u2014 a meaningfully stronger axle and the reason 5th gen owners can run 33s and a locker without much anxiety. Both take the same fluid and the same service procedure.\n\nBoth plugs are 24mm hex heads on most years; a few use a 10mm internal hex. Both use single-use crush gaskets \u2014 90430-24003 for the drain, 12157-10010 for the fill, about $2 each. Buy them. Reusing a crushed gasket is how a clean job turns into a slow weep that stains your driveway for the next two years.\n\n**Fill plug first. Every time.** It is the one sequencing rule on this job and ignoring it can strand you.\n\n**Check the breather.** The differential breather vents pressure as the housing heats. If the hose is cracked, pinched, or its cap is clogged, a hot axle dropped into cold water pulls water past the seals \u2014 and water in gear oil destroys bearings quickly. Many owners relocate the breather high into the body with a hose extension. If your 4Runner sees water above the hubs, drain the diff within a week of the crossing and look for milky contamination.\n\n**Read the magnet honestly.** Metallic paste is fine. Flakes and chips are not, and a rear axle rebuild is a $2,000+ conversation. Finding it at a fluid change is exactly why fluid changes are worth doing.\n\n**Do not overtighten the plugs.** They seal on the crush gasket, not on thread torque. Stripping a fill plug thread in an aluminum-free but still finite steel housing is an avoidable, expensive mistake.\n\n**75W-85 usually needs an online order.** Toyota's own fluid goes on national backorder periodically. Order it a week ahead or plan on the 75W-90 substitution.\n\n**Set the interval to how you use the truck.** Toyota's guidance is 15,000 miles under heavy load, roof-mounted gear, or sustained towing, and inspection-based intervals otherwise. Thirty thousand miles is the reasonable default for a weekend-wheeling truck. If you regularly ford water or run deep silt, tighten it.\n\n| Item | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Toyota 08885-02506 75W-85, 3-4 quarts | $54-72 |\n| Aftermarket synthetic 75W-90, 3-4 quarts | $39-56 |\n| Crush gaskets (drain + fill) | $4 |\n| Fluid transfer hand pump (one-time) | $12-20 |\n| Shop labor, rear diff service | $90-150 |\n\nDoing all three driveline fluids at once \u2014 front diff, rear diff, transfer case \u2014 is the efficient move since the truck is already up and warm. Budget around $120-160 in fluid for the set if you use the Toyota transfer case oil, less with the Red Line substitute. Related: [[4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner]].","steps":["**Drive the truck 10-15 minutes** to warm the gear oil. Warm oil drains far more completely, and it carries the suspended debris out with it.","**Park level.** This matters more than people expect \u2014 the fill port is the capacity gauge, and a truck on a slope fills wrong. Chock the front wheels and set the parking brake.","**Raise and support the truck on jack stands** if you need the clearance. Never work under a truck supported only by a jack. Pulling the spare tire gives you noticeably more room to work.","**Position a drain pan** under the differential.","**Remove the FILL plug first**, with a 24mm socket and a breaker bar. This is the step people skip and regret. If the fill plug is seized and you have already drained the housing, you now have an undrivable truck. Confirm it comes loose before anything else happens.","**Remove the drain plug** and let it drain fully. Give it 10 minutes.","**Inspect the drain plug magnet.** A fine gray metallic paste is normal and expected. What is not normal: shiny flakes, chunks, or anything you can feel between your fingers. Milky or coffee-colored oil means water intrusion. Either finding changes the job from maintenance to diagnosis.","**Clean the magnet and the plug threads.**","**Install a fresh crush gasket on the drain plug** and torque to 36 ft-lb. Verify against the service manual for your year.","**Pump fresh gear oil into the fill port** until it reaches the threshold and begins to weep back out. Give it two extra pumps, pull the tube, and let the excess run out until it stops dripping. That is the correct level \u2014 there is no dipstick and no measured quantity.","**Install a fresh crush gasket on the fill plug** and torque to 36 ft-lb.","**Wipe the housing clean** with degreaser. A clean housing makes any future seep visible immediately.","**Check the axle breather hose** while you are under there \u2014 see below.","**Lower the truck**, drive it, and check for drips the next morning."],"pros":["30-45 minutes with one socket and a hand pump","No cover to pull, no gasket to scrape, no RTV cure time","The drain plug magnet is a free early-warning system for bearing and gear wear","Cheap insurance on an axle that costs $2,000+ to rebuild"],"cons":["The OEM 75W-85 is frequently out of stock and usually an online order","No underbody drain access without a jack and stands","Crush gaskets are single-use and skipping them causes a slow, persistent leak","The transfer case sits right there and takes a different fluid, which trips up a lot of people doing all three in one afternoon"]},{"id":"4runner-serpentine-belt","title":"Serpentine Belt and Tensioner Replacement (4th and 5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":260},"tools_required":["3/8 breaker bar or serpentine belt tool","metric socket set","14mm and 19mm sockets","torque wrench","flashlight"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","serpentine-belt","tensioner","1gr-fe","accessory-drive"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM Serpentine Belt (2010-2024 4.0L)","part_number":"90916-A2015","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Toyota OEM Serpentine Belt (2003-2009 4.0L)","part_number":"99367-H2120","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Toyota Belt Tensioner Assembly (1GR-FE)","part_number":"16620-31013","vendor":"Toyota / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Idler Pulley (grooved)","part_number":"16603-31040","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Idler Pulley (smooth)","part_number":"16604-31020","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":42},{"name":"Gates / Bando aftermarket 6-rib belt","vendor":"Gates / Bando","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"Genuine Toyota 90916-A2015 Serpentine Belt (2010-2024 4.0L 4Runner / FJ Cruiser / Tundra)","url":"https://parts.longotoyota.com/oem-parts/toyota-serpentine-belt-90916a2015"},{"title":"Genuine Toyota 99367-H2120 Drive Belt (2003-2009 4Runner 4.0 V6)","url":"https://parts.frontiertoyota.com/oem-parts/toyota-serpentine-drive-belt-2003-2009-4runner-4-0-v6-2007-2009-fj-cruiser-99367h2120"},{"title":"1GR-FE Serpentine Belt Replacement Walkthrough (2010-2016 4Runner)","url":"https://www.paulstravelpictures.com/2010-2016-Toyota-4Runner-Serpentine-Belt-Replacement-Guide/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-water-pump","4runner-power-steering-service","4runner-alternator-replacement"],"summary":"One belt runs everything on the 1GR-FE \u2014 alternator, water pump, power steering pump, A/C compressor, and the fan. Replace it around 90,000-100,000 miles regardless of how it looks, because modern EPDM belts wear smooth instead of cracking and the visual inspection everyone relies on does not work anymore. The part is 90916-A2015 on a 2010-2024 truck, 99367-H2120 on a 2003-2009, about $35 either way, and an hour in the driveway. If it chirps, the tensioner is the more likely culprit than the belt.","description":"There is no separate power steering belt on this platform. Some Toyota trucks split the accessory drive across two belts; the 4Runner's 4.0L V6 does not. One 6-rib serpentine wraps the crank, water pump, alternator, power steering pump, A/C compressor, an automatic tensioner, and two idlers. That single-belt architecture is the whole reason this job matters more than its price suggests \u2014 when the belt goes, you lose charging, steering assist, and coolant circulation at the same moment. On pavement that is a tow. Twenty miles up a Forest Service road it is a considerably worse afternoon.\n\nBelts made before roughly 2005 used neoprene, which hardened and cracked across the ribs as it aged. You could look at one and know. EPDM belts \u2014 everything current, including the Toyota part \u2014 do not crack. They abrade, losing rib material a fraction of a millimeter at a time, and a belt with 40% of its material gone still looks glossy and intact to the naked eye.\n\nTwo things replace the old eyeball test:\n\n**Mileage.** Plan on replacement at 90,000-100,000 miles. Under 60,000 miles a belt is almost never the problem. Over 120,000 miles it is overdue no matter how it presents.\n\n**A wear gauge.** Gates and Continental both give away a small plastic go/no-go gauge that drops into the rib valleys. If it sits flush with the belt surface instead of standing proud, the ribs have worn past spec. Any parts counter that sells belts will hand you one.\n\n**Noise, read correctly.** A chirp that appears on cold start and fades as the engine warms is a tensioner or misalignment signature far more often than a worn belt. A continuous squeal under electrical or steering load points at tension. Replacing the belt alone on a chirping truck is the most common wasted repair on this platform.\n\nThe automatic tensioner (Toyota 16620-31013 on the 1GR-FE) uses an internal spring and damper to hold constant tension as the belt stretches and the accessories load and unload. Two things fail in it: the spring weakens, and the pulley bearing dries out.\n\nTest it with the belt off. Rotate the tensioner arm through its full travel with a breaker bar \u2014 it should move smoothly against firm, even spring pressure and return under control, not fall back. Spin the pulley by hand: it should be silent and free, with no grit, no wobble, and no side play at the arm pivot. Any roughness, any lateral rock, or a spring that feels soft means replace it.\n\nAt around $95 for the tensioner and $42-45 per idler, doing all three with the belt is roughly $220 in parts on a 150,000-mile truck. That is defensible preventive work on a vehicle you take away from pavement. On an 80,000-mile daily driver, belt only is the right call.\n\nA 3/8 breaker bar (the tensioner arm on the 1GR-FE takes a square drive directly, so a dedicated serpentine tool is optional but makes the reach around the fan far less unpleasant), a metric socket set, a torque wrench for the tensioner and idler bolts, and a flashlight. Access on the 5th gen is tight between the fan shroud and the radiator \u2014 some people pull the shroud, most work around it. Photograph the routing before anything comes apart, and photograph it again from a second angle.\n\n1. Engine cold, key out. Photograph the belt routing from the front and from above, or find the routing decal on the radiator support.\n2. Optional but recommended on the 5th gen: remove the upper fan shroud (a few 10mm bolts and clips) for working room.\n3. Seat the breaker bar on the tensioner arm and rotate it to release tension. On the 1GR-FE the arm swings toward the passenger side.\n4. With tension released, slip the belt off the top of the alternator pulley \u2014 the accessible one \u2014 then let the tensioner return under control. Do not let it snap; the internal damper does not appreciate it.\n5. Work the old belt out around the fan. It comes out, but not on the first path you try.\n6. Inspect while it is open: spin every pulley by hand and check the tensioner as described above. This is the moment to decide on the tensioner, not after the new belt is on.\n7. If replacing the tensioner or idlers, unbolt and swap them now, torquing the mounting bolts to the service-manual value for your year. These bolts thread into aluminum \u2014 do not eyeball them.\n8. Route the new belt per your photos, over every pulley except the alternator, keeping every rib seated in every groove as you go.\n9. Rotate the tensioner again, slip the belt onto the alternator pulley, and release tension smoothly.\n10. Walk every pulley with a flashlight. The belt must be centered on each ribbed pulley with no rib hanging off either edge.\n11. Reinstall the shroud, start the engine, and watch the belt track for 30 seconds. Then drive it and listen.\n\n**One pulley off in the routing.** The classic mistake is running the belt on the wrong side of a smooth idler, which reverses the water pump. It will run, it will look almost right, and it will overheat. Your photo is the check.\n\n**A belt riding on an edge.** If the new belt is not centered on a ribbed pulley, stop. That means a misrouted path or a misaligned accessory bracket, and it will shred the belt in short order \u2014 sometimes in miles.\n\n**Letting the tensioner slam.** Controlled release, every time. A snapped-back tensioner can fail the internal damper immediately.\n\n**Chirp after a new belt.** Do not replace the belt a second time. The remaining suspects, in order: tired tensioner, dry idler bearing, a misaligned pulley, or a contaminated belt surface from a coolant or power steering weep. Track down the leak before you install a third belt \u2014 see [[4runner-power-steering-service]] if the fluid is coming from the front of the pump.\n\n**Overheating right after the job.** If the temperature climbs after a belt change, shut it down and re-verify routing before doing anything else. Related reading: [[4runner-water-pump]] and [[4runner-cooling-service]].\n\n| Item | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Toyota 90916-A2015 belt (2010-2024) | $35-42 |\n| Toyota 99367-H2120 belt (2003-2009) | $32-38 |\n| Gates or Bando aftermarket belt | $25-32 |\n| Tensioner assembly 16620-31013 | $85-105 |\n| Grooved idler 16603-31040 | $40-50 |\n| Smooth idler 16604-31020 | $38-48 |\n| Shop labor, belt only | $120-200 |\n| Shop labor, belt plus tensioner and idlers | $350-500 |\n\nCarrying a spare belt is worth the $30 if you travel remote. A serpentine belt is one of the few failures that can be limped past with a spare and hand tools on the trail, and it is the only single part whose failure takes out charging, steering assist, and cooling at once."},{"id":"4runner-spark-plugs-4runner","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 1GR-FE 4.0L","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":160},"tools_required":["3/8\" ratchet","10mm socket","long extension","5/8\" spark plug socket","torque wrench","T20 Torx"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","intake removal patience"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["ignition","preventative","1GR-FE"],"parts":[{"name":"Denso K20HR-U11 nickel (set of 6) \u2014 OEM","part_number":"90919-01235","vendor":"Toyota / Denso","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"NGK LFR6C11 nickel (set of 6) \u2014 OEM alt","part_number":"90919-01236","vendor":"Toyota / NGK","price_estimate_usd":42},{"name":"Denso SK20HR11 iridium long-life (set of 6) \u2014 extended interval","part_number":"3438","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Dielectric grease","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":4}],"citations":[{"title":"TheTrackAhead \u2014 4th gen 4Runner spark plug guide","url":"https://thetrackahead.com/projects/4th-gen-toyota-4runner/spark-plug-replacement-1gre-fe-40l-v6-4th-gen-toyota-4runner/"},{"title":"Toyota-4runner.org \u2014 1GR-FE plug change instructions","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org/maintenance-detailing/57478-4-0-v6-1gr-fe-spark-plug-change-instructions.html"},{"title":"ToyotaNation \u2014 Changing plugs on the 1GR-FE","url":"https://www.toyotanation.com/threads/changing-the-spark-plugs-on-the-1gr-fe-4-0-v6.378215/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-oil-change-4runner","4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues"],"summary":"The 1GR-FE V6 calls for nickel plugs at 30,000 miles. You can run Denso iridium long-life plugs to push the interval out, but plan on pulling the intake plenum to reach the rear three plugs on either choice.","description":"The 1GR-FE 4.0L V6 in the 4th and 5th gen 4Runner has been one of Toyota's most reliable engines, but Toyota specced it with nickel-copper plugs and a short 30,000-mile interval. That short interval is the price you pay for getting at the rear three plugs \u2014 they sit underneath the upper intake plenum, and reaching them means removing six plenum bolts, the throttle body harness, and the PCV hose. Many owners buy iridium long-life plugs once and skip the chore for the next 100,000 miles.\n\nOEM front-three plugs are Denso K20HR-U11 (Toyota 90919-01235) or NGK LFR6C11 (Toyota 90919-01236) \u2014 both nickel. The factory-equivalent iridium upgrade is Denso SK20HR11 (Denso #3438), which fits identically and runs roughly 100,000 miles. Gap spec is 0.043\" (1.1mm) \u2014 verify with a feeler gauge on every new plug, even iridium pre-gapped sets, because shipping drops happen.\n\nThe front three plugs are manageable: pop the coil pack cover, unbolt each coil with a 10mm, lift it out, and pull the plug with a 5/8\" deep socket on a long extension. Twenty minutes total. The rear three are the actual job. Sequence matters: disconnect the throttle body connectors, the EVAP line, the PCV hose, the brake booster vacuum hose, and unbolt the upper plenum (six 12mm bolts). Lift the plenum off, set it aside on a clean shop rag, and inspect the gasket \u2014 if it is the original rubberized gasket, replace it now (Toyota 17171-31040, about $35).\n\nTorque the plugs to 13 ft-lb (17.5 N\u00b7m). The 1GR-FE uses 14mm plugs, but the same caution applies as any aluminum head \u2014 go past 18 ft-lb and you risk pulling threads. No anti-seize on the plug threads (Denso and NGK both say not to). A dab of dielectric grease in each coil boot keeps the boot from sticking the next time.\n\nReinstall the plenum with a fresh gasket if you pulled the old one. Hit each bolt in a crisscross pattern at 18 ft-lb. Reconnect every harness, hose, and clip you removed. Disconnect the battery for 5 minutes before reconnecting to force the ECU to relearn idle on first start.","steps":["Cold engine. Disconnect negative battery cable.","Remove the engine cover (pulls up off rubber grommets).","Front three plugs first: unbolt each coil pack (10mm), lift coil, pull plug with 5/8\" socket on a long extension.","Verify gap (0.043\"), install new plug hand-tight, torque to 13 ft-lb.","Reinstall coil with dielectric grease in the boot.","Rear three: disconnect throttle body connectors, EVAP line, PCV hose, brake booster line.","Unbolt the upper plenum (six 12mm bolts). Lift off. Cover the lower intake openings with rags.","Repeat coil-and-plug procedure on cylinders 2, 4, 6.","Reinstall plenum with fresh gasket if needed. Torque bolts in a crisscross pattern to 18 ft-lb.","Reconnect all hoses, sensors, and harnesses. Reconnect battery. Start, listen for vacuum leaks at the plenum gasket."],"pros":["Iridium upgrade pushes the next service to 100,000 miles","Restores smooth idle and fuel economy on a high-mileage 1GR-FE","Catches a leaking plenum gasket while you have the intake apart","Skill builds your confidence for bigger intake jobs later"],"cons":["Rear three plugs require intake plenum removal","14mm aluminum head threads strip if you over-torque","Plenum gasket may need replacement if original (extra $35)","3\u20134 hour first-time job \u2014 set a full afternoon aside"]},{"id":"4runner-transfer-case-fluid-4runner","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Change","vehicle":"4runner","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":45},"tools_required":["24mm socket","1/2 ratchet","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["fluids","drivetrain","preventive"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota Genuine 75W Transfer Case Fluid (1 quart)","part_number":"08885-81080","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Toyota Drain Plug Gasket (crush washer)","part_number":"12157-10010","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"Blau Parts \u2014 Toyota 4Runner Transfer Case Fluid Types","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-transfer-case-fluid-does-my-toyota-4runner-take.html"},{"title":"5th gen 4Runner transfer case oil \u2014 BobIsTheOilGuy","url":"https://bobistheoilguy.com/forums/threads/5th-gen-4runner-transfer-case-oil.396812/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-diff-fluid-4runner","4runner-rear-diff-fluid"],"summary":"5th gen 4Runners (2010\u20132024) take 1.4 liters of Toyota's 75W transfer case fluid \u2014 part 08885-81080. Plan on 30 minutes, two crush washers, and a fluid pump.","description":"Toyota's recommended service interval is 60,000 miles for severe-use and never for normal-use \u2014 meaning if you ever tow, drive dusty roads, hit dirt, or live in Phoenix, you fall under severe service. Real-world: change it at 60k regardless. The factory fill is dark amber when new and looks like burnt coffee when worn. The 5th gen V8 RX is gone \u2014 this guide covers the 4.0L 1GR-FE with the VF4BM transfer case found in SR5, TRD Off-Road, TRD Pro, and Limited.\n\nThe critical part-number note: the 5th gen takes 75W transfer case fluid, not 75W-90 gear oil. Toyota changed the spec around 2010 to a lighter fluid to reduce drivetrain drag. Part 08885-81080 (sometimes labeled 08885-01806) is the correct fill. Old-school internet advice telling you to use 75W-90 is leftover from 3rd and 4th gen 4Runners. Use the right fluid \u2014 your case will thank you.\n\nPull the 4Runner up onto level ground (ramps or jack stands; do not work on a slope or you'll over- or under-fill). The transfer case sits behind the transmission, easily accessible from below. The fill plug is on the driver's side, upper position, 24mm hex head. The drain plug is on the bottom, also 24mm. Crack the fill plug first \u2014 same rule as every other case fluid job. If the fill won't open, you don't want to find that out after the drain is empty on your driveway.\n\nDrain into a clean catch pan so you can inspect the fluid. Light metallic shimmer is normal, chunks are not. Install a fresh crush washer on the drain plug (don't reuse the old one; it won't seal right) and torque to 27 ft-lb. Pump 1.4 liters / 1.47 quarts of fresh 75W through the fill hole \u2014 the case is full when fluid trickles out the fill port. Install the fill plug with a new crush washer at 27 ft-lb. Check for leaks after a short drive.","steps":["Park on level ground. Get the 4Runner up on ramps or jack stands.","Locate the transfer case behind the transmission. Identify fill plug (driver side, upper) and drain plug (bottom).","Crack the fill plug loose first with a 24mm socket. If it won't open, stop and address.","Position drain pan, remove drain plug, let fluid drain fully (~10 min).","Inspect drained fluid for metal chunks or unusual contamination.","Install fresh crush washer on drain plug. Torque to 27 ft-lb.","Pump fresh Toyota 75W (part 08885-81080) into fill hole until fluid trickles back out \u2014 about 1.4 liters.","Install fill plug with new crush washer. Torque to 27 ft-lb.","Lower the vehicle, start engine, run through 4H and 4L to circulate.","Park on level again and re-check the fill level after 5 minutes."],"pros":["30-minute job; transfer case rebuilds run $1,500+","Toyota OE fluid is widely available from any Toyota dealer","Crush washers are cheap and prevent slow weeps"],"cons":["Wrong fluid (75W-90 instead of 75W) increases drag and can affect mileage","Aluminum case threads strip if you over-torque the plugs","Reusing crush washers is a guaranteed slow leak"]},{"id":"4runner-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Starter Kit for the 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["none required for kit assembly"],"skills_required":["basic recovery technique","rigging awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["recovery","kinetic-rope","snatch-strap","traction-boards","hi-lift"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Recovery Bag Kit (kinetic rope, snatch block, tree saver, gloves)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Bubba Rope 20' 7/8\" Gator-Jaw Kinetic Recovery Rope","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":119},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Traction Boards (pair)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Rhino USA 3/4\" Forged D-Ring Shackles (pair)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Recovery Gear Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Maxtrax MKII Product Page","url":"https://www.maxtrax.com.au"}],"alternatives":["4runner-winch","4runner-front-bumper","4runner-rear-bumper"],"summary":"A kinetic rope, D-rings, traction boards, and a snatch block cover most trail recovery situations \u2014 and the 4Runner's factory front tow hooks are genuine recovery points, which streamlines the attachment question compared to some other IFS platforms.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner includes factory tow hooks in the front that are frame-mounted, painted in body color, and often overlooked. These are functional recovery points for straight-ahead pull recovery with a kinetic rope. Attach a D-ring shackle (3/4\" rated, like the Rhino USA units) to the factory hook eye and you have a legitimate front attachment point \u2014 no steel bumper required for basic recoveries. For rear recovery, the OEM receiver hitch is the correct attachment point; use a hitch-mount D-ring receiver shackle to connect a rope.\n\nThe kinetic rope is the most important item in any recovery kit. Bubba Rope's Gator-Jaw 7/8\" 20' kinetic rope stores energy during extension and releases it as a smooth forward pull \u2014 far more effective than a static tow strap and much gentler on the recovery points. Static straps shock-load the anchor points on a stuck vehicle; kinetic ropes don't. A snatch block doubles the pull force when rigged as a mechanical advantage system \u2014 essential for deep mud or heavy-vehicle recoveries where a single vehicle pull isn't enough.\n\nMaxtrax traction boards are the self-recovery go-to for sand, mud, and snow. Dig out the wheel, slide the board under the drive tire, and drive off it. The 4Runner's locked rear differential (on Trail and TRD Pro trims) combined with Maxtrax turns single-vehicle self-recovery into a reliable procedure even in soft terrain. If your 4Runner is an SR5 or Limited without a locker, Maxtrax plus controlled throttle will still get you out of most situations where only one wheel is buried.\n\nKeep the recovery kit in a dedicated bag or box where it can be reached without unloading the cargo area. ARB's recovery bag includes everything in a single roll-top bag with organized pockets for each item \u2014 a practical solution for 4Runners where the cargo floor is already occupied by gear.","steps":["Verify front tow hook condition \u2014 inspect for cracks or deformation before relying on them","Obtain hitch-mount D-ring receiver shackle for rear recovery point if steel bumper is not installed","Store kinetic rope and snatch block in ARB or similar recovery bag, accessible from rear hatch without full unloading","Mount Maxtrax boards to roof rack or exterior mount where they can be accessed when the truck is stuck \u2014 not buried under gear","Carry two D-ring shackles at minimum \u2014 one for the front and one for the rear simultaneously","Practice a vehicle-to-vehicle kinetic rope recovery in a low-stakes situation before relying on the technique","After any recovery: inspect rope for cuts, check shackles for deformation, dry out gear before storing"],"pros":["Factory front tow hooks on the 4Runner are genuine frame-mounted recovery points \u2014 eliminates the bumper prerequisite for basic recovery","ARB Recovery Bag bundles kinetic rope, snatch block, tree saver, and gloves in one organized kit","Maxtrax boards enable self-recovery \u2014 valuable for solo overland trips where a second vehicle isn't available","Kinetic rope is safer and more effective than static tow straps in vehicle-to-vehicle recovery","Compact kit \u2014 the full setup fits in a medium bag under the rear cargo floor"],"cons":["Factory tow hooks are for straight pulls only \u2014 angled recovery loads can bend the hook eye","Rear recovery requires a hitch-mount shackle without a steel bumper \u2014 a weak link if the recovery involves a hard pull","Maxtrax boards are expensive for what they are; cheaper traction boards exist but with lower grip and durability","Without a locker (SR5/Limited), traction boards require correct throttle technique to work \u2014 recovery is not automatic","Recovery gear provides no value without operator technique \u2014 learn proper kinetic rope handling before the first trail trip"]},{"id":"4runner-recovery-points","title":"Recovery Points for the 4Runner \u2014 Frame-Mounted D-Rings and Shackle Mounts","vehicle":"4runner","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":350},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill (if adding supplemental points)","thread locker"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","recovery rigging awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["recovery","recovery_points","d-ring","shackle","self-recovery","tow points"],"parts":[{"name":"Bullet Proof Fab Rear Recovery Points \u2014 5th Gen 4Runner","vendor":"Bullet Proof Fab","price_estimate_usd":239},{"name":"Factor 55 FlatLink Locking Shackle","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":79},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Rear Receiver Hitch Recovery Point","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":89}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Recovery Products Line","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Factor 55 Shackle Products","url":"https://www.factor55.com"},{"title":"Warn Vehicles 4Runner Recovery Point Info","url":"https://www.warn.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-recovery-gear-kit","4runner-winch","4runner-front-bumper"],"summary":"The 4Runner's OEM tow hooks are not recovery points \u2014 they're stamped steel rated for towing, not the dynamic shock loads of a vehicle extraction. Adding purpose-built frame-mounted D-ring mounts is a $100\u2013$250 job that makes every recovery safer for your rig and for the vehicle helping you out.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner's factory tow hooks are bolted to the frame but are not rated for the shock loads a vehicle extraction generates. When a strap goes tight at full stretch, the load spike can be 2\u20133x the vehicle weight. The OEM hooks are adequate for controlled towing on flat ground \u2014 they're not tested for the directional loading and shock forces that occur when a stuck vehicle gets yanked.\n\nDedicated recovery points address two problems: they're rated for the required MBS (minimum breaking strength), and they provide a clean connection point that won't deform or strip when loaded from an angle.\n\n**Tools:**\n- Socket set (17mm, 19mm for frame bolt sizes)\n- Torque wrench\n- Threadlocker (Loctite Blue on the shackle pin collar screw, Red on frame hardware if specified)\n- Drill with step bit if adding supplemental recovery tabs to a receiver hitch\n\n**Parts \u2014 choose a tier:**\n\n| Option | Product | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Budget front | ARB Recovery Points (pair, bolt-in) | ~$189 | Clean frame-mount, rated 10,000 lb each |\n| Budget rear | Receiver hitch D-ring mount | ~$40\u2013$90 | Works with factory receiver hitch; angle matters |\n| Mid rear | Bullet Proof Fab Rear Recovery Points | ~$239 | Frame-mount weld tabs, cleaner than receiver option |\n| Shackles | Rhino USA 4.75-ton D-rings (2-pack) | ~$38 | Adequate for most extractions; replace if gate shows wear |\n| Shackles (premium) | Factor 55 FlatLink | ~$79 each | Screw-lock gate, won't back out in dynamic load |\n\n**Front recovery point install (ARB or similar bolt-in kit):**\n\n1. Locate the factory tow hook mounts on the 4Runner frame \u2014 they're behind the front bumper, typically visible after removing the lower front fascia trim piece (two bolts, clips).\n2. Remove the OEM tow hooks (two bolts per side, typically 17mm or 19mm). Retain the hardware if the replacement kit uses it, or discard if the new kit supplies its own grade-8 hardware.\n3. Position the new recovery point bracket against the frame. These are typically designed to use the factory mounting holes with longer-reach grade-8 bolts.\n4. Thread in by hand, apply threadlocker if specified by the kit instructions, and torque to spec \u2014 typically 70\u201390 ft-lb for frame-mounted recovery hardware.\n5. Repeat opposite side.\n6. Install D-ring shackle through the recovery point eye. Tighten the screw pin to finger-tight plus 1/4 turn. If using a Factor 55 FlatLink, install per its instructions (the collar locks the gate).\n\n**Rear recovery point install (receiver hitch mount):**\n\n1. The receiver hitch mount slides into the factory 2\" receiver and is secured with a pin/clip. Choose one with a full-width bar rather than a single-point mount \u2014 load distribution matters.\n2. Thread the D-ring through the mount eye. Apply thread locker to the collar screw if using an unlocked shackle.\n3. For a permanent frame-mount rear point (Bullet Proof Fab or similar weld-on kit), installation requires a MIG welder and appropriate frame prep \u2014 confirm the mounting surface is clean steel, not galvanized, before welding.\n\n- **D-ring screw pins back out** during recovery under dynamic load. A wrap of electrical tape over the pin after tightening prevents this for shackles without a locking mechanism. Factor 55 FlatLink shackles use a mechanical collar that eliminates this failure mode.\n- Receiver hitch recovery points are only as good as the receiver hitch's attachment to the frame. Inspect the receiver mounting bolts before relying on it for recovery.\n- The angle of the recovery point relative to the pull direction matters. A recovery point mounted for a straight rearward pull loses rated capacity significantly when loaded at an angle. Frame-mounted points rated for multi-directional loading (ARB, Factor 55 bumper integrated) are preferable for trail use where the recovery direction is unpredictable.\n- Inspect D-ring gates before every recovery season. A bent gate indicates the shackle was shock-loaded beyond its safe working load \u2014 replace it.\n- Soft shackles (Dyneema) are an alternative to steel D-rings for weight-conscious builds and won't damage a vehicle if the connection fails. Rated soft shackles at 30,000\u201340,000 lb MBS are the preference of most dedicated overland travelers.\n\n- **Factor 55 FlatLink:** ~$75\u2013$85 per shackle from Factor 55 directly and off-road specialty retailers.\n- **Bullet Proof Fab rear recovery points:** ~$230\u2013$260, available direct from Bullet Proof Fab.\n- **Receiver hitch D-ring mount (budget):** ~$40\u2013$60 at any 4WD or towing retailer, or Amazon.\n\nFor most 4Runner owners, the correct approach is ARB bolt-in recovery points front (~$189) + receiver hitch D-ring mount rear (~$50) + two quality steel shackles or soft shackles (~$40\u2013$80). That's a complete, tested recovery point setup for under $320 \u2014 skip the cheaper stamped-bracket alternatives and buy this once."},{"id":"4runner-traction-boards","title":"Traction Boards: Picking and Using Them (4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"recovery","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":300},"tools_required":["mounting hardware","small shovel"],"skills_required":["recovery basics"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["recovery","traction-boards","self-recovery","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"MAXTRAX MKII Recovery Boards (pair)","vendor":"MAXTRAX","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"ActionTrax / X-Bull Budget Boards (pair)","vendor":"ActionTrax","price_estimate_usd":70},{"name":"Bed/Rack Mount Pins for Boards","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Self-Recovery with Traction Boards","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Recovery Board Technique","url":"https://www.expeditionportal.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-recovery-gear-kit","4runner-winch"],"summary":"Traction boards are the most useful recovery tool a solo 4Runner driver can carry, because they need no anchor, no second vehicle, and no winch \u2014 the boards and some patience. For most owners a quality pair in the $200\u2013$300 range is worth it; the $70 budget boards work but flex and wear faster. Either beats being stuck alone with nothing.","description":"When a 4Runner gets stuck in sand, mud, or snow, the problem is almost always lost traction at one or two wheels, not lost power. Traction boards solve that directly: you clear the wheel, wedge a board under the tire in the direction you want to go, and drive out gently. They're the one recovery tool that works when you're alone with no anchor point in sight \u2014 which describes a lot of Arizona desert two-tracks.\n\nThe market splits into premium boards (MAXTRAX and similar, stiff reinforced nylon, replaceable nubs, lifetime-grade) and budget boards (lighter plastic, more flex, shorter life). Both will recover a stuck 4Runner. The premium boards earn their price if you wheel often, recover heavy, or want boards that won't melt their nubs under wheelspin. For occasional use, budget boards are an honest starting point \u2014 resist the urge to spin the tires on them.\n\nA pair of boards sized for a full-size SUV, a mounting solution (rack pins, bed mount, or rear-door carrier), and a small shovel to clear the wheels. Gloves keep your hands clean and protected. That's the whole kit \u2014 no kinetic rope, no shackles required for a board recovery, though you'll want those for other recovery methods.\n\n1. Stop spinning the moment you're stuck \u2014 continued wheelspin digs you deeper and overheats the boards later\n2. Clear the sand, mud, or snow from in front of (or behind) the stuck tires with the shovel\n3. Wedge a board firmly under each stuck tire, ramp end toward your intended direction of travel, pushed as far under as it will go\n4. With a spotter clear of the path, drive out slowly and steadily \u2014 minimum throttle, no spinning\n5. Keep moving until all four tires are on solid ground; don't stop on the boards\n6. Retrieve the boards (a leash or rope through the handles makes this more manageable) and clean them before stowing\n\nSpinning the tires on the boards is the cardinal sin \u2014 it melts the traction nubs on cheap boards and glazes even good ones. Use the least throttle that moves the truck. Never stand in the path of travel during a recovery; a board can launch if a tire climbs and slips. Mount boards securely \u2014 an unsecured board becomes a projectile in a rollover or hard stop. And carry a shovel; boards work far better after you've cleared the wheel than jammed against a packed wall of sand.\n\nPremium boards like MAXTRAX MKII run about $300 a pair; capable budget boards are around $70. Mounting hardware adds $35 or so. For a daily-driven 4Runner that sees occasional sand or snow, budget boards plus a shovel are a reasonable $100 entry. If you wheel hard or solo regularly, the premium pair is the buy-once option \u2014 and they double as a leveling base for a jack on soft ground."},{"id":"4runner-winch","title":"Winch for the 4Runner \u2014 10,000 lb Minimum","vehicle":"4runner","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire cutters/stripper","multimeter","drill and bits"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic electrical wiring"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery","synthetic-rope","waterproof"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 10K Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Warn M10000-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Warn Zeon 10-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1099}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Winch Selection Guide","url":"https://www.warn.com"},{"title":"Smittybilt X20 10K Product Page","url":"https://www.smittybilt.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-front-bumper","4runner-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"The 4Runner's GVWR of 4,750\u20135,300 lbs puts the minimum winch rating at 10,000 lbs \u2014 and a steel front bumper with an integrated winch plate is required infrastructure before the winch itself can be safely installed.","description":"The 1.5x GVWR rule puts the 4Runner firmly at 10,000 lbs minimum line pull for the winch. The Warn VR EVO 10-S is the correct choice for most 4Runner builds: it's a proven IP68-rated waterproof motor, uses Warn's quality control standard, runs 10,000 lbs of line pull on synthetic rope, and the price point is realistic. The Warn Zeon 10-S is the premium alternative \u2014 machined aluminum fairlead, faster line speed, and better heat dissipation for prolonged use. For rock crawling where the winch may run multiple full-drum cycles in a session, the Zeon's thermal capacity matters.\n\nWaterproofing ratings matter for the 4Runner more than for some other applications. The 4Runner is commonly used in stream crossings and muddy terrain. IP67 or IP68 sealing on the winch motor and control box is the specification to verify \u2014 lower ratings can allow water ingress during repeated water crossings, which damages the motor over time. The Warn VR EVO, M10000-S, and Zeon series all meet IP68. The Smittybilt X20 is rated IP67 \u2014 one step down \u2014 and the control box is the more vulnerable component to watch.\n\nRemote vs in-cab control: every winch on this list includes a wireless remote. Using the remote from a safe distance is the correct operating procedure \u2014 stand clear of the synthetic rope line and operate from a 90\u00b0 angle to the pull direction where possible. Route the hardwired in-cab control lead for a backup control option.\n\nSteel front bumper with winch plate is a firm prerequisite. The 4Runner OEM bumper has no winch mounting provision. The load path from the winch through the bumper to the frame is the structural element that makes the recovery work \u2014 a winch bolted to a body-mounted plate or bolted through the plastic bumper skin is a failure waiting to happen under load.","steps":["Install steel front bumper with integrated winch plate before ordering winch","Confirm winch plate dimensions match winch (most use 10\" x 4.5\" SAE standard pattern)","Mount winch to winch plate; torque all four mounting bolts to manufacturer spec","Route positive cable from winch to battery; do not connect to battery terminal yet","Route negative cable to chassis ground or battery negative with clean connection point","Route control harness into cab; mount in-cab switch or store wireless remote accessibly","Connect positive cable to battery terminal last","Test winch in both directions with no load; verify all control options function","Spool out full rope under light tension to seat rope evenly on drum","Test pull against a fixed anchor before trusting the system in the field"],"pros":["Warn VR EVO 10-S is the benchmark mid-range winch \u2014 IP68 rated, reliable, backed by Warn's warranty and parts availability","Wireless remote standard on all listed models \u2014 operate from a safe distance from the line","Snatch block doubles effective pull to 20,000 lbs for heavy recoveries","Synthetic rope is lighter, safer, and more manageable to handle than steel cable","Warn Zeon 10-S is worth the premium for rock crawling builds that will run the winch hard"],"cons":["Steel front bumper is a mandatory prerequisite \u2014 total winch system cost is winch + bumper ($1,100\u2013$2,800 combined)","Smittybilt X20 is adequate for occasional trail recovery but has a weaker motor and less robust internals than Warn \u2014 not recommended for frequent heavy use","Winch adds 60\u201390 lbs to the front axle \u2014 check front axle gross weight if the build is heavily loaded","Synthetic rope can be cut by sharp metal edges during recovery \u2014 use a rope protector sleeve at any contact point","In-cab wiring at 400\u2013500 amp draw requires proper gauge wire (2/0 AWG minimum) \u2014 undersized wire is a fire hazard"]},{"id":"4runner-power-steering-service","title":"Power Steering Fluid Service and Pump Replacement (4th and 5th Gen 4Runner)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"steering","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":3.5},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":400},"tools_required":["fluid extractor or turkey baster","drain pan","metric socket set","flare wrench set","3/8 breaker bar","hose clamp pliers","funnel","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["steering","power-steering","fluid","maintenance","atf","whine"],"parts":[{"name":"Dexron II/III-compatible ATF (2-3 quarts for a full exchange)","vendor":"Valvoline / Mobil 1 / Toyota","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Toyota / Aisin power steering pump assembly (vane type, 1GR-FE)","vendor":"Toyota / Aisin","price_estimate_usd":230},{"name":"Pressure line O-rings and return hose clamps","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Serpentine belt (90916-A2015, 2010-2024) \u2014 replace if removed and worn","part_number":"90916-A2015","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":38}],"citations":[{"title":"4Runner owner's manual power steering fluid specification \u2014 Dexron II or III ATF","url":"https://bobistheoilguy.com/forums/threads/4runner-dexron-ii-iii-atf-for-ps.263787/"},{"title":"Toyota power steering fluid recommendations discussion","url":"https://bobistheoilguy.com/forums/threads/toyota-ps-recommendations.314758/"},{"title":"1GR-FE accessory drive \u2014 single serpentine belt drives the power steering pump","url":"https://www.paulstravelpictures.com/2010-2016-Toyota-4Runner-Serpentine-Belt-Replacement-Guide/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tie-rods-4runner","4runner-serpentine-belt","4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner"],"summary":"A whine that rises with engine speed, a groan at full lock, or dark foamy fluid in the reservoir almost always means the fluid is exhausted \u2014 not that the pump is dead. Do a fluid exchange first. It costs about $15 and takes half an hour, and it resolves the majority of 4Runner power steering complaints. And get the fluid right: the 4Runner calls for **Dexron II/III-compatible ATF**, not ATF WS and not bottled \"power steering fluid.\" Only after fresh fluid and a good belt fail to quiet things does a $230 pump enter the conversation.","description":"The 4Runner uses a conventional engine-driven hydraulic rack-and-pinion system through both the 4th and 5th generations. The vane pump is bolted to the front of the engine and driven by the **main serpentine belt** \u2014 there is no separate power steering belt on the 1GR-FE, which means a belt or tensioner problem shows up as a steering symptom and gets misdiagnosed as a pump. Fluid goes from the reservoir to the pump, out under pressure through a steel line to the rack, and back through a low-pressure return hose to the reservoir, passing a filter screen in the reservoir on the way.\n\nPower steering is a safety-critical system and this job has two ways to bite. **First:** the pressure line runs at high pressure with the engine running \u2014 never loosen a pressure fitting on a running engine, and give the system a minute to settle after shutdown. **Second:** if you disturb any line, you must confirm zero leaks and full, air-free assist before the truck goes back on the road. A slow leak that empties the reservoir on a highway on-ramp takes the assist away exactly when you need it. Check the reservoir level and look under the truck for drips before the first drive, after the first drive, and again the next morning.\n\nWork through this in order. Each step is cheaper than the next one.\n\n**Check the fluid.** Cold engine, look at the reservoir. Healthy ATF is translucent red or amber. Dark brown, opaque, foamy, or smelling burnt means the fluid is finished. Foam specifically indicates aerated fluid \u2014 either a low level or air being drawn in on the suction side.\n\n**Check the level, cold and hot.** The reservoir has separate COLD and HOT marks and they matter. A level read hot against the cold line reads falsely full.\n\n**Check the belt and tensioner.** A glazed or loose belt slips under steering load and produces a squeal that sounds exactly like a starving pump. Grab the belt and inspect the tensioner's function before condemning anything hydraulic \u2014 see [[4runner-serpentine-belt]].\n\n**Listen to what the noise does.** A whine that tracks engine RPM and gets louder at full lock is normal pump behavior amplified by tired fluid. A whine present at idle in a straight line with no steering input is a pump nearing the end. A groan or shudder only at full lock, especially cold, is usually air or fluid.\n\n**Look for the leak.** If the level keeps dropping, find where. The pressure line's crimped fittings, the rack's inner boots (pull a boot back and look for fluid), and the pump's front seal are the three usual sources. A rack leaking into its boots is a rack, not a pump, and no amount of fluid service will fix it.\n\nThe suck-and-fill method refreshes the entire system over a few cycles without opening a single line. It is slower than a return-line flush and considerably harder to get wrong.\n\n1. Engine cold and off. Wipe the reservoir cap and its surroundings so nothing falls in.\n2. Draw the reservoir empty with a fluid extractor or a dedicated turkey baster. Do not use the one from the kitchen and do not put it back.\n3. Refill to the COLD line with fresh Dexron II/III-compatible ATF.\n4. Start the engine and turn the wheel lock to lock slowly, four or five times. Do not hold it against the stop \u2014 holding at full lock deadheads the pump and heats the fluid fast.\n5. Shut the engine off, let it settle, and check the level.\n6. Repeat steps 2 through 5. Three or four cycles, ideally spread across a couple of drive cycles, will get the system to clean fluid.\n7. After the final fill, check the level cold, then drive the truck and check it hot.\n\nFor a faster full exchange, disconnect the return hose at the reservoir, run it into a catch bottle, plug the reservoir port, and have a helper turn the wheel at idle while you keep the reservoir topped up. It moves all the old fluid in about two minutes, and the risk is real: run the reservoir dry for a few seconds and you aerate the pump. If you do it this way, keep pouring and do not let the level drop below half.\n\nOnly after fluid, level, belt, and leak checks come back clean.\n\n1. Engine cold and off, truck on stands, front wheels off the ground.\n2. Release the serpentine belt with a breaker bar on the tensioner and remove it. Inspect it \u2014 if it has real miles, replace it while it is off.\n3. Place a drain pan under the pump. Loosen the return hose clamp and pull the hose; let it drain.\n4. Loosen the pressure line fitting with a **flare wrench**, not an open-end. That fitting is soft and rounding it turns a pump job into a line job.\n5. Unbolt the pump from its bracket. Transfer the pulley to the new pump if it does not come with one \u2014 a pulley puller and installer are the correct tools and most parts stores loan them.\n6. Install the new pump with fresh O-rings on the pressure fitting. Torque the mounting bolts and the pressure fitting to the service-manual values for your year.\n7. Reconnect the return hose with a fresh clamp. Reinstall the belt, confirming it sits centered on every ribbed pulley.\n8. Fill the reservoir to the COLD line.\n9. **Bleed before starting.** With the engine off and the front wheels off the ground, turn the wheel lock to lock 15-20 times, topping up as the level drops. This pulls most of the air out without the pump ever running dry.\n10. Start the engine, let it idle, and turn lock to lock slowly several more times. Shut it off, check the level, and repeat until no bubbles return and the noise is gone.\n11. Lower the truck, check for leaks, drive it gently, and check for leaks again.\n\n**Wrong fluid is the most common mistake.** ATF WS is transmission fluid for the automatic \u2014 it belongs nowhere near this system. Bottled universal \"power steering fluid\" also does not meet the Dexron spec and can swell the seals over time. Dexron II/III-compatible ATF, from any reputable brand, is the answer. Toyota is not brand-sensitive here.\n\n**Never run the reservoir dry with the engine running.** Air on the suction side is what kills vane pumps, and it can do it in seconds. This is the number one way a fluid service turns into a pump replacement.\n\n**Air is why a new pump still whines.** If a freshly installed pump is noisy, do not assume a defective part. Bleed it again, thoroughly, wheels off the ground, engine off, 20 cycles. Trapped air takes several sessions to work out.\n\n**Do not hold the wheel at full lock.** On a running engine, holding against the stop puts the pump into relief and spikes fluid temperature. Approach the stop and back off.\n\n**Rule out the belt first, every time.** A $38 belt is a cheaper first guess than a $230 pump, and belt noise under steering load is a genuinely common misdiagnosis on this platform.\n\n**Check the rack boots.** If the fluid keeps disappearing with no visible drips, the rack is leaking internally into its boots. That is a rack replacement and a different job entirely.\n\n| Item | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Dexron II/III ATF, 2-3 quarts | $12-25 |\n| Fluid extractor / dedicated baster | $12-30 |\n| Serpentine belt (90916-A2015) | $35-42 |\n| Toyota / Aisin power steering pump | $200-280 |\n| Aftermarket remanufactured pump | $80-150 |\n| Pressure line O-ring kit | $8-15 |\n| Shop labor, pump replacement | $180-250 |\n| Shop labor, fluid flush | $90-150 |\n\nWork up from the cheap end \u2014 fluid, then level, then belt, then pump. Most 4Runners stop at step one, and the ones that do not are usually leaking somewhere you can find with a flashlight. On the reman pumps: the $80 ones have a poor reputation for whining out of the box on this platform, and a pump job is not one you want to do twice. Spend the money on OEM or Aisin."},{"id":"4runner-tie-rods-4runner","title":"Tie Rod Replacement","vehicle":"4runner","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":350},"tools_required":["22mm socket","19mm wrench","tie rod removal tool","torque wrench","grease gun","pickle fork or puller"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["steering","alignment","wear-item","5th-gen"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM inner tie rod","part_number":"45503-39395","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Moog outer tie rod (greasable)","part_number":"ES80895","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"555 Japan outer tie rod","part_number":"SE-T291L","vendor":"555","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Apex Chassis HD tie rod kit","vendor":"Apex Chassis","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"How To Change Inner and Outer Tie Rods on 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/02/01/how-to-change-inner-and-outer-tie-rods-on-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Apex Chassis Tie Rod Kit Install \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2024/12/05/apex-chassis-tie-rod-kit-install-5th-gen/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-power-steering-service","4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner"],"summary":"If your 5th gen 4Runner wanders, clunks over bumps, or shows uneven tire wear at 80\u2013120k miles, the outer tie rods are usually the cause \u2014 replace in pairs, use OEM for inners, and get an alignment the same day.","description":"Tie rods on the 5th gen 4Runner are a wear item. Outer tie rod ends carry the steering load through a ball-and-socket joint, and the rubber boot is the only thing protecting that joint from dirt and water. Boot tears, water gets in, the ball goes dry, the joint develops play, and the steering loses its center. Expect to replace outers somewhere between 80,000 and 130,000 miles on a stock truck \u2014 sooner if you wheel hard or run oversized tires.\n\nThe diagnosis is straightforward. Jack the front, grab the tire at 3 and 9 o'clock, and shake side to side. Any clunk or play from the steering linkage points to the tie rods. Watch the outer joint as someone rocks the steering wheel \u2014 visible movement in the ball joint means it is worn out. Uneven tire wear, especially feathering on the inside edge, is the long-term symptom of a misaligned front end driven on bad tie rods.\n\nParts choice matters more than usual on this truck. For the outer, Moog ES80895 is the standard aftermarket choice and is greasable, which lets you extend its service life with regular maintenance. The 555 brand (made in Japan, OEM supplier to many Toyota platforms) is the no-grease quality match for the factory part at a lower price than dealer. The Toyota OEM outer is excellent but expensive. For the inner, go OEM (45503-39395) \u2014 Toyota inners run forever, while aftermarket inners are the most common cause of premature replacement on this platform. If you wheel hard or run armor and lockers, the Apex Chassis kit at $320 uses heim joints and a larger-diameter forged rod that survives rock contact and heavy steering loads.\n\nThe replacement itself is straightforward but earns its difficulty rating from rust. The outer tie rod threads onto the inner with a jam nut, and on any truck that has lived in the salt belt, that jam nut and the boot clamp on the inner can be seized. Plan for penetrating oil overnight before you start. Count threads or measure the assembled length before disassembly \u2014 that measurement becomes your baseline toe setting and gets you home for the alignment shop.","steps":["Measure the existing assembled tie rod length from the jam nut to the end of the outer ball joint. Record it.","Loosen the lug nuts, raise the front, support on jack stands, remove the wheel.","Loosen the jam nut between the inner and outer tie rod. Penetrating oil overnight if it is seized.","Remove the 22mm castle nut from the outer tie rod stud. Separate the outer from the steering knuckle with a tie rod puller or pickle fork.","Count and record the number of turns as you spin the outer off the inner. Replace with the new outer and thread it on the same number of turns.","For the inner, remove the boot clamp, slide the boot back, and use a tie rod tool to break the inner free from the steering rack. Thread the new inner in and torque to 60 ft-lbs.","Slide the boot back over, clamp, then reinstall the outer and the steering knuckle. Torque the castle nut to 67 ft-lbs and the jam nut to 65 ft-lbs.","Reinstall the wheel, lower the truck, and drive directly to an alignment shop. Do not skip the alignment."],"pros":["Restores steering feel and centerline immediately","Eliminates the clunk over bumps","OEM inner plus greasable outer is the durability sweet spot","Apex HD kit handles trail abuse if you wheel often"],"cons":["Inner tie rod tool ($30) is needed if you do not already own one","Salt-belt trucks fight the jam nut and boot clamp","Alignment is mandatory afterward \u2014 budget another $100","Cheap aftermarket inners fail in 20\u201330k miles"]},{"id":"4runner-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit \u2014 Bilstein 5100 and Spacers (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":649},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","spring compressor","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","torque specs"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","budget","5100","spacer","non-KDSS"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Front and Rear Set (5th Gen 4Runner 2010-2024)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 1 Lift System (spacer, 5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Lift Kit (5th Gen 4Runner, non-KDSS)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Product Specifications","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/"},{"title":"4Runner World Lift Kit Discussion","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-mid-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The Bilstein B8 5100 set is the correct budget lift for non-KDSS 4Runners \u2014 a direct replacement of the factory shocks that delivers 1.5\"\u20132\" of lift and meaningfully better damping without touching the suspension geometry.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner (2010\u20132024) comes in two suspension configurations: non-KDSS (SR5, Limited, and base trims) and KDSS-equipped (Trail Edition, TRD Pro, and some Limited packages). KDSS stands for Kinetic Dynamic Suspension System \u2014 a hydraulic sway bar linkage that passively adjusts front-to-rear roll resistance for trail articulation. Standard coilovers and spacer lifts are not compatible with KDSS because the KDSS system requires specific shock valving to function correctly. Before ordering any budget lift, confirm whether your 4Runner has KDSS by looking under the front and rear for hydraulic cylinders connecting the sway bar end links to the axle. Non-KDSS 4Runners have a conventional mechanical sway bar.\n\nFor non-KDSS 4Runners, the Bilstein B8 5100 is the cleanest budget approach. The 5100 is a monotube shock with a height adjustment collar on the front units \u2014 set to roughly 1.75\" of lift front and use the preload-adjusted rear to match. You get improved damping, the lift, and no geometry compromise at this height. The Icon Stage 1 spacer lift is an alternative that uses a urethane spacer above the OEM spring \u2014 it lifts by 1.5\"\u20132\" but keeps the factory shock, which is fine if the factory dampers still have life. The Rough Country kit is the lowest-cost option but uses lower-quality dampers and is not recommended for builds that will see regular trail use.\n\nAt 2\" of lift on a non-KDSS 4Runner, the factory UCAs are operating at their outer geometry limit but don't yet require replacement. Caster changes are manageable. For anything above 2\", UCAs become necessary on the 4Runner as on the Tacoma.","steps":["Confirm KDSS or non-KDSS configuration before ordering \u2014 this lift path is for non-KDSS only","Lift front of vehicle, support on jack stands, remove front wheels","Remove OEM front shock/strut assembly (disconnect sway bar end link, brake line bracket, strut-to-knuckle bolts, upper mount)","Install Bilstein 5100 coilover; set height collar to desired lift per Bilstein's height chart","Torque strut-to-knuckle and upper mount to spec","Reconnect sway bar end link and brake line bracket; reinstall wheel","Repeat opposite side","Lift rear of vehicle; remove rear wheels","Support rear axle under differential","Disconnect rear sway bar end links","Remove and replace rear shocks (two bolts each \u2014 upper and lower mount)","Reinstall wheels, lower vehicle","Bounce suspension to settle; torque all fasteners at ride height","Verify ride height front and rear; adjust front collar if needed for level stance","Get an alignment \u2014 even at 2\" the alignment shifts enough to affect tire wear"],"pros":["Clean direct-replacement install with no geometry changes required at 2\" lift","Bilstein 5100 is a proven monotube design \u2014 significantly better than OEM damping for trail and washboard","Front height adjustment collar allows fine-tuning for precise level stance","No KDSS system involvement on non-KDSS models \u2014 straightforward swap","Fits 285/75R16 (33\") tires with minor fender trimming at 2\" lift"],"cons":["Non-KDSS only \u2014 standard shocks will compress the KDSS system incorrectly on KDSS-equipped models","Does not address CV angle or caster above 2\" \u2014 UCAs required for 2.5\"+","Rough Country kit uses lower-quality shocks \u2014 acceptable for mild use, not recommended for regular trail driving","Spacer lifts (Icon Stage 1) retain factory damper \u2014 if factory shocks are worn, you're adding lift without improving damping","Get an alignment \u2014 it's not optional after a lift, even at this height"]},{"id":"4runner-coilover-replacement","title":"Coilover Replacement \u2014 Bilstein 6112 / Icon / Old Man Emu","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","subcategory":"coilovers","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":2200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets (12, 14, 17, 19, 22mm)","torque wrench","pry bar","tie rod separator","alignment-pen marker"],"skills_required":["suspension disassembly","torque sequencing","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","coilovers","front-suspension","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 6112 Front Coilover Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","part_number":"47-310025","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Bilstein 5160 Rear Reservoir Shocks (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":425},{"name":"Icon Stage 2 4Runner Suspension Kit","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":1850},{"name":"Old Man Emu BP-51 Front Coilover Set","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":1650},{"name":"Dobinsons IMS Front Strut (assembled)","vendor":"Dobinsons","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 6112 Install on 5th Gen 4Runner (Trail4Runner)","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/07/03/bilstein-6112-5100-install-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Full List of Suspension Torque Specs For the 5th Gen 4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/06/17/5th-gen-4runner-torque-specs/"},{"title":"Bilstein 47-310025 Front 6112 Kit (Exit Offroad)","url":"https://exitoffroad.com/product/bilstein-47-310025-front-6112-series-kit/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-upper-control-arms-4runner","4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner"],"summary":"Bilstein 6112 coilovers give the 5th gen 4Runner a 1.5\"\u20133.2\" front lift with better damping than stock and bolt-in installation \u2014 pair with 5160 rear shocks for the most common build path.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner (2010-2024) ships with soft front coilover struts tuned for highway ride. They sag noticeably under bumper, winch, and skid plate weight, and they fade hard on washboard. The two most common upgrade paths are Bilstein 6112 (best value, ~$850 front) and Icon Stage 2 (better damping, ~$1850 for the matched kit). Old Man Emu BP-51 and Dobinsons IMS sit in between.\n\nThe 6112 is a 60mm-piston monotube with a snap-ring-adjustable spring perch \u2014 five positions giving 1.5\", 1.85\", 2.25\", 2.75\", and 3.2\" of front lift. Most builders run the middle position (around 2.25\") which gives clearance for 33\" tires without burning through CV axles. Going to 3.2\" puts the front CV angles past their happy range \u2014 expect shorter CV life unless you also drop the differential.\n\nPair with Bilstein 5160 remote-reservoir shocks at the rear (about $425/pair) for a matched-tune ride. 5160s help with brake fade and heat dissipation on long descents and have rebuildable internals. If you're going further than 2\" of rear lift, you need new rear coils too (not longer shocks) \u2014 Bilstein doesn't make them, so source from Icon or OME.\n\nInstall requires breaking the upper ball joint (or removing the upper control arm), separating the tie rod end, and dropping the lower strut bolt. The struts come pre-assembled \u2014 no spring compressor needed. Key torque values (verify against the trail4runner torque list before final tightening): upper strut nuts 47 ft-lb; lower strut bolt 100 ft-lb; tie rod castle nut 67 ft-lb; upper ball joint nut 80 ft-lb. Alignment is mandatory after \u2014 caster and toe both change.\n\nIf you're doing the upper control arms at the same time (recommended for any lift above 2\"), order those first so they're ready to install alongside.","steps":["Park on level ground; loosen front lug nuts.","Lift the front; support the frame on jack stands; remove front wheels.","Disconnect the sway bar end links from the lower control arms.","Separate the tie rod end from the steering knuckle (penetrant, tie rod separator).","Remove the upper ball joint nut; pop the upper control arm free from the knuckle (or remove the UCA entirely).","Support the lower control arm with a floor jack.","Remove the three upper strut nuts from the strut tower.","Remove the lower strut bolt at the lower control arm.","Drop the old strut down and out.","Install the new 6112 with the spring perch at your chosen lift height (factory-set or your choice).","Snug the upper nuts; install the lower bolt with the suspension at ride height (load the LCA with the floor jack before final torque).","Reconnect tie rod (67 ft-lb), upper ball joint (80 ft-lb), sway bar end links.","Repeat the other side.","Torque upper strut nuts to 47 ft-lb; lower strut bolt to 100 ft-lb (at ride height).","Reinstall wheels; torque lug nuts to 83 ft-lb.","Drive immediately to alignment shop \u2014 do not skip this step."],"pros":["Bilstein 6112 is the best damper-per-dollar in the 4Runner aftermarket","Spring perch adjustability lets you tune lift height after install","Pre-assembled \u2014 no spring compressor","Rear 5160 reservoirs add real cooling capacity for trail use"],"cons":["Lift above 2.25\" stresses CV axles; budget for shorter CV life","Alignment required immediately after","Upper control arms become a near-mandatory companion upgrade above 2\" lift","6112 is fixed-valving \u2014 not adjustable like Icon or OME BP-51"]},{"id":"4runner-expedition-lift","title":"Expedition Lift \u2014 Long-Travel and Dual Remote Reservoir ($2,500+)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":32},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":6000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","ball joint press","welder or fab shop access","angle grinder","breaker bar","pry bar","drill and bits"],"skills_required":["advanced suspension work","fabrication basics","shock tuning","alignment"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","long-travel","remote-reservoir","expedition","KDSS-delete"],"parts":[{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 3 Coilovers + Rear Shocks (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"Fox 2.5 Performance Elite Remote Reservoir Coilovers (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":1999},{"name":"Total Chaos Fabrication Long-Travel Suspension Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Total Chaos Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":4200},{"name":"King Shocks OEM Performance+ Remote Reservoir (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":2799}],"citations":[{"title":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 3 4Runner Product Page","url":"https://www.iconvehicledynamics.com"},{"title":"Total Chaos 4Runner Long-Travel Kit","url":"https://www.totalchaos.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-mid-lift","4runner-budget-lift"],"summary":"The expedition lift tier for the 4Runner means 3\"\u20134\" of lift with remote reservoir coilovers, extended UCAs, and \u2014 for full long-travel \u2014 a KDSS delete that trades the factory hydraulic sway bar for fixed end links and dramatically more suspension travel.","description":"At the expedition tier, the 4Runner build follows two distinct paths. The first is a high-end coilover upgrade with extended UCAs \u2014 Icon Stage 3, Fox 2.5 PE, or King OEM Performance+ \u2014 which keeps the stock geometry footprint but adds 12\"+ of high-speed damping performance. This path is KDSS-compatible on KDSS-equipped trucks (specify at order), and it's the correct approach for overland rigs that need refined high-speed comfort and a clean build without irreversible modifications.\n\nThe second path is Total Chaos long-travel, which replaces the entire front suspension geometry with extended lower control arms, a wider track, and repositioned shock mounts. This delivers 12\"\u201314\" of wheel travel and transforms the 4Runner into a genuine high-speed trail machine. Full long-travel kits on KDSS-equipped 4Runners require a KDSS delete \u2014 you replace the KDSS hydraulic end links with fixed end links and remove the KDSS system. This is irreversible in the sense that you'd need to source replacement KDSS parts and rebuild to return to stock. The payoff is unrestricted suspension travel and the ability to run larger shocks that the KDSS plumbing physically prevented.\n\nKDSS delete is not a decision to make lightly if you use the 4Runner daily. On pavement, KDSS meaningfully reduces body roll and improves stability. Without it, you'll notice more roll on on-ramp entries and spirited driving. On the trail, you won't miss it. If the 4Runner is a dedicated trail and overland rig that sees normal roads mostly as transit between trailheads, KDSS delete is fine. If it's a genuine daily driver that also goes off-road, preserve the KDSS and spec the KDSS-compatible high-end shock route instead.\n\nRemote reservoir coilovers (Fox 2.5, Icon 2.5 VS, King OEM+) hold more fluid and run cooler on repeated high-speed hits. The reservoir doubles the oil volume, which significantly delays thermal fade on desert washboards or repeated technical obstacles. At this price tier, the damping quality is the primary deliverable \u2014 these shocks are tunable in the field (compression adjustment knob) and rebuildable for long-term ownership.","steps":["Decide: KDSS-compatible high-end coilovers, or full long-travel with KDSS delete \u2014 plan accordingly","Order complete kit with matching UCAs and rear shocks; do not mix front-tier with budget rear","If KDSS delete: obtain fixed sway bar end links and bracket set before starting; remove KDSS hydraulic lines and cylinders first and cap all ports per manufacturer guidance","Lift and fully support vehicle on jack stands","Remove front suspension assembly completely: shocks, UCAs, lower control arms (on long-travel), sway bar","Install new lower control arm brackets and arms per kit template for long-travel; or install new extended UCAs only for coilover-only upgrade","Install new coilovers; set preload per shock manufacturer spec for target ride height","Install bumpstop extensions per kit requirements","Reconnect sway bar (with fixed end links if KDSS delete) or install long-travel sway bar system","Reinstall brakes and wheels","Complete rear: install new shocks; replace or rebuild rear suspension per kit instructions","Lower vehicle; bounce to settle","Drive to alignment shop \u2014 extended-travel geometry requires specialist alignment","Break in shocks per manufacturer protocol; recheck and adjust compression valve after 500 miles"],"pros":["Remote reservoir coilovers at this tier are rebuildable and tunable \u2014 long-term ownership investment","Icon Stage 3 KDSS-compatible option allows full expedition performance without irreversible modifications","Long-travel kits deliver 12\"\u201314\" of wheel travel \u2014 the highest-performance IFS 4Runner front end available","Fox 2.5 PE shocks handle desert high-speed terrain with consistent damping on repeated hits","King shocks are field-rebuildable in remote locations with basic tools"],"cons":["KDSS delete is effectively irreversible without sourcing and reinstalling OEM KDSS components","Long-travel kits require inner fender modification or cutting in some configurations","Total cost for full long-travel with rear conversion approaches $8,000\u2013$10,000 complete","Extended-travel builds require an alignment specialist \u2014 standard shops may not have 4Runner long-travel specs","Expedition tier is genuinely unnecessary for most trail and overland use \u2014 mid-tier lift delivers 90% of the capability at 40% of the cost"]},{"id":"4runner-kdss-bleed-4runner","title":"KDSS System Bleed and Leak Inspection","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":250},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","10mm and 12mm wrenches","torque wrench","fluid catch pan","flashlight","KDSS bleeder pump or hand pressure pump (1015 psi)"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","hydraulic systems comfort"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["suspension","KDSS","fluid","TRD Off-Road","Trail"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota KDSS hydraulic fluid (1L)","part_number":"08886-80506","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"KDSS bleeder valve seal kit","vendor":"Toyota dealer","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Replacement accumulator (only if pressure fault)","part_number":"48830-60130","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":850}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota 4Runner KDSS Bleeding Service Manual","url":"https://www.to4runner.net/bleeding-1189.html"},{"title":"Lexus GX KDSS Bleed Procedure (PDF)","url":"https://lexusgxor.com/_media/general:kdss_bleed.pdf"},{"title":"Carspec MN \u2014 KDSS service and repair","url":"https://carspecmn.com/toyota-and-lexus-kdss-service-and-repair-gx460-gx460-4runner-and-land-cruiser/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-coilover-replacement-4runner","4runner-upper-control-arms-4runner"],"summary":"KDSS is Toyota's hydraulic sway bar system on Trail and TRD Off-Road 4Runners; it rarely fails, but if you've lifted the truck or notice uneven ride height side-to-side, it needs a bleed to redistribute pressure and check for leaks.","description":"KDSS (Kinetic Dynamic Suspension System) connects the front and rear sway bars with hydraulic cylinders and an accumulator. On-road it locks up for flat cornering; off-road it goes slack to allow articulation. The system uses high-pressure hydraulic fluid running through hard lines and bleeders at the cylinders. It's sealed from the factory and Toyota lists no service interval \u2014 which means most owners forget it exists until something looks wrong.\n\nThe two most common reasons to touch the KDSS system: you've installed a lift and the front-to-rear ride height looks off, or you spot fluid on the underbody near the front cylinder or accumulator. If both bleeders are at different heights side-to-side after a lift, the cylinders are at different positions and need to be re-equalized. Skipping this leaves the system preloaded \u2014 one side carries more spring force than the other, and the truck will sag or lean.\n\nThe bleed procedure requires applying 7 MPa (1015 psi) to the system through the bleeder valves while cycling them in sequence. You can do this with a Toyota SST high-pressure pump or build a DIY setup from a $40 hand pump and a hydraulic gauge \u2014 many owners do, and it works. Do not exceed 8 MPa (1160 psi) or you risk damaging the accumulator. Bleeding without pressure does nothing \u2014 you have to push fluid, not open valves.\n\nLeak inspection is mostly visual: check the front cylinder seal (behind the front sway bar, driver side), the rear cylinder, the lines running along the frame, and the accumulator (mid-frame, passenger side). Any wet hydraulic film means a seal is going. Replace before fluid level drops enough to introduce air.\n\nA complete bleed takes about 6 liters of fresh fluid. Use Toyota part 08886-80506 specifically \u2014 generic hydraulic oils have wrong viscosity and will damage seals.\n\nThe shop labor on this is $400-$600 because most independents won't touch it. If you have the tools and patience, doing it yourself is well within reach.","steps":["Park on level ground. Check ride height at all four corners and note the differences.","Locate both bleeder valves: one on the front stabilizer control cylinder (driver side, rearward of the front diff), one on the stabilizer control with accumulator housing (mid-frame, passenger side).","Wipe both bleeders clean. Inspect surrounding lines and cylinders for any sign of leak.","Connect your pressure pump to the front cylinder bleeder. Apply 7 MPa (1015 psi). Stay below 8 MPa.","Loosen the front bleeder slightly to let air escape with fluid. Close. Repeat until output is clean fluid with no bubbles.","Move to the rear cylinder bleeder. Apply 7 MPa, loosen, close, repeat.","Move to the accumulator housing bleeder. Same procedure.","Cycle steps 4-7 until all three bleed points are running clean fluid.","Final torque: bleeder plugs to 11 ft-lb. Wipe fluid off, check for new leaks.","Drive 5-10 miles. Re-check ride height and re-bleed if side-to-side variance remains."],"pros":["Restores even ride height and articulation","Catches leaks before the system fails","DIY-able with a hand pressure pump and Toyota fluid","No alignment needed after"],"cons":["Wrong fluid will damage seals","Exceeding 8 MPa damages the accumulator","Most independent shops decline this work","Doing it without pressure (opening valves) accomplishes nothing"]},{"id":"4runner-lower-ball-joint-4runner","title":"Lower Ball Joint Replacement \u2014 4th and 5th Gen","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","subcategory":"ball_joints","type":"task","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":480},"tools_required":["ball joint press kit","torque wrench (250+ ft-lb capable)","30mm and 32mm sockets","pickle fork or ball joint separator","snap ring pliers","heavy hammer","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["heavy suspension work","press tool use","safety discipline"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["ball-joint","suspension","safety-critical","lca"],"parts":[{"name":"Toyota OEM lower ball joint (pair)","part_number":"43330-39755","vendor":"Toyota","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"555 (Japanese OEM supplier) ball joint","part_number":"SB-T872","vendor":"555 Sankei","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Moog K500080 (limited-life aftermarket)","part_number":"K500080","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":60}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota Service Information System \u2014 1GR lower ball joint torque specs","url":"https://techinfo.toyota.com"},{"title":"4Runner forum: ball joint failure thread and OEM vs aftermarket data","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org"},{"title":"NHTSA: 1996-2002 4Runner ball joint recall history (precursor to current design)","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov"}],"alternatives":["4runner-upper-control-arms-4runner"],"summary":"Toyota OEM lower ball joints (43330-39755) are the only ones that reliably last 100K+ miles on a 4Runner. Aftermarket budget joints fail in 20\u201340K. The joint presses into the lower control arm; total job is 4\u20138 hours per side and demands a ball joint press kit. Castle nut torques to 105 ft-lb.","description":"The lower ball joint connects the lower control arm to the steering knuckle on every IFS 4Runner (1996\u2013present). When it fails, the wheel tucks under the truck \u2014 at speed, this is a wreck. There is no safe \"wait and see\" with a clunking ball joint.\n\n**OEM-only is not optional.** The 1996\u20132002 4Runner had a famous ball joint recall (NHTSA Campaign 04V-432) because the design was undersized. Toyota redesigned it for the 4th gen (2003+) and the OEM joint has been reliable since. Aftermarket Moog, Beck/Arnley, and parts-store-brand joints use thinner stamped housings and inferior grease seals; they fail at 20K\u201340K miles. This is the one part on a 4Runner where you do not save money \u2014 Toyota OEM only (43330-39755 for lower; 555 SB-T872 is the only acceptable Japanese OEM-equivalent alternative).\n\n**Symptom checklist.** Clunking over speed bumps at low speed. Steering wander on the highway that's not corrected by alignment. Uneven inside-edge tire wear that returns within 5K of an alignment. Visible grease at the boot. Any one of these means inspect; two means replace.\n\n**Press tool is required.** The ball joint presses into the lower control arm with significant interference fit. You cannot drive it out with a hammer (you'll bend the arm). Rent or buy a ball joint press kit \u2014 Harbor Freight #66828 is $80 and works.","steps":["Lift front of truck, support frame on stands, remove wheel. Penetrate all suspension bolts you're about to touch with PB Blaster the night before.","Disconnect the sway bar end link from the LCA (14mm).","Remove the lower shock bolt (17mm) \u2014 you'll need to drop the shock to get clearance.","Remove the ABS sensor harness and brake line bracket from the knuckle.","Remove the castle nut on the ball joint stud (32mm). Use a pickle fork or ball joint separator to pop the stud loose from the knuckle.","Remove the LCA from the frame \u2014 two bolts, typically 22mm. Lift the LCA out of the truck.","Remove the snap ring on top of the ball joint with snap ring pliers.","Use the ball joint press kit to press the old joint OUT of the LCA. (Cup on one side, drive sleeve on the other; thread the press tight slowly.)","Clean the bore in the LCA. Apply a thin film of grease to the new joint's outer surface.","Press the new joint IN, going the opposite direction. The joint will seat with the snap ring groove showing on top.","Install the new snap ring.","Reinstall the LCA onto the frame. Torque LCA-to-frame bolts to 137 ft-lb (front) and 100 ft-lb (rear). Tighten the front bolt only at full droop; tighten the rear at ride height.","Insert ball joint stud through the knuckle. Install castle nut, torque to 105 ft-lb, then back off only enough to align the cotter pin slot (never overtighten past alignment). Install cotter pin.","Reconnect shock, sway bar end link, ABS, brake bracket.","Reinstall wheel, lower truck. Re-torque ball joint nut after first 500 miles.","Get an alignment within 50 miles. Camber and caster will be off."],"pros":["Restores steering precision and safety","OEM joint is good for 100K+ miles","Eliminates uneven tire wear from worn joint","Once done, you know the joint history"],"cons":["4\u20138 hours per side","Ball joint press is required","Aftermarket joints fail quickly \u2014 must spend $180 on OEM","Alignment required after","LCA bolts are torqued at ride height, not droop \u2014 straightforward to miss"]},{"id":"4runner-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift with UCAs \u2014 2.5\"\u20133\" Lift ($1,000\u2013$2,500)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":1000,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","ball joint press","spring compressor","breaker bar","pry bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","intermediate wrenching","ball joint service","torque specs"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","UCA","KDSS","coilover","mid-lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Stage 2 Complete Lift Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Dobinsons MRR Coilovers + UCAs Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Dobinsons","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Old Man Emu BP-51 Bypass Shock Kit (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":1499},{"name":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics Billet Upper Control Arms (5th Gen 4Runner)","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":749}],"citations":[{"title":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics 4Runner Stage 2 Product Page","url":"https://www.iconvehicledynamics.com"},{"title":"Dobinsons 4Runner Suspension Products","url":"https://www.dobinsons.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-budget-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The mid-tier lift on the 4Runner is where KDSS compatibility becomes the deciding factor \u2014 Icon Stage 2, Dobinsons MRR, and OME BP-51 all have KDSS-compatible shock valving options, but you must order the correct variant for your trim.","description":"At 2.5\"\u20133\" of lift, the 4Runner's IFS geometry requires the same fix as the Tacoma: upper control arms to correct caster, restore CV shaft angle, and provide more droop travel. The OEM UCAs on the 5th gen 4Runner are aluminum-bodied units that are not adjustable \u2014 at 2.5\" they push the ball joint to an angle that causes premature wear. Aftermarket UCAs restore the correct geometry for lifted height.\n\nKDSS compatibility is the critical specification for this tier. KDSS-equipped 4Runners (Trail, TRD Pro, some Limited) use hydraulic sway bar cylinders that require specific shock damping rates to function correctly. Installing a standard shock on a KDSS-equipped 4Runner compresses the KDSS circuit improperly, causing the sway bar to bind or the hydraulic lines to be over-pressurized on articulation. Icon's CDCV (Continuous Damping Control Valve) coilovers and Fox's remote reservoir units are specifically valved for KDSS 4Runners. Check the product description for \"KDSS compatible\" before ordering any shock or coilover kit.\n\nFor non-KDSS 4Runners, the mid-tier lift is more straightforward \u2014 any of the listed kits work cleanly. Icon Stage 2 is the most complete package (UCAs, coilovers, rear shocks, add-a-leaf). Dobinsons MRR offers remote reservoir coilovers at a competitive price with excellent tunability. OME BP-51 is the premium choice with internal bypass damping that handles rough terrain exceptionally well at this price point.\n\nUCAs for the 4Runner from Total Chaos ($949), Camburg ($899), or Icon ($749) all fit correctly and are meaningfully better than OEM. For a trail-focused build, Total Chaos billet UCAs are the most durable; Icon UCAs are the best value.","steps":["Confirm KDSS vs non-KDSS configuration; order KDSS-compatible shocks if applicable","Lift and support front of vehicle; remove front wheels","Disconnect OEM front strut assembly (sway bar end link, brake line bracket, strut-to-knuckle bolts, upper mount)","Disconnect OEM upper control arm from frame and spindle \u2014 use ball joint separator on spindle connection","Install new UCAs: torque frame bolts snug (final torque at ride height); connect new ball joint to spindle and torque","Install new coilover; set height collar per manufacturer spec for target lift","Reconnect sway bar end link and brake line; reinstall wheel","Repeat opposite side","Lift and support rear; remove rear wheels; support axle","Disconnect rear sway bar end links","Replace rear shocks; install add-a-leaf or full rear spring per kit instructions","Reassemble rear; torque at ride height","Lower vehicle; bounce to settle; perform final torque on all suspension fasteners at ride height","Drive to alignment shop \u2014 request 4Runner-specific caster and camber specs for lifted height"],"pros":["KDSS-compatible shock options allow full mid-lift on Trail and TRD Pro trims without disabling KDSS","UCAs correct the geometry that makes everything else work better \u2014 steering feel, tire wear, CV longevity","Icon Stage 2 is the most complete all-in-one kit for this tier \u2014 no compatibility guesswork","OME BP-51 delivers bypass-style damping at a price point below most US boutique shocks","285/75R16 (33\") tires fit cleanly on most 4Runners at 2.5\"\u20133\" lift without trimming"],"cons":["KDSS compatibility error is expensive to fix \u2014 double-check spec before ordering","Ball joint press required for UCA swap \u2014 not an optional tool","Requires professional alignment after install \u2014 budget $100\u2013$150","Dobinsons MRR valving is firm; less comfortable on pavement than Icon or OME for daily drivers","3\" lift on a KDSS 4Runner reduces KDSS effectiveness somewhat \u2014 the system works best at stock-adjacent heights"]},{"id":"4runner-rear-lift-options","title":"Rear Lift Options \u2014 Spacers vs. New Coils","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets 17mm-22mm","torque wrench","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["suspension R&R","coil compression awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","leveling","ride-quality","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"Daystar 1.5\" rear coil spacer kit","part_number":"KT09125BK","vendor":"Daystar","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Old Man Emu 2890 rear coils (medium load)","part_number":"2890","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Dobinsons C59-319V variable rate rear coils","part_number":"C59-319V","vendor":"Dobinsons","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Eibach Pro-Truck stage 2 rear coils","vendor":"Eibach","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Icon Stage 2 rear coil and shock kit","vendor":"Icon Vehicle Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Lift & Leveling Kits: Beginners Guide","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2017/06/14/5th-gen-4runner-mods-lift-kits/comment-page-1/"},{"title":"Top 10 5th Gen 4Runner Suspension Lift Kits","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2020/12/18/top-10-5th-gen-4runner-suspension-lift-kits/"},{"title":"Eibach vs Dobinson vs OME (4Runners.com)","url":"https://www.4runners.com/threads/eibach-vs-dobinson-vs-ome.6417/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-shocks","4runner-upper-control-arms"],"summary":"If you only drive on pavement and want the truck level, a $100 rear spacer is fine. If you carry a roof rack, drawer system, or bumper, swap to new rear coils \u2014 spacers preload the stock spring, accelerate sag, and ride worse on every surface.","description":"The 5th-gen 4Runner sags in the rear from the day you drive it home, and any added weight makes it worse. Stock rear coils were specced for a near-empty cargo area; bolt on a roof tent, sliders, a rear bumper with a tire carrier, and a Trasharoo full of recovery gear and you'll measure two-plus inches of rear droop within a few months. There are two ways to get the rear back up: a spacer that sits on top of the stock coil, or replacement coils sized for your real load.\n\nSpacers are cheaper. A Daystar 1.5-inch rear coil spacer kit runs about $90 and installs in two hours. The catch is that a spacer doesn't fix anything \u2014 it pre-compresses the stock spring, which means the truck rides on a stiffer portion of the spring curve and the spring is closer to coil bind under articulation. Forum consensus on 4Runners.com and Trail4Runner is consistent: spacers ride harshly compared to new coils, both on and off-road. They're acceptable on a stock-weight, mall-crawler 4Runner that needs to look level. They're a bad answer on a built truck.\n\nNew coils are the right answer for any 4Runner carrying real weight. The three brands that come up over and over: Old Man Emu (OME 2890 medium-load coils at about $320 give 2 to 2.5 inches with up to 250 lb constant load), Dobinsons (variable-rate C59 series, around $360, broad load ratings), and Eibach Pro-Truck (around $280, slightly firmer ride). Pair them with appropriate shocks \u2014 Bilstein 5100s at $80 each for a budget setup, Bilstein 5160 remote reservoirs for heat dissipation on long trails, or Icon 2.0/2.5 series for a high-end build. Spring rate matters more than brand: get a constant-load coil rated for the weight you actually carry. OME's \"medium\" is right for a roof rack and some recovery gear. \"Heavy\" is right for a rear bumper and tire carrier. Going too heavy ride harshly; too light squats under load.\n\nA common mistake: installing rear lift coils without addressing the upper control arms or sub-frame contact points. Once the rear is up 2+ inches, the rear shock may run out of droop travel \u2014 check shock length and consider extended-travel rear shocks if you've gone above 2.5 inches.","steps":["Weigh the truck loaded with everything you usually carry (CAT scale or estimate by adding component weights).","Pick a coil rated for that real-world weight \u2014 not stock weight.","Remove the rear wheels; support the axle with a floor jack.","Disconnect the rear sway bar end links and the rear shock lower bolts to let the axle drop further.","Disconnect the rear track bar at the axle (leave the frame side).","Lower the axle until the stock coils are loose; pull them out by hand.","Install new coils with the isolator pads. Use a coil retainer if articulation is unrestricted.","Reconnect shocks, end links, and track bar. Torque to spec.","Drive 10 miles and re-measure ride height. Allow 100 miles for coils to settle."],"pros":["New coils restore actual spring capacity, not height","Better ride on every surface compared to spacers","Wide load-rating selection from OME, Dobinsons, Eibach","Two-hour install with hand tools"],"cons":["Spacers preload the stock spring and ride harshly under articulation","May need extended rear shocks if going above 2.5\"","Heavy-load coils ride stiff when the truck is empty","Coil spring R&R can pinch fingers \u2014 control the axle drop carefully"]},{"id":"4runner-shocks","title":"Best Shocks for the 4Runner \u2014 Bilstein 5100, Fox 2.0, and OME Nitrocharger","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","breaker bar","spring compressor (if swapping struts)"],"skills_required":["basic suspension work","torque specs","KDSS awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2024"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","Bilstein","Fox","OME","KDSS"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series IFP Shock Set \u2014 4Runner","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Old Man Emu Nitrocharger Sport Shock Set \u2014 4Runner","vendor":"Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Bilstein B8 5160 Remote Reservoir Shock Set \u2014 4Runner","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":950}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Series Product Page","url":"https://www.bilstein.com"},{"title":"Fox Factory 2.0 Performance Series","url":"https://www.ridefox.com"},{"title":"Old Man Emu 4Runner Shock Line","url":"https://www.omeoffroad.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-budget-lift","4runner-mid-lift","4runner-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The Bilstein 5100 is the correct answer for most 4Runner owners who want a meaningful improvement over the OEM shocks without committing to a full suspension overhaul \u2014 they fit at stock height or up to 1.5\" of lift, they're available for both KDSS and non-KDSS trims, and they last.","description":"**Tools:**\n- Floor jack and jack stands (4-point support required \u2014 you'll be under the vehicle)\n- Socket set (17mm, 19mm, 21mm are the common shock mount sizes on 5th gen)\n- Torque wrench\n- Breaker bar for the upper shock nut\n- Spring compressor only if replacing the entire strut assembly (not needed for shock-only swap on rear)\n\n**Parts \u2014 choose a tier:**\n\n| Tier | Product | Price | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Budget | Bilstein 5100 set | ~$380 | Stock or mild lift, daily driver |\n| Mid | Old Man Emu Nitrocharger Sport | ~$480 | Trail use, loaded overland rigs |\n| Premium | Fox 2.0 Performance IFP | ~$699 | Off-road performance, adjustable preload |\n| Top | Bilstein 5160 remote reservoir | ~$950 | Repeated high-speed off-road use |\n\n**KDSS note:** The 5th gen 4Runner (2010\u20132024) Trail, TRD Pro, and some Limited trims come with KDSS (Kinetic Dynamic Suspension System). Shocks for KDSS-equipped vehicles must be specifically valved to work with the system \u2014 ordering standard shocks for a KDSS 4Runner causes sway bar binding and over-pressurizes the hydraulic circuit. The Bilstein 5100 has KDSS-specific part numbers. Verify your trim before ordering.\n\n**Front (strut-style front suspension):**\n\n1. Loosen the lug nuts before jacking. Lift the front of the vehicle and place jack stands under the frame rails \u2014 not the lower control arm.\n2. Remove the front wheel.\n3. Disconnect the sway bar end link from the lower strut mount (19mm typically).\n4. Unclip the brake line bracket from the strut body.\n5. Break loose the two strut-to-knuckle bolts (21mm). These are torque-to-yield bolts on some model years \u2014 check a factory service manual if in doubt; replace them if so.\n6. Working under the hood, locate the three upper strut mount nuts (usually 14mm) and break them loose before fully removing the lower bolts.\n7. Support the lower control arm with the floor jack as you remove the upper mount nuts and the lower strut-to-knuckle bolts. The assembly will drop \u2014 control the descent.\n8. If swapping shocks only (not the full strut): use a spring compressor to safely compress the front spring, then remove the upper strut nut and separate the shock from the mount hardware. Transfer all mount hardware to the new shock.\n9. Reinstall in reverse order. Torque strut-to-knuckle bolts to factory spec (typically 184 ft-lb on 5th gen). Torque upper mount nuts to 30 ft-lb.\n\n**Rear:**\n\n1. Lift and support the rear of the vehicle. Place jack stands under the frame \u2014 not the axle.\n2. Support the rear suspension (floor jack under the rear differential or axle housing).\n3. Remove the lower shock bolt first (usually 17mm), then the upper mount (usually 17mm or 19mm).\n4. Note the orientation of the shock \u2014 some have a specific mounted angle.\n5. Install the new shock, torque lower bolt first (with the suspension at ride height, or close to it), then upper. Factory spec is approximately 72 ft-lb rear lower, 18 ft-lb rear upper on 5th gen.\n6. Reinstall rear wheels.\n\n**After both axles:**\nLower the vehicle, bounce to settle. Drive to an alignment shop if you changed ride height \u2014 a shock-only swap at the same ride height doesn't require alignment, but confirm visually that nothing has shifted.\n\n- **KDSS trim mismatch** is the most common expensive mistake. Cross-reference your trim code before clicking Add to Cart.\n- The 5th gen front upper strut mount nuts are notoriously difficult to access with a standard socket \u2014 a wobble extension is worth sourcing before the job.\n- Bilstein 5100 shocks come pre-set for specific lift ranges (e.g., 0\"\u20131.5\" or 1.5\"\u20132.5\" depending on part number). Order the correct range for your current or planned lift height.\n- Rear shock lower mounts on high-mileage 4Runners often corrode to the bolt. PB Blaster applied the night before the job saves significant time.\n- Fox 2.0 shocks require a break-in period (~500 miles) before the damping reaches its full rated performance \u2014 they feel slightly firm out of the box.\n\n- **Bilstein 5100 (rear pair):** ~$160\u2013$190 depending on the model year.\n- **Fox 2.0 Performance IFP (full set):** ~$649\u2013$749. Available from Fox directly and from dedicated off-road retailers.\n- **OME Nitrocharger Sport (full set):** ~$450\u2013$520. Available from ARB distributors and Outback Equipment.\n- **Professional installation:** $150\u2013$300 at a 4WD-specialist shop if you don't have a spring compressor and are replacing the full front strut assembly.\n\nFor daily-driver 4Runners with mild lift, the Bilstein 5100 at ~$380 for the full set is the correct answer. For built overland rigs running loaded weight over rough terrain regularly, the OME Nitrocharger Sport or Fox 2.0 earn their price premium in damping performance and longevity."},{"id":"4runner-upper-control-arms","title":"Upper Control Arm Upgrade After Lift \u2014 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":550,"high":1100},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","pickle fork or tie rod separator","pry bar"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","ball joint work","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["lift","caster","uca","ifs"],"parts":[{"name":"SPC Adjustable UCAs 4Runner 5th gen","part_number":"25470","vendor":"SPC","price_estimate_usd":650},{"name":"Total Chaos UCAs 4Runner (uniball)","part_number":"96514","vendor":"Total Chaos","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Icon Delta Joint UCAs 5th gen 4Runner","part_number":"58460DJ","vendor":"Icon","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Dirt King Ball Joint UCAs","vendor":"Dirt King","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"Total Chaos UCA Install on 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2019/02/14/total-chaos-uca-install-toyota-4runner/"},{"title":"6 Upper Control Arm Setups For 5th Gen 4Runner \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2023/07/07/upper-control-arm-setups-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"SPC 4Runner Adjustable UCAs \u2014 4Runner Customs","url":"https://4rcustoms.com/spc-4th-5th-gen-4runner-adjustable-upper-control-arms/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-coilover-replacement","4runner-rear-lift-options","4runner-lower-ball-joint"],"summary":"If you've lifted a 5th gen 4Runner more than 2 inches and the alignment shop tells you caster is at the lower spec, you need adjustable upper control arms \u2014 the stock arms run out of caster adjustment before you do.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner is a double-wishbone IFS truck. When you raise the front by extending the coilover, the upper control arm pivots downward at the spindle end and the caster angle goes negative. The alignment cam adjustment at the lower control arm can recover a little caster, but at about 2\"\u20132.5\" of lift you'll max out the cams and the alignment shop will hand the truck back with caster at 1\u00b0 or below. The truck will feel vague at highway speed, wander when crowned roads transition, and won't self-center coming out of a turn.\n\nAftermarket adjustable UCAs solve this by either re-locating the ball joint higher in the arm body (Dirt King) or providing a uniball at the spindle end that lets the arm swing through a wider range (Total Chaos, Camburg). Either way, you recover 2\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 of caster and the truck drives like Toyota intended again. SPC's arms are the budget pick at $600\u2013$700, use a sealed ball joint, and are quiet. Total Chaos and Icon use uniballs that flex more on articulation but make noise as they wear and need rebuilding every 30K\u201350K trail miles. Dirt King's ball joint version splits the difference \u2014 quiet like SPC, more articulation than stock.\n\nThe choice between sealed ball joint and uniball isn't really about caster \u2014 both give you caster back. It's about how the truck will be used. A 4Runner that's daily-driven 25K miles a year with occasional trail trips wants SPC or Dirt King. A 4Runner that lives off-road wants Total Chaos or Icon, with the understanding that you're committing to a uniball rebuild program. Anyone telling you uniballs \"don't wear\" is selling them.\n\nInstallation is straightforward IFS work. The upper ball joint comes apart with a pickle fork or tie rod separator. The two pivot bolts at the frame are 19mm and torqued in the 80\u201390 ft-lb range. Brake hose bracket relocates to the new arm. ABS sensor wire either re-routes or gets a new bracket from the UCA kit. None of the kits require modifying the frame \u2014 these are bolt-in. After install, the alignment shop will dial in caster between +2\u00b0 and +4\u00b0 depending on whether you want quick steering response (low) or stable highway tracking (high).\n\nThe KDSS-equipped 4Runner (Trail Premium, TRD Off-Road Premium, TRD Pro) doesn't change the UCA install \u2014 KDSS is a sway bar system, not a control arm system. You don't need to bleed KDSS to install UCAs.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock rear wheels.","Loosen front lug nuts (22mm). Lift the front, support on stands under the frame, let the lower control arm hang.","Remove front wheel.","Support the lower control arm with a floor jack \u2014 keeps the coilover loaded.","Remove the ABS sensor wire and brake hose bracket from the stock UCA.","Break the upper ball joint cotter pin, remove the nut (19mm).","Separate the ball joint from the spindle with a pickle fork or ball joint press.","Remove the two UCA pivot bolts at the frame (19mm).","Pull the stock UCA out.","Install the new UCA. Pivot bolts go to factory torque (80\u201390 ft-lb).","Slot the new ball joint into the spindle, torque the nut (60\u201375 ft-lb depending on brand), install cotter pin.","Re-mount the brake hose bracket and ABS wire on the new arm.","Reinstall the wheel, lug nuts to 83 ft-lb.","Lower the truck.","Get a four-wheel alignment. Caster target: +2.5\u00b0 to +3.5\u00b0. Camber: 0\u00b0 to +0.5\u00b0. Toe: factory spec."],"pros":["Recovers caster after 2\"+ lift","Lets the alignment shop actually dial in spec","SPC and Dirt King options are sealed ball joint \u2014 no rebuild program","Bolt-in install, no frame modification","Stops the wandering and vague highway feel after a lift"],"cons":["Uniball arms need periodic rebuilding (30K\u201350K trail miles)","Quality arms are $600\u2013$900 \u2014 budget for them when planning a lift","Some kits require a brake line relocation or longer line","Alignment after install is required, not optional"]},{"id":"4runner-common-trouble-codes","title":"Common 4Runner Trouble Codes Decoded (4th and 5th Gen)","vehicle":"4runner","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["OBD2 scanner","smartphone (for Bluetooth dongles)"],"skills_required":["basic diagnosis"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","obd2","check-engine-light","codes","diagnosis","dtc"],"parts":[{"name":"OBDLink MX+ Bluetooth scanner","vendor":"OBDLink","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"BAFX 34t5 Bluetooth OBD2 dongle (budget)","vendor":"BAFX","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Techstream-compatible cable (Toyota-specific diagnostics)","vendor":"aftermarket","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"Toyota TSB T-SB-0230-12 Rev1 \u2014 MIL ON, DTC P2440/P2442/P2445 (1GR-FE)","url":"https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2012/SB-10075945-0699.pdf"},{"title":"NHTSA TSB \u2014 Air Injection System MIL ON DTC P2440/P2442 (1GR-FE)","url":"https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2018/MC-10141580-9999.pdf"},{"title":"Toyota / Lexus secondary air injection code reference","url":"https://hewitt-tech.com/codes/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues","crawl-vs-atrac","4runner-engine-air-filter-4runner"],"summary":"Read the code before you spend a dollar. A $25 Bluetooth dongle and a phone app pay for themselves the first time they stop you from buying a catalytic converter you did not need. On the 1GR-FE 4Runner the same handful of codes come up again and again, and most of them point at inexpensive, well-documented fixes. The one genuinely expensive code on this platform is the secondary air injection family \u2014 P2440, P2442, P2445 \u2014 and it has a Toyota TSB behind it.","description":"A check engine light is information, not a verdict. What separates a cheap repair from an expensive guess is three pieces of data your scanner gives you for free: the code itself, whether it is pending or confirmed, and the freeze-frame snapshot of what the engine was doing when it set. A code that sets only at cold start under load tells a different story than one that sets at idle when warm, and a parts counter cannot see that difference.\n\n**Budget ($25-40):** A BAFX-class Bluetooth dongle paired with Torque Pro on Android or OBD Fusion on iOS. Reads and clears generic powertrain codes, streams live data, and shows readiness monitors. This covers 90% of what a 4Runner owner needs.\n\n**Better ($90-130):** OBDLink MX+ or similar. Faster sampling for live data, and \u2014 the reason to spend the money \u2014 it reads manufacturer-specific ABS, SRS, and body codes that a generic dongle skips entirely. On a truck with an ABS or traction light, a generic reader will tell you nothing.\n\n**Toyota-specific:** A Techstream-compatible cable gets you into the factory diagnostic software, including active tests and A-TRAC/VSC module data. It is the right tool for chasing chassis and network faults, and overkill for a P0455.\n\nWhatever you buy, get one that shows readiness monitor status. In emissions counties that single screen determines whether a trip to the testing station is worth making.\n\n**P2440 / P2442 / P2445 \u2014 Secondary air injection.** The signature expensive fault on the 1GR-FE, and worth understanding before it happens. The secondary air system pumps air into the exhaust on cold start to light the catalysts faster. Water intrudes into the switching valves, corrodes the internals, and the valve sticks open \u2014 P2440 is bank 1, P2442 is bank 2, P2445 is the pump itself reported stuck off. Toyota addressed it in TSB T-SB-0230-12, and the bulletin's repair replaces the air pump assembly, the emission control valve set, and gaskets, and adds an air pump cover to keep water out. The bulletin is explicit that if the pump filter shows damage, both the pump and the switching valves get replaced together \u2014 a partial repair invites a repeat. Dealer repairs commonly land in the $1,500-2,500 range; the DIY parts bill is substantially less but the job is not the place to start your wrenching career. Check whether your VIN has any warranty enhancement coverage before paying out of pocket. Related: [[4runner-1gr-fe-known-issues]].\n\n**P0455 / P0456 / P0442 \u2014 EVAP leak, large or small.** Start with the fuel cap: reseat it, listen for the clicks, and drive a few cycles. If it returns, the usual suspects are a cracked EVAP hose, a failed vent valve, or a leaking charcoal canister. On trucks that see water crossings and mud, the canister and vent solenoid under the rear are exposed and worth inspecting physically. A smoke test at a shop is $80-150 and finds in ten minutes what guessing finds in three parts.\n\n**P0420 / P0430 \u2014 Catalyst efficiency below threshold.** This is the code that sells the most unnecessary catalytic converters. Before condemning a cat, look at the downstream oxygen sensor's live data: a healthy cat produces a lazy, nearly flat downstream signal, while a failed one mirrors the upstream sensor's rapid switching. If the downstream sensor is slow, biased, or heater-faulted, the sensor is the fault. Also rule out an exhaust leak upstream of the sensor \u2014 a small manifold or flange leak sets P0420 with a perfectly good converter.\n\n**P0171 / P0174 \u2014 System lean, bank 1 / bank 2.** Both banks lean at once means something common to both: a vacuum leak, a dirty or contaminated MAF sensor, or an intake boot that has split. Check the intake tract physically first, then clean the MAF with MAF-specific cleaner. If you run an oiled cotton air filter, this is the code it causes \u2014 see [[4runner-engine-air-filter-4runner]]. One bank lean alone points more toward that bank's air-fuel sensor or an injector.\n\n**P0031 / P0037 / P0051 / P0057 \u2014 Air-fuel or oxygen sensor heater circuit.** Common on high-mileage trucks and usually the sensor itself rather than wiring. Buy Denso \u2014 this is a Denso-supplied platform and the aftermarket sensors have a poor reputation for setting new codes within a year.\n\n**P0011 / P0021 \u2014 Camshaft timing over-advanced.** Frequently oil-related. Overdue oil, wrong viscosity, or a clogged VVT filter screen restricts the oil control valve. Change the oil with the correct grade and clean or replace the OCV before assuming a cam phaser.\n\n**P0300-P0306 \u2014 Misfire, random or cylinder-specific.** A cylinder-specific misfire on a 1GR-FE with over 100,000 miles is coils and plugs until proven otherwise. Swap the suspect coil to another cylinder and see whether the code follows it \u2014 that test costs nothing and settles it. See [[4runner-spark-plugs-4runner]].\n\n**C1201, C1241, C0327 and other C-codes.** Chassis codes tied to ABS, VSC, and the transfer case. Before chasing modules: test the battery and clean the terminals. A weak battery or a poor ground throws a startling number of phantom chassis codes on these trucks, and every one of them clears when the electrical system is healthy again. Wheel speed sensors are the next suspect, especially on a truck that sees mud.\n\n**U-codes (U0100, U0101, and similar).** Network communication faults. Same rule: battery and grounds first. These also appear briefly after any battery disconnect and clear on their own after a few drive cycles.\n\n1. Plug the scanner into the port under the dash, left of the steering column, and turn the key to ON without starting.\n2. Pull **all** codes \u2014 powertrain, chassis, body, and network \u2014 not only the ones the check engine light triggered.\n3. Note whether each code is **pending** (set once, not yet confirmed) or **confirmed**. A pending code that never returns is often a one-off from a bad tank of fuel or a loose cap.\n4. Capture **freeze frame** before clearing anything. Engine speed, load, coolant temperature, and fuel trims at the moment of the fault are the most useful diagnostic data you will get.\n5. Look at **long-term fuel trim** at idle and at cruise. Double-digit positive trims confirm a genuine lean condition; near-zero trims alongside a lean code point at a sensor rather than an actual air leak.\n6. Only then clear the code, drive it, and see what comes back.\n\n**Do not clear codes before an emissions test.** Clearing resets the readiness monitors, and an incomplete monitor fails the test exactly like an active code. Depending on the monitor, it can take 50-100 miles of mixed driving to reset. Clear, repair, then drive the cycle, then test.\n\n**Do not buy a catalytic converter off a P0420.** Sensors and exhaust leaks are more common causes and an order of magnitude cheaper.\n\n**Test the battery before chasing any C-code or U-code.** This single habit saves more money on these trucks than any other diagnostic step.\n\n**A cleared code is not a repair.** If it comes back after a drive cycle, the fault is real. Repeatedly clearing to make a light go away hides a condition that may be damaging the catalysts.\n\n**Aftermarket parts set codes.** Non-Denso oxygen and air-fuel sensors, cheap MAF sensors, and off-brand thermostats are all overrepresented in \"I fixed it and the light came back\" stories on this platform.\n\n| Item | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Budget Bluetooth dongle + phone app | $25-40 |\n| OBDLink MX+ (reads ABS/SRS) | $90-130 |\n| Techstream cable | $25-40 |\n| Shop smoke test for EVAP | $80-150 |\n| Denso air-fuel or oxygen sensor | $80-180 |\n| Coil pack (Denso, each) | $60-90 |\n| Secondary air injection repair, dealer | $1,500-2,500 |\n\nThe scanner is the highest-leverage tool purchase on the truck. Its value is not that it fixes anything \u2014 it is that it moves you out of the parts-cannon conversation and into the cheap-fix one before you have spent money."},{"id":"crawl-vs-atrac","title":"CRAWL Control vs. A-TRAC \u2014 When to Use Each","vehicle":"4runner","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"off_road_systems","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["4LO transfer case","CRAWL Control switch","A-TRAC button","Multi-Terrain Select dial"],"skills_required":["basic off-road experience"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["crawl-control","a-trac","trd-off-road","trd-pro","4lo"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Off-Road Features \u2014 Trail4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/01/30/5th-gen-4runner-off-road-features/"},{"title":"What is the difference between ATRAC and Crawl control? \u2014 Toyota 4Runner Forum","url":"https://www.toyota-4runner.org/5th-gen-t4rs/156373-what-difference-between-atrac-crawl-control.html"}],"alternatives":["4runner-common-trouble-codes"],"summary":"Use A-TRAC when you need brake-based traction at any low speed and want to stay on the throttle yourself; use CRAWL Control when terrain is steep or technical enough that you'd rather hand off throttle and brakes to the truck and focus only on steering.","description":"Both systems live on 5th-gen 4Runner TRD Off-Road, TRD Pro, and Trail trims, and both require 4LO to engage. That's where the similarity ends. Mixing them up \u2014 or not knowing which one is doing the work \u2014 leaves drivers either fighting the truck or missing the more manageable line through an obstacle.\n\nA-TRAC (Active Traction Control) is the system that pulses the brakes on a spinning wheel to send torque across the differential to the wheel with grip. It's effectively a brake-based locker simulation. You're still driving \u2014 modulating throttle, steering, and brakes yourself \u2014 and A-TRAC works in the background to keep you moving when one or two tires lose contact. It's the right tool for moderate obstacles where you have one wheel in the air on a flex line, slick rock with intermittent grip, or mud where momentum matters and you need to feed throttle.\n\nCRAWL Control is more aggressive automation. You select a speed (1\u20135 mph), lift off the throttle and brake entirely, and steer. The truck handles everything else, pulsing throttle and brakes individually to each wheel to maintain that exact speed. It's loud \u2014 you'll hear the ABS pump groaning and the engine surging \u2014 but it's incredibly effective on terrain where smooth, slow, consistent input matters more than driver feel. Steep rocky descents, deep sand at controlled speed, and difficult ledges where modulating four pedals with two feet is genuinely hard are where CRAWL earns its keep.\n\nToyota's own guidance is to use speeds 1\u20132 for descents, 3\u20135 for ascents, and any speed on flat technical terrain. Lower settings give the system more time to react. Setting 5 in deep sand keeps you from bogging down.\n\nWhere drivers go wrong: leaving CRAWL Control engaged in terrain that doesn't need it. The system constantly cycles the ABS, which wears the pump and is unnecessary on moderate dirt roads where A-TRAC alone (or no electronics at all) would handle the conditions. Treat CRAWL Control like a tool you reach for in genuinely difficult spots, not a default setting.\n\nMulti-Terrain Select (MTS) is a separate adjustment that modifies throttle response and traction sensitivity for surface type. It works alongside both A-TRAC and CRAWL \u2014 it doesn't replace either. Mud & Sand allows more wheel spin before A-TRAC intervenes; Rock allows the least.","steps":["Shift transfer case to 4LO with the truck stopped and in neutral.","For A-TRAC: press the A-TRAC button on the overhead console. Drive normally \u2014 system intervenes automatically when wheel slip is detected.","For CRAWL: press the CRAWL button on the overhead console. Use the dial to select speed 1\u20135.","Release throttle and brake. Steer only.","To disengage CRAWL: press the button again, or tap brake/throttle.","To disengage A-TRAC: press button again or shift out of 4LO."],"pros":["A-TRAC: driver retains full control, less wear on brakes","A-TRAC: excellent for momentum-based obstacles","CRAWL: hands-off speed control on technical terrain","CRAWL: superior for steep, slow descents"],"cons":["CRAWL is noisy and wears ABS components with overuse","Neither replaces a real locker \u2014 both are brake-based","Both require 4LO, so highway use is out","New drivers often default to CRAWL when A-TRAC would do the job"]},{"id":"4runner-tire-fitment-33s","title":"Running 33\" Tires on the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":1500},"tools_required":["torque wrench","breaker bar or impact","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["tire mounting","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["tires","fitment","33s","285","wheels_tires","TPMS"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 All-Terrain T/A 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":225},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak AT3W 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Nitto Terra Grappler G2 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Cooper Discoverer ST/MAXX 285/75R16 (each)","vendor":"Cooper","price_estimate_usd":235}],"citations":[{"title":"4Runner World Tire Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"BFGoodrich KO2 Specifications","url":"https://www.bfgoodrichtires.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-wheels","4runner-mid-lift","4runner-budget-lift"],"summary":"285/75R16 is the cleaner fit for the 5th gen 4Runner at 2\"\u20132.5\" of lift \u2014 it runs without rubbing on most builds, and the 16\" wheel drops the tire into a more affordable and widely available size range than 17\" equivalents.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner's front fender opening is slightly more generous than the Tacoma's, which means 285/75R16 (33.8\" actual diameter) clears cleanly at 2.5\" of lift without any trimming on most builds. At 2\", these tires may graze the front mud flap bracket at full compression and full lock \u2014 trimming the plastic bracket resolves it in minutes. The 295/70R17 (33.5\" diameter) in the 17\" size is the alternative, but it's more expensive per tire, requires 3\"+ lift for clean clearance on the 4Runner, and the 17\" size reduces sidewall height which is a disadvantage for off-road cushioning and airing down.\n\nTire compound and tread pattern matter more than brand loyalty. The BFG KO2 remains the all-terrain benchmark \u2014 excellent wet traction, proven trail performance, and tread life of 50,000+ miles under normal conditions. The Falken Wildpeak AT3W has overtaken the KO2 in several independent mud and wet traction comparisons at a slightly lower price. Cooper's ST/MAXX is a mud-terrain pattern that's quieter than most MTs while retaining genuine muddy terrain capability \u2014 worth considering if the 4Runner sees mixed use.\n\nTPMS sensors require attention when switching to aftermarket wheels. The 5th gen 4Runner uses a tire pressure monitoring system with sensors clocked to the OEM wheels \u2014 a swap to aftermarket wheels requires either transferring the OEM sensors to the new wheels (possible with most designs) or purchasing aftermarket-compatible sensors. Confirm sensor compatibility with your wheel vendor before ordering. The system will throw a fault code until sensors are reinstalled and relearned.\n\nFor builds running the stock 17x7 OEM wheels, note the relatively conservative 4.5\" backspacing \u2014 285/75R16 in a 16x8 aftermarket wheel with 4.75\"\u20135\" backspacing gives better clearance than cramming a 285 onto the OEM 17\" wheel.","steps":["Confirm current lift height; 285/75R16 requires minimum 2\" for clean fitment, 2.5\" for zero contact","Order tires; plan for simultaneous wheel upgrade if OEM wheels have inadequate backspacing","Have tires mounted and balanced at tire shop; transfer or install TPMS sensors","Install wheels on vehicle; torque lug nuts to 90 ft-lb in star pattern","Test clearance at full lock in both directions and at full bump before trail use","Trim plastic mudflap bracket if needed for 2\" lift clearance","Perform TPMS relearn procedure per owner's manual (or have the shop do it at tire install)","Recheck lug torque at 50 miles"],"pros":["285/75R16 fits cleanly at 2.5\" lift without any trimming on the 5th gen 4Runner","Taller sidewall compared to 295/70R17 \u2014 better trail cushioning and lower air-down pressure threshold","16\" size is more affordable than 17\" equivalents across all tire brands","BFG KO2 and Falken AT3W are proven AT choices in this size with wide dealer availability","No gear ratio change required for normal trail use at 33\" diameter"],"cons":["295/70R17 on the 17\" OEM wheel requires 3\"+ lift \u2014 more clearance needed than most assume","TPMS sensor transfer or replacement adds $50\u2013$150 to the install cost","Taller tires reduce fuel economy 1\u20132 mpg typical \u2014 more pronounced on highway","285/75R16 on OEM 17\" wheels can work but wheel offset may cause inner fender contact on some builds \u2014 confirm before committing","Cooper ST/MAXX is louder on pavement than AT-pattern tires \u2014 a real trade-off for daily drivers"]},{"id":"4runner-tires-at-budget","title":"Budget All-Terrain \u2014 Big Capability Per Dollar","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_at","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":950,"high":1500},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire shop mount/balance"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["tires","all-terrain","wheels_tires","budget"],"parts":[{"name":"Value all-terrain tires (set of 5, 265/70R17 or 285/70R17)","vendor":"Falken Wildpeak A/T4W / General Grabber ATX / Cooper Discoverer AT3 4S","price_estimate_usd":1150},{"name":"Mount, balance, and road-force check (5 tires)","vendor":"local tire shop","price_estimate_usd":175}],"citations":[{"title":"Falken Tires \u2014 Wildpeak A/T4W (successor to the A/T3W)","url":"https://www.falkentire.com/wildpeak/at4w"},{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 fitting bigger tires on a 5th gen: what to cut and trim","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/01/25/bigger-tires-4runner-cutting-trimming/"},{"title":"Offroadium \u2014 proven 4Runner mods and tire picks","url":"https://offroadium.com/toyota-4runner-mods.html"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tire-fitment-33s","4runner-tires-mt"],"summary":"The premium all-terrain gets the headlines, but a value-tier AT covers the vast majority of how a 4Runner is used \u2014 and the money saved buys sliders or a winch that matter more than the last few percent of tread compound. This is the sensible-money tire pick, with the two sizes that bolt on without a body shop.","description":"A mid-priced all-terrain from a reputable maker gives up little to a flagship on dry dirt, gravel, and light trail, while running quieter on the highway and costing meaningfully less per set. For a daily-driven 4Runner that sees weekend forest roads and the occasional moderate trail, that is the right balance of grip, manners, and price.\n\nThe gap between tiers has narrowed. Falken's Wildpeak A/T4W replaced the long-running A/T3W with a revised compound, staggered shoulder blocks, and a tread warranty up to 65,000 miles \u2014 flagship-adjacent durability at a value-tier price. General's Grabber ATX and Cooper's Discoverer AT3 4S sit in the same band. Where a value tire still gives ground is severe-service sidewall toughness and the last increment of wet-rock and deep-snow bite.\n\nEvery 4Runner from the 3rd gen forward runs a 6x139.7 bolt pattern on a 106.1 mm hub bore, and the common 5th gen wheel is a 17x7 at roughly +15 mm offset. That wheel takes both of the sizes worth considering:\n\n- 265/70R17 \u2014 about 31.6 in tall. This is the factory SR5 and Trail size. It bolts to a stock truck with no trimming, no rub at full lock, and no regear conversation. Figure roughly $170-215 per tire in the value tier.\n- 285/70R17 \u2014 about 32.7 in tall and 11.2 in wide. This is the size most builds land on. It fits a stock-height 4Runner with mild rubbing at full lock, and clears cleanly with a leveling or mild lift plus fender-liner and plastic trimming. A body mount chop is usually optional at this width. Figure roughly $210-265 per tire.\n\nStep past 285/70R17 to a 285/75R17 (about 33.8 in) and the job changes character \u2014 that is a body mount chop, liner surgery, and rocker cap trimming. That path is covered in [[4runner-tire-fitment-33s|the 33-inch fitment guide]], and it stops being a value-tier decision.\n\nThree markings decide whether a value AT suits your truck:\n\n1. Load range. A stock-ish 4Runner is fine on an SL or a P-metric. A 4Runner carrying a rear bumper, a drawer system, and a roof load wants a C or D load range so the sidewall does not squirm under weight. The heavier casing costs a little ride comfort and a little fuel economy.\n2. Three-Peak Mountain Snowflake. If you see genuine winter, the 3PMSF stamp separates an all-terrain that works in snow from a summer tire with blocks on it. Most current value ATs carry it \u2014 confirm on the specific size, because the rating is granted per size, not per model line.\n3. Load index and speed rating. Match or exceed the figures on the door jamb. Dropping load index to save money is the one corner not worth cutting on a 4,800-5,000 lb truck.\n\nSpend up only when the use demands it. Flagship all-terrains and mud-terrains pull ahead in deep snow, on wet rock, and in severe-service durability against sidewall cuts. A value AT is not the tire for a sharp-rock crawler \u2014 there the tougher three-ply casing of a premium AT or an MT earns its premium. If mud, wet granite, and snow are your normal, read [[4runner-tires-mt|the mud-terrain guide]] and make the trade deliberately.\n\nThe other real difference is tread life. A value AT that gets rotated every 5,000 miles and lives at correct pressure often outlasts a neglected premium tire, so the cheapest path to tread life is a rotation schedule rather than a bigger receipt.\n\n- Buy five, not four. The 4Runner's full-size spare is the whole point of the platform, and a mismatched spare is dead weight the moment you need it on a locked axle.\n- Torque the lug nuts to 83 lb-ft in a star pattern and re-torque after 50-100 miles. Shops overtighten with an impact and warp rotors.\n- Ask for a road-force balance on 285-width tires. Standard spin balancing hides the stiff-spot vibration that shows up at 65-70 mph.\n- A 32.7 in tire on the factory 3.727 ratio costs noticeable low-end response on the 4.0L. That is livable; a 33.8 in tire generally is not, and that is when [[4runner-regear|regearing]] enters the budget.\n- Recalibrate the speedometer or accept roughly a 3-4 percent optimistic reading on 285/70R17.\n\nAs of 2026, five value-tier all-terrains in 285/70R17 run roughly $1,050-1,325 for the tires plus $150-200 for mount, balance, and disposal \u2014 call it $1,200-1,500 out the door. The same five in a flagship AT land $400-600 higher.\n\nThat difference is roughly a set of [[4runner-sliders|bolt-on rock sliders]], or most of a mid-tier [[4runner-winch|winch]] install, or a full recovery kit with a soft shackle, a kinetic rope, and two rated recovery points. On a 4Runner build, that gear changes outcomes more often than tread compound does.\n\nA value all-terrain is the high-value default for most 4Runners: quiet enough to daily, capable enough for moderate trail, and kind to the budget. Run 265/70R17 for zero modification, 285/70R17 for the size the aftermarket is built around. Confirm 3PMSF and load range on the exact size, buy five, and put the saved money into armor and recovery gear."},{"id":"4runner-tires-mt","title":"Mud-Terrain Tires \u2014 When the AT Is Not Enough","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_mt","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2200},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire shop mount/balance"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2009","2010-2024"],"tags":["tires","mud-terrain","wheels_tires"],"parts":[{"name":"Mud-terrain tires (set of 5, 285/70R17 or 285/75R17)","vendor":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 / Nitto Trail Grappler M/T / Falken Wildpeak M/T01","price_estimate_usd":1750},{"name":"Road-force mount and balance (5 tires)","vendor":"local tire shop","price_estimate_usd":200}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 running 34-inch tires on a 5th gen 4Runner","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2017/09/28/34-tires-on-5th-gen-4runner/"},{"title":"Trail4Runner \u2014 fitting bigger tires: what to cut, chop and remove","url":"https://trail4runner.com/2018/01/25/bigger-tires-4runner-cutting-trimming/"},{"title":"RCI Off Road \u2014 4Runner off-road parts and armor","url":"https://rcimetalworks.com/product-category/toyota/4runner/"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tire-fitment-33s","4runner-wheels"],"summary":"Most 4Runner owners are better served by a quality all-terrain \u2014 but if the truck spends real time in mud, on wet rock, and in deep snow, a mud-terrain is the tire that finally bites where an AT slips. This is the honest case for the MT and the case against it, with the sizes, the supporting work, and the numbers.","description":"The wide voids and aggressive shoulder lugs of an MT clear mud and bite loose rock and sand far better than an all-terrain. On wet granite and in deep snow ruts the chunkier tread finds edges an AT rounds over. For a moderate-to-hard trail 4Runner on 33s, that traction is the difference between cleaning an obstacle and spinning.\n\nThe second thing you buy is casing. The current MTs worth running \u2014 the BFGoodrich KM3, the Nitto Trail Grappler, the Falken Wildpeak M/T01 \u2014 use three-ply sidewalls with wrapped shoulder rubber. On sharp desert rock and shale, that casing is what stops a $300 tire from becoming a trailside plug-and-pray. An all-terrain in the same size is meaningfully thinner in the sidewall, and sidewall cuts are the failure mode that ends trips.\n\nThere is no free traction, and the MT bill comes due every day you are not on a trail:\n\n- Noise. An MT adds a constant drone above roughly 45 mph that no amount of rotation fully removes. It gets louder as the tread wears unevenly.\n- Wet pavement. Fewer sipes and a stiffer compound mean longer wet stops and less warning before the tire lets go. This is the trade owners underestimate most.\n- Tread life. Expect 35,000-45,000 miles from an MT against 55,000-65,000 from a good AT, and expect it to get loud in the last third.\n- Fuel and acceleration. A 285/70R17 MT weighs roughly 60-70 lb against about 50 lb for an AT in the same size. Five of those is real rotating mass on a 270 hp, 278 lb-ft 4.0L, and it shows up in both mpg and braking distance.\n- Cold-weather compliance. Many MTs carry an M+S marking but not the Three-Peak Mountain Snowflake. If you drive chain-control or winter-tire-required roads, confirm 3PMSF on the exact size before you commit.\n\nIf 80 percent of your miles are pavement and graded road, [[4runner-tires-at-budget|the value all-terrain]] is the smarter daily tire and the money is better spent elsewhere.\n\nThe MT decision is really a size decision, because on a 4Runner the size determines how much cutting you sign up for:\n\n1. 285/70R17 (about 32.7 in, 11.2 in wide). The sane MT size. Clears with a leveling kit or mild lift plus fender-liner and inner-plastic trimming. A body mount chop is usually optional here.\n2. 285/75R17 (about 33.8 in). This is where the truck goes to the shop. A 3 in lift plus anything taller than about 33.5 in and wider than about 11 in requires a body mount chop \u2014 cutting the outer portion of the front body mount bracket behind the front tire and welding in a fill plate \u2014 along with pushed-back and trimmed liners, trimmed rocker caps, flattened pinch welds, and mud flap removal.\n3. 34 in and up. Full lift, body mount chop, extended bumpstops, and usually [[4runner-upper-control-arms|aftermarket upper control arms]] to recover caster and clearance. At this point the tire is driving the whole build.\n\nCheck backspacing before you order. The factory 17x7 at roughly +15 mm offset tucks a 285 well; going to a lower-offset aftermarket wheel pushes the tire out into the liner and the fender law in some states. [[4runner-wheel-offset-backspacing|The offset and backspacing guide]] has the math.\n\n- Regear. The factory 5th gen ratio is 3.727. On 33s the 4.0L falls into a hole \u2014 sluggish off the line and hunting between gears. 4.56 restores near-factory feel on 33s and 4.88 suits a heavy, armored truck. See [[4runner-regear|the regear guide]]; on an IFS truck front and rear must match.\n- Five matched tires. Run a full-size matching spare, not a mismatched one. With a rear locker a mismatched spare loads the axle every mile it turns.\n- Road-force balance. MT lugs are heavy and unevenly distributed. Standard spin balancing frequently leaves a 60-70 mph shimmy that gets blamed on the lift.\n- Airing down. The soft, tall MT sidewall is most of what you paid for. Running 15-18 psi on the trail and re-inflating to about 32-35 psi on the road is the difference between the tire working and the tire being loud.\n- Re-torque to 83 lb-ft after 50-100 miles, and rotate every 5,000 miles including the spare.\n\nAs of 2026, a set of five 285/70R17 mud-terrains from the three brands above runs roughly $1,400-1,900 for the tires plus $175-225 for road-force mounting and disposal. The 285/75R17 size lands $200-350 higher, and the body mount chop adds $300-600 at a shop unless you can weld.\n\nTotal realistic spend to put a 4Runner on 33 in MTs, including a regear, is $3,500-5,000. That number is the honest reason to be certain about the tire before you start.\n\nChoose the mud-terrain only if mud, wet rock, and snow are your normal, then accept the noise, the wet grip, and the tread-life penalty as the price of the bite. For everything else the all-terrain is the better all-round tire. Buy the size your lift and body work actually support, regear to match, run five, and air down."},{"id":"4runner-wheel-offset-backspacing","title":"Wheel Offset and Backspacing for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1200},"tools_required":["tape measure","straightedge"],"skills_required":["measuring"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["wheels_tires","offset","backspacing","wheels","fitment","rubbing"],"parts":[{"name":"Method Race Wheels 17x8.5 (+0 offset, 5x150)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":230},{"name":"SCS Stealth 6 Forged Wheel (17x8.5, configurable offset)","vendor":"SCS Wheels","price_estimate_usd":410},{"name":"Spidertrax 1.25\" Wheel Spacers (5x150, pair)","vendor":"Spidertrax","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"5th Gen 4Runner Wheel Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4runner.org"},{"title":"Method Race Wheels 4Runner Application","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tire-fitment-33s","4runner-wheels"],"summary":"For a 5th gen 4Runner running 33s, the cleanest wheel spec is a 17x8.5 with around 0 to +15mm offset (roughly 4.75\"\u20135\" backspacing). That pushes the tire out enough to clear the upper control arm and sway bar at full stuff without throwing the tire so far out that it batters the fender and loads the wheel bearings.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner uses a 5x150mm bolt pattern \u2014 the same as the Tundra and shared with several Toyota trucks, which narrows your wheel options compared to the common 6x139.7 pattern but still leaves good choices. The factory wheels run a high positive offset (around +15mm with deep backspacing), which tucks the tire under the fender for on-road manners. When you add bigger tires, that factory offset is part of why they rub: the tire sits too far inboard and contacts the UCA, the sway bar, and the inner fender liner during articulation and steering.\n\nOffset and backspacing describe the same thing from two directions. Offset is the distance from the wheel's mounting face to its centerline \u2014 positive means the face is outboard of center (tire tucks in), negative means inboard (tire pokes out). Backspacing is the distance from the mounting face to the inner lip. Lower (or negative) offset and less backspacing push the tire outward, gaining clearance from suspension components at the cost of a wider track, more fender contact, and added leverage on the wheel bearings and ball joints. The goal is the least poke that clears your tires \u2014 not maximum stance.\n\nNothing to measure your current setup but a tape measure and a straightedge laid across the wheel face. To change fitment, either new wheels in your target offset or wheel spacers to effectively reduce the offset of the wheels you have.\n\n1. Identify your current wheel's offset (often stamped on the back, e.g. \"ET15\") and width\n2. Decide your tire target \u2014 33s on a 5th gen generally want the tire moved out from the factory position to clear the UCA and sway bar\n3. For new wheels, target 17x8.5 at roughly 0 to +15mm offset (about 4.75\"\u20135\" backspacing) as the proven 33\" range\n4. To measure backspacing on a wheel: lay a straightedge across the back rim, measure from the straightedge down to the mounting face\n5. If using spacers instead of new wheels, a 1.25\"\u20131.5\" spacer reduces effective offset by that amount \u2014 use hub-centric, properly torqued spacers and re-torque after the first drive\n6. Test fit and cycle the steering lock-to-lock and the suspension through its travel (or wheel a known obstacle) to check for rubbing before committing\n\nMore poke is not free \u2014 every millimeter outboard adds leverage on the wheel bearings, ball joints, and steering components, and accelerates their wear, so don't run more negative offset than you need to clear your tires. Spacers are a legitimate tool, but only hub-centric spacers properly torqued and re-checked; cheap lug-centric spacers are a safety risk. Watch for fender liner and pinch-weld contact at full stuff with wide, low-offset setups \u2014 a little fender trimming or a body-mount chop is often part of fitting 33s+ cleanly. Wider track also means more spray and stone-chipping down the body side, and it can push the tire past the fender's legal coverage in some jurisdictions.\n\nA set of capable 17x8.5 aftermarket wheels in the right offset runs $900\u2013$1,200 (Method around $230 each, forged options like SCS more). Quality hub-centric spacers are about $150\u2013$200 a pair and are the budget way to fix offset on factory or existing wheels. The cheapest move is to verify your current offset first \u2014 sometimes the wheels you have will clear your tires with a modest spacer, saving the cost of a new set entirely."},{"id":"4runner-wheels","title":"Wheel Selection for the 5th Gen 4Runner","vehicle":"4runner","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":356,"high":1240},"tools_required":["torque wrench","breaker bar or impact","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["tire mounting","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2010-2024"],"tags":["wheels","beadlock","steel","alloy","bolt-pattern","backspacing","4runner"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Steel Wheel 17x9 (6x139.7, -6mm offset)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"Method Race Wheels 701 Trail 17x8.5 (6x139.7, 0mm offset)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Fuel Off-Road Assault 17x9 (6x139.7, -12mm offset)","vendor":"Fuel Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":165},{"name":"Black Rhino Armory 17x9 (6x139.7, -12mm offset)","vendor":"Black Rhino","price_estimate_usd":155},{"name":"KMC XD Machete 17x8.5 (6x139.7)","vendor":"KMC Wheels","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"Method Race Wheels 701 Trail Product Page","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com"},{"title":"4Runner World Wheel Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4runner.org"}],"alternatives":["4runner-tire-fitment-33s"],"summary":"The 5th gen 4Runner shares the Tacoma's 6x139.7 bolt pattern, which opens the same wide wheel market \u2014 but the 4Runner's slightly wider track and taller body mean backspacing in the 4.75\"\u20135.25\" range for lifted builds.","description":"The 5th gen 4Runner's factory wheel is a 17x7 alloy with roughly 4.5\" of backspacing \u2014 a relatively conservative offset. On a lifted 4Runner with UCAs at 2.5\"+, moving to a 17x8.5 or 17x9 with 4.75\"\u20135.25\" backspacing (0mm to +10mm offset range) gives better tire-to-body clearance while keeping the wheel in the correct geometric position relative to the hub. Too little backspacing (high negative offset) pushes the tire outside the fender \u2014 requires flares and increases wheel bearing stress. Too much backspacing (high positive offset) tucks the tire inward toward the upper control arm and inner fender, especially on builds with UCAs.\n\nThe 4Runner's hub bore is 106.1mm \u2014 the same as the Tacoma and most Toyota 6x139.7 applications. Most aftermarket wheels ship with 108mm bore and a set of hub-centric rings to fit Toyota applications. Verify hub-centric ring inclusion before ordering, and install them \u2014 a wheel that's not seated concentrically on the hub bore will vibrate at highway speeds regardless of balance quality.\n\nMethod Race Wheels 701 Trail remains the top recommendation for lifted 4Runners. The multi-spoke design sheds mud, the 0mm offset on the 17x8.5 sits correctly for most 2.5\"\u20133\" lift builds, and the quality control is excellent. Black Rhino Armory and Fuel Assault are lower-cost alternatives with similar design philosophy \u2014 they're heavier than Method but adequate for overland and mild trail use. KMC XD Machete is a budget alloy option that works well for the price.\n\nBeadlock wheels are the correct choice for rock crawling at tire pressures of 8\u201312 PSI. At those pressures, a standard wheel can unseat the tire bead under side loading on a rock. KMC XD Machete beadlock and similar DOT-compliant designs allow legal street use while providing bead retention at low pressures. Ring bolt torque on beadlocks requires periodic checks \u2014 check every few months and after every hard trail session.","steps":["Confirm lift height and UCA configuration; select backspacing accordingly (4.75\"\u20135.25\" for 2.5\"+ lift)","Verify hub-centric ring size (106.1mm hub, 108mm bore on most aftermarket wheels)","Order wheels with hub-centric rings if not included","Have tires mounted and balanced at shop; transfer TPMS sensors from OEM wheels","Confirm TPMS sensor compatibility \u2014 4Runner TPMS sensors vary by year; verify before mounting","Install wheels; torque lug nuts to 90 ft-lb in star pattern (4Runner lug nut torque spec)","Perform TPMS relearn per owner's manual","Test clearance at full lock in both directions and at full bump before trail use","Recheck lug torque at 50 miles"],"pros":["6x139.7 bolt pattern gives access to the widest wheel selection in the off-road market","Method 701 Trail is the correct specification wheel for lifted 4Runners \u2014 proven fit and offset","Steel wheels are nearly indestructible and cost-effective for a dedicated spare","Beadlock options allow genuine low-pressure rock crawling on DOT-legal designs","KMC XD Machete delivers alloy construction at a steel wheel price point"],"cons":["Incorrect backspacing on IFS 4Runners causes inner fender contact at full compression \u2014 verify before ordering","TPMS sensors require transfer or replacement; varies by year \u2014 confirm compatibility","Low-offset wheels (-18mm and below) push tires outside fender edge \u2014 requires flares to be street legal","Beadlock ring bolts require periodic torque checks \u2014 skip this and the ring can loosen, defeating the purpose","Hub-centric rings must be installed for proper wheel seating \u2014 missing rings cause highway vibration regardless of balance quality"]}];